hadiths[600]{hadithnumber,text}:
"8401","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Arim Abu al-Numan, from Hammad ibn Zayd, from Ali ibn Zayd, from al-Hasan, that Ziyad appointed Kilab ibn Umayya al-Laythi over al-Ubulla. Uthman ibn Abi al-As passed by him and said: O Abu Harun, what keeps you sitting here? He said: This one sent me over al-Ubulla. He said: Shall I not tell you a hadith I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: The Prophet of Allah Dawud (peace be upon him) used to say to his family in an hour of the night: O family of Dawud, rise and pray, for this is an hour in which supplication is answered, except for a sorcerer or a collector of tithes. So he boarded a ship in his place, then returned to Ziyad and asked to be excused."
"8402","Narrated Ibrahim ibn Hashim al-Baghawi from Ibrahim ibn al-Hajjaj al-Sami, from Hammad ibn Salama, from Ali ibn Zayd, from al-Hasan, that Abd Allah ibn Amir appointed Kilab ibn Umayya over al-Ubulla. Uthman ibn Abi al-As passed by him and said to him: What is your matter? He said: I was appointed over al-Ubulla. He said: Shall I not inform you? He said: Yes. He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: In the night there is an hour in which the gates of heaven are opened, saying: Is there any asker that I may give him? Is there any supplicant that I may answer him? Is there any seeker of forgiveness that I may forgive him? And Dawud went out one night saying: No one asks Allah tonight for anything but He gives it to him, except a sorcerer or a collector of tithes. So he boarded a small boat and went to Abd Allah ibn Amir, saying: Take back your office, for Uthman ibn Abi al-As told me such and such."
"8403","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Ibn al-Asbahani, and narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba, both saying: Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us from Ashath, from al-Hasan, from Uthman ibn Abi al-As, who said: Among what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) enjoined upon me was not to take a wage for the adhan."
"8404","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Abd Allah ibn Umar ibn Aban, from Muhammad ibn Fudayl, from Ashath, from al-Hasan, from Uthman ibn Abi al-As, who said: The last thing the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) enjoined upon me was: Pray with your companions the prayer of their weakest, for among them are the old, the weak, and the one with a need."
"8405","Narrated Bishr ibn Musa from al-Humaydi, and narrated Zakariyya ibn Yahya al-Saji from Muhammad ibn Zanbur, both saying: Fudayl ibn Iyad narrated to us from Ashath ibn Sawwar, from al-Hasan, from Uthman ibn Abi al-As, who said: The last thing the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) enjoined upon me was: Pray with your companions the prayer of their weakest, for among them are the old, the weak, and the one with a need; and take a muezzin who does not take a wage for the adhan."
"8406","Narrated Musa ibn Harun from Ismail ibn Ubayd ibn Abi Karima, from Muhammad ibn Salama, from Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Ulatha, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from al-Hasan, from Uthman ibn Abi al-As, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me when he sent me to Thaqif: Be brief in prayer, O Uthman, and measure the people by their weakest, for among them are the weak, the one with a need, the pregnant, and the nursing mother. I hear the crying of the infant and so am brief."
"8407","Narrated Muhammad ibn Asim al-Asbahani from Ali ibn Harb al-Mawsili, from Amr ibn Abd al-Jabbar, from Ubayda ibn Hassan, from al-Hasan, from Uthman ibn Abi al-As, who said: The last thing the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) enjoined upon me was: When you pray with your companions, pray with them the prayer of their weakest, for among them are the weak and the sick."
"8408","Narrated Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal from his father, from Muhammad ibn Salama al-Harrani, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Talha ibn Ubayd Allah ibn Kurayz, from al-Hasan, who said: Uthman ibn Abi al-As was invited to a circumcision feast but refused to attend. He was asked, and said: We did not attend circumcision feasts in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), nor were people invited to them."
"8409","Narrated Abd Allah ibn al-Saqr al-Sukkari from Bakr ibn Khalaf, from Umar ibn Sahl al-Mazini, from Abu Hamza al-Attar, from al-Hasan, who said: Uthman ibn Abi al-As was invited to food, and it was said: Do you know what this is? This is the circumcision of a girl. He said: This is something we did not see in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and refused to eat."
"8410","Narrated Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Mandah al-Asbahani from Abu Kurayb, from Ishaq ibn Sulayman, from Anbasa, from Ismail ibn Muslim, from al-Hasan, from Uthman ibn Abi al-As, who said: He fixed forty days for the postpartum woman."
"8411","8384 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Abu al-Rabi' al-Zahrani narrated to us, Hibban ibn Ali narrated to us, from Ash'ath, from al-Hasan, from Uthman ibn Abi al-As, who said: A period of forty days was fixed for women in postnatal bleeding."
"8412","8385 - Muhammad ibn Salih ibn al-Walid al-Narsi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, (ha) and Abu Khalifah al-Fadl ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Abd al-Wahhab al-Hujabbi narrated to us, both saying: Abd al-Wahhab al-Thaqafi narrated to us, Anbasah al-Ghanawi narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from Uthman ibn Abi al-As, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Allah, Exalted is His remembrance, says: Whoever does a good deed will have ten like it, except fasting, for it is Mine and I reward for it."
"8413","8386 - Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Abd al-Wahhab al-Hujabbi narrated to us, Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us, from Anbasah, from al-Hasan, from Uthman ibn Abi al-As, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Fasting is a shield with which the servant shields himself from the Fire."
"8414","8387 - Muhammad ibn Muhammad al-Judhu'i al-Qadi narrated to us, Uqbah ibn Mukram al-Ammi narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Isa al-Khazzaz narrated to us, Yunus ibn Ubayd narrated to us, from al-Hasan, that a freedman of Uthman ibn Abi al-As asked him to give him wealth to trade with, the profit to be shared between them, so he gave him twenty thousand dirhams, with which he bought wine, then brought it to al-Ubullah. Uthman went out to him and did not leave a single jar or anything else without breaking it. Uthman said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cursed wine, its drinker, its buyer, its seller, its presser, and its carrier."
"8415","Muhammad ibn Sirin from Uthman ibn Abi al-As. 8388 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal and Ibrahim ibn Nailah al-Asbahani narrated to us, both saying: Shabab al-Usfuri narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Umar narrated to us, Harun al-Ahwazi narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Uthman ibn Abi al-As, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: O Allah, I seek refuge in You from poverty, the punishment of the grave, the trial of life and the trial of death."
"8416","8389 - Muhammad ibn al-Abbas al-Akhram al-Asbahani narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Ubaydullah ibn al-Hasan al-Anbari narrated to us, Khalid ibn al-Harith narrated to us, Ash'ath narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Uthman ibn Abi al-As, that he used to like fasting while traveling, saying: It was a concession."
"8417","8390 - Ahmad ibn Abdullah al-Bazzaz al-Tustari narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Abi Safwan al-Thaqafi narrated to us, Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Uthman ibn Abi al-As, who said: Breaking the fast while traveling is a concession."
"8418","8391 - Ibrahim ibn Hashim al-Baghawi narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Sallam al-Jumahi narrated to us, Dawud ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Attar narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Uthman ibn Abi al-As al-Thaqafi, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The gates of heaven are opened at midnight, and a caller calls out: Is there any supplicant so that he may be answered? Is there any asker so that he may be given? Is there any distressed one so that he may be relieved? No Muslim remains who supplicates with a supplication except that Allah answers him, except an adulteress who trades with her private parts, or a tax collector."
"8419","Abu Nadrah al-Mundhir ibn Malik from Uthman ibn Abi al-As. 8392 - Abu Khalifah al-Fadl ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Khuza'i narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Abu Nadrah, who said: We came to Uthman ibn Abi al-As on a Friday to compare a mushaf of ours with his mushaf. When the prayer time came, he ordered us and we bathed, then we came to the mosque and sat with an old man who was narrating. When Uthman came, we moved to him, and Uthman said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The Muslims have three garrison cities: a city at the junction of the two seas, a city at al-Hirah, and a city in al-Sham. The Muslims will be struck with three panics, and the Dajjal will come out among the fringes of an army and defeat those on the eastern side. The first city he will come to is the city at the junction of the two seas, with seventy thousand wearing cloaks with him, and most of his followers will be Jews and women. Its people will split into three groups: a group that stays, saying: Let us confront him and see what this is; a group that joins the Bedouins; and a group that joins the city next to them. Then he will come to al-Sham, and its people will take refuge at the pass of Afiq. They will send out their grazing livestock, and their livestock will be seized, and that will weigh heavily on them, and severe hunger and hardship will afflict them, until one of them will burn the string of his bow and eat it. While they are like that, a caller will call out before dawn: O people, relief has come to you! And they will say: This is the voice of a satiated man. And Isa ibn Maryam (peace be upon him) will descend at the Fajr prayer, and the people will say to him: O Spirit of Allah, come forward and lead us in prayer. He will say: You, the community of Muhammad, are leaders, some of you over others, so you come forward and lead us in prayer. The commander will come forward and lead them in prayer. Then Isa ibn Maryam will take his spear and set out toward the Dajjal, and when he sees him he will melt as lead melts. He will place his spear between his collarbones and kill him, and rout his companions. On that day nothing will shelter any of them; even a tree will say: O believer, this is a disbeliever, kill him; and a stone will say: O believer, this is a disbeliever, kill him."
"8420","Abu Mihraz from Uthman ibn Abi al-As. 8393 - Ahmad ibn Amr al-Bazzar narrated to us, Hudbah ibn Khalid narrated to us, Mubarak ibn Fadalah narrated to us, from Abu Mihraz, that Uthman ibn Abi al-As came as a delegate to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with some people of Thaqif. They entered upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said to him: Guard our goods - or our mounts - for us. He said: On condition that when you leave, you wait for me until I come out from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: So I entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him for a mushaf that was with him, and he gave it to me, appointed me over them, and made me their imam though I was the youngest of them."
"8421","769 - Uthman ibn Talhah ibn Abi Talhah ibn Abd al-Uzza ibn Uthman ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Dar ibn Qusayy al-Hajabi. He accepted Islam before the Conquest, and his mother was Umm Sa'id bint Shahid, of Banu Amr ibn Awf of the Ansar, of the people of Quba; he accepted Islam before the Conquest. 8394 - Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahim al-Barqi narrated to us, Abd al-Malik ibn Hisham narrated to us, Ziyad ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, who said: The Islam of Amr ibn al-As, Khalid ibn al-Walid, and Uthman ibn Talhah took place at the court of the Negus, and they came to Madinah in Safar in the eighth year of the Hijrah."
"8422","From his reports. 8395 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Uthman ibn Talhah on the day of the Conquest: Bring me the key of the Ka'bah. He was slow with it while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood waiting for him, until sweat like pearls dripped from him, and he kept saying: What is holding him back? A man hastened to him, and the woman who had the key - and I think he said: Uthman's mother - kept saying: If he takes it from you, he will never give it back to you. Uthman kept persuading her until she gave him the key, and he set out with it to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He opened the door, entered the House, then came out with the people accompanying him, and sat by the Siqayah. Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) said to him: O Messenger of Allah, if we were given prophethood, given the Siqayah, and given the Hijabah, what people would have a greater share than us? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seemed to dislike his remark. Then he called Uthman ibn Talhah and handed him the key, saying: Hide it away. Abd al-Razzaq said: I narrated it to Ibn Uyaynah, and he said: Ibn Jurayj informed me - I think from Ibn Abi Mulaykah - that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Ali that day when he spoke to him about the key: I have only given you what you spend on, and I have not given you what you take from - meaning: I gave you the Siqayah because you spend on it, and I did not give you the House, meaning that they take from its gifts. This is the statement of Abd al-Razzaq."
"8423","What Uthman ibn Talhah narrated. 8396 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn Uyaynah, from Mansur ibn Safiyyah, from his maternal uncle, from his mother, from a woman of Banu Sulaym, who said: I asked Uthman: Why did the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) send for you after he came out of the Ka'bah? He said: He said to me: I saw the two horns of the ram and forgot to order you to cover them, for there should not be anything in the House that distracts one who prays."
"8424","8397 - al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Ali al-Muqaddami narrated to us, al-Ala' ibn Akhdar al-Ijli al-Ram narrated to us, Musafi' al-Hajabi narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from my grandfather, that he saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praying two rak'ahs behind the middle pillar of the House - or he said: of the Ka'bah - and in the House there were three pillars."
"8425","8398 - Ahmad ibn Amr al-Khallal al-Makki narrated to us, Ya'qub ibn Humayd narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Uthman ibn Talhah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed inside the House."
"8426","770 - Uthman ibn al-Azraq. 8399 - Muhammad ibn Ali al-Jarudi al-Asbahani narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Mu'awiyah al-Ziyadi narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Harun Abu Qurrah narrated to us, Hisham ibn Ziyad narrated to us, Ammar ibn Sa'd narrated to us, who said: Uthman ibn al-Azraq entered upon us in the mosque on Friday while the imam was delivering the sermon; he shortened his prayer and sat in the mosque. We said: May Allah have mercy on you, if you had joined us it would have been easier for you. He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever steps over people's necks on Friday after the imam has come out, or separates two people, will be like one dragging his intestines in the Fire."
"8427","771 - Uthman ibn Amr al-Ansari, a Badri. 8400 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Abu al-Aswad, from Urwah, regarding the naming of those of the Ansar who were present at Badr: Uthman ibn Amr ibn Rifa'ah ibn al-Harith ibn Sawad."
"8428","Those named Abdullah. 772 - Abdullah ibn Mas'ud al-Hudhali, kunyah Abu Abd al-Rahman, an ally of Banu Zuhrah, a Badri; he was among those who migrated to the land of Abyssinia in the first migration. The lineage of Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, his age and death, and from his reports, virtues, speech, and fatwas. 8401 - Ahmad ibn Rushdin al-Misri narrated to us, saying: Musa ibn Awn ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah ibn Mas'ud dictated to me the lineage of Abdullah ibn Mas'ud: Abdullah ibn Mas'ud ibn Kahil ibn Habib ibn Tamir ibn Makhzum ibn Sahilah ibn Kahil ibn al-Harith ibn Tamim ibn Sa'd ibn Hudhayl ibn Mudrikah ibn Ilyas ibn Mudar ibn Nizar."
"8429","8402 - And Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahim al-Barqi narrated to us, Abd al-Malik ibn Hisham narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Abdullah, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, who said: Abdullah ibn Mas'ud ibn al-Harith ibn Shamkh ibn Makhzum ibn Sahilah ibn al-Harith ibn Tamim ibn Hudhayl ibn Mudrikah ibn Ilyas ibn Mudar ibn Nizar ibn Ma'dd ibn Adnan, an ally of Banu Zuhrah, was present at Badr."
"8430","8403 - Muhammad ibn Ali Fustuqah narrated to us, al-Husayn ibn Ali ibn Yazid al-Suda'i narrated to us, Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from his father, from Ibn Ishaq, who said: Abdullah ibn Mas'ud ibn al-Harith ibn Shamkh ibn Makhzum ibn Kahil ibn al-Harith ibn Sa'd ibn Hudhayl, among the allies of Banu Zuhrah."
"8431","8404 - Abu al-Zinba' Rawh ibn al-Faraj al-Misri narrated to us, Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated to us, saying: Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, kunyah Abu Abd al-Rahman, died at the age of a little over sixty years, in the year thirty-two, in Madinah. He entrusted his affairs to al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam, who led his funeral prayer, and he was buried in al-Baqi'."
"8432","8405 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Abu Kurayb narrated to us, Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Abi Sulayman, from Abu Hashim, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Abdullah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave him the kunyah Abu Abd al-Rahman though no child had been born to him."
"8433","8406 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Abu Kurayb narrated to us, (ha) and Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, both saying: Muhammad ibn Abi Ubaydah narrated to us, from his father, from al-A'mash, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from Ibn Mas'ud, who said: I saw myself as one of six, with no Muslim on the face of the earth besides us."
"8434","The description of Abdullah ibn Mas'ud. 8407 - Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Hubayrah ibn Yarim, who said: Ibn Mas'ud used to wash his head then leave his hair behind his ears."
"8435","8408 - Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Abu Usamah narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, who said: I saw Abdullah ibn Mas'ud as a slender, thin man."
"8436","8409 - Muhammad ibn Salih ibn al-Walid al-Narsi narrated to us, Abu Hafs Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, Safwan ibn Isa narrated to us, from al-Harith ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Dhubab, from Mujahid, from Abu Ma'mar, that Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) had two braids, bore the appearance of the people of the pre-Islamic era, and had thin shanks."
"8437","From the virtues of Ibn Mas'ud. 8410 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Za'idah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Shaqiq, from Masruq, who said: We mentioned Abdullah ibn Mas'ud in the presence of Abdullah ibn Amr, and Abdullah ibn Amr said: He is a man whom I will always love after something I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Learn the Qur'an from four men: from Ibn Umm Abd - beginning with him - and from Ubayy ibn Ka'b, and from Salim, the freedman of Abu Hudhayfah, and from Mu'adh ibn Jabal."
"8438","8411 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, (ha) and Abu Khalifah al-Fadl ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, both saying: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Wa'il, from Masruq, from Abdullah ibn Amr, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Learn the Qur'an from four: from Ubayy ibn Ka'b, Mu'adh ibn Jabal, Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, and Salim, the freedman of Abu Hudhayfah."
"8439","8412 - al-Husayn ibn Ja'far al-Qattat al-Kufi narrated to us, Minjab ibn al-Harith narrated to us, Ali ibn Mushir narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Wa'il, from Masruq, from Abdullah ibn Amr, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Learn the Qur'an from four: from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud - beginning with him - and from Ubayy ibn Ka'b, Mu'adh ibn Jabal, and Salim, the freedman of Abu Hudhayfah."
"8440","A chapter. 8413 - Uthman ibn Umar al-Dabbi narrated to us, Amr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Ubaydah, from Abdullah, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by, accompanied by Abu Bakr and Umar, and they passed by Abdullah while he was praying. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Ibn Umm Abd, ask and you will be given. Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Abu Bakr and I raced, and whenever we raced toward good, he beat me to it, and he gave him the glad tidings. Abdullah said: I know only that among my supplication which I hardly ever omit in my prayer is: O Allah, I ask You for bliss that does not end, coolness of eyes that does not perish, and the companionship of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the highest Garden of Eternity."
"8441","8414 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Zuhayr narrated to us, Abu Ishaq narrated to us, he heard Abu Ubaydah mention, from Abdullah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out, he and Abu Bakr and Umar (may Allah be pleased with them); Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) had invited them, and they went out from his house to the mosque, the mosque of Madinah, in which Abdullah was standing praying and reciting. Then he sat, bore tashahhud, praised Allah as He deserves in the finest manner a man praises, invoked blessings on His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then beseeched in supplication while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept saying: Ask and you will be given. Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Who is this, O Messenger of Allah? He said: This is Abdullah Ibn Umm Abd. Whoever wishes to recite the Qur'an fresh as it was revealed, let him recite as Ibn Umm Abd recited. Abu Bakr and Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) hastened to him, and Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) beat him to it. Umar mentioned that Abu Bakr had beaten him, and Umar said: He was ever foremost in good deeds."
"8442","8415 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Bakkar narrated to us, Hudayj ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Ubaydah, from Abdullah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) invited Abu Bakr and Umar, and they ate dinner with him. When they finished, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out between Abu Bakr and Umar to Ibn Mas'ud while he was standing praying. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever wishes to recite the Qur'an fresh as it was revealed, let him recite upon the recitation of Ibn Umm Abd."
"8443","8416 - Ubayd al-Ijli narrated to us, Abu Kurayb narrated to us, Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Ubaydah, who said: Abdullah was asked: What was it that you supplicated with on the night the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to you: Ask and you will be given? He said: O Allah, I ask You for faith that does not turn back, bliss that does not end, and the companionship of Your Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the highest degree of Eternity."
"8444","8417 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Za'idah narrated to us, Asim ibn Abi al-Najud narrated to us, from Zirr, from Abdullah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to him between Abu Bakr and Umar, while Abdullah was praying; he had begun Surat al-Nisa' and recited it through. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever loves to recite the Qur'an fresh as it was revealed, let him recite the recitation of Ibn Umm Abd. Then he sat, then asked, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept saying: Ask and you will be given, ask and you will be given. Among what he asked, he said: O Allah, I ask You for faith that does not turn back, bliss that does not end, and the companionship of Your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the Garden of Eternity. Then Umar came to Abdullah ibn Mas'ud to give him glad tidings and found Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) coming out, having beaten him to it. He said: If you did so, you were indeed foremost in good deeds."
"8445","8418 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Abi al-Rabi' al-Samman narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Salamah ibn Abi al-Husam narrated to us, Sharik ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Namir narrated to us, from Awn ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Ibn Mas'ud, that while he was reciting in the mosque, the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by while he was supplicating; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out with Abu Bakr and Umar (may Allah be pleased with them), and when he drew level with him he heard his supplication though he did not recognize him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Ask and you will be given. Then Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) returned to Ibn Mas'ud and said: What was the supplication you supplicated with? He said: I praised Allah and glorified Him, then said: O Allah, there is no god but You; Your promise is true, meeting You is true, Paradise is true, the Fire is true, Your messengers are true, the prophets are true, and Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is true."
"8446","8419 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Sharik ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Namir, from Awn ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah, from his father, who said: While Ibn Mas'ud was in the mosque supplicating with a supplication, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Abu Bakr passed by him; when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) drew level with him he heard his supplication, though the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not recognize him. He said: Who is this? Ask and you will be given. Then Abu Bakr returned to Abdullah and said: The supplication you were supplicating with? He said: I praised Allah and glorified Him, then said: O Allah, there is no god but You; Your promise is the truth, meeting You is true, Your Book is true, the prophets are true, and Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is true, Paradise is true, the Fire is true, and Your messengers are true."
"8447","8420 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz and Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, both saying: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, al-A'mash narrated to us, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, who said: A man came to Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) and said: I have come to you from a man who dictates the codices from memory. Umar was alarmed and said: Woe to you, consider what you say, and he grew angry. He said: I have brought you nothing but the truth. He said: Who is he? He said: Abdullah ibn Mas'ud. He said: I know no one more entitled to that than him. And I shall narrate to you about Abdullah: We spent an evening in a house at Abu Bakr's (may Allah be pleased with him) concerning some need of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then we went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) walking between me and Abu Bakr. When we reached the mosque, there was a man reciting; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood listening to him. I said: O Messenger of Allah, night has grown late. He nudged me with his hand to be silent. He recited, bowed, prostrated, sat supplicating and seeking forgiveness, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Ask and you will be given. Then he said: Whoever wishes to recite the Qur'an moist as it was revealed, let him recite the recitation of Ibn Umm Abd. My companion and I knew that it was Abdullah. In the morning I went to him early to give him glad tidings, and he said: Abu Bakr beat you to it; never did I race him to good but he beat me to it."
"8448","8421 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Ubayd al-Qasim ibn Sallam narrated to us, Mus'ab ibn al-Miqdam narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever wishes to recite the Qur'an fresh, let him recite it upon the recitation of Ibn Umm Abd."
"8449","8422 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Za'idah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, and from Khaythamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Qays ibn Marwan, both saying: A man came to Umar and said: O Commander of the Faithful, I have left in Iraq a man who dictates the codices from memory. Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) grew angry until his face reddened, and Umar said to him: Know what you say - several times. He said: I inform you of nothing but the truth. Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Who is he? He said: Ibn Umm Abd. He said: He calmed down until the anger left him, then said: No one on the face of the earth has become more entitled to that than him. And I shall narrate to you about that: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) spent the evening in the house of Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) one night for some need until night grew late, then returned between me and Abu Bakr until we reached the mosque, when a man of the Emigrants was praying. He stood listening to his recitation; neither I nor my companion knew who he was. He said: When he had stood a while, I said: O Messenger of Allah, a man of the Emigrants is praying; if only you would return, for night has grown late. He said: He nudged me, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept listening to his recitation. He said: He bowed and prostrated, then sat supplicating, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept saying: Ask and you will be given, ask and you will be given - while neither I nor my companion knew who he was - until we heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever wishes to recite the Qur'an fresh as it was revealed, let him recite as Ibn Umm Abd recites. He said: That was when my companion and I knew who he was. He said: In the morning I went to Abdullah to give him glad tidings, and he said: Abu Bakr has beaten you to it. By Allah, never did I race Abu Bakr to good but he beat me to it."
"8450","8423 - Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Furat ibn Mahbub narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, that Abu Bakr and Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) gave him glad tidings that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever wishes to recite the Qur'an as it was revealed, let him recite it upon the recitation of Ibn Mas'ud."
"8451","8424 - Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn Bukayr al-Tayalisi narrated to us, Ubaydullah ibn A'ishah narrated to us, Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Ubaydullah narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Yazid al-Nakha'i narrated to us, from Alqamah ibn Qays, from al-Qartha', from Qays, or Ibn Qays, from Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by - I and Abu Bakr with him - by Ibn Mas'ud while he was reciting. He stood listening to his recitation, then Abdullah bowed and prostrated. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever wishes to recite the Qur'an as it was revealed, let him recite from Ibn Umm Abd. He said: I set out early in the dark to Ibn Mas'ud to give him glad tidings of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had said. When I knocked on the door he heard my voice and said: What brings you? I said: I have come to give you glad tidings of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said. He said: Abu Bakr has beaten you to it. I said: If he does so, he is indeed foremost in good deeds; never did we race to good but Abu Bakr beat me to it."
"8452","8425 - Aslam ibn Sahl al-Wasiti narrated to us, Zakariyya ibn Yahya Zahmawayh narrated to us, Ali ibn Hashim narrated to us, Habib ibn Hassan narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Wahb, who said: A man came to Umar ibn al-Khattab and said: O Commander of the Faithful, I have left in Kufah a man who dictates the codices from memory. Umar grew angry until his face swelled, then said: Who is he? He said: Abdullah ibn Mas'ud. He said: May your father be bereft of you - who is more entitled to that than him? I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever wishes to recite the Qur'an fresh as it was revealed, let him recite it upon the recitation of Ibn Umm Abd."
"8453","A chapter. 8426 - al-Hasan ibn al-Abbas al-Razi narrated to us, Sahl ibn Uthman narrated to us, Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from his father, (ha) and Salamah ibn Ibrahim ibn Isma'il ibn Yahya ibn Salamah ibn Kuhayl narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from his father, from his grandfather, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Abu al-Za'ra', from Abdullah, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Follow the two after me, Abu Bakr and Umar; follow the guidance of Ammar; and hold fast to the covenant of Abdullah ibn Mas'ud."
"8454","A chapter. 8427 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Za'idah narrated to us, Sulayman narrated to us, from Shaqiq, who said: Abdullah addressed us when the codices were rent asunder while the mosque was full of the people of Badr. He said: The Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) know that I am the most knowledgeable of them in the Book of Allah - Shaqiq said: Then I saw that he felt shy at what he had said - and he said: Yet I am not the best of them. If I knew a man more knowledgeable in the Book of Allah than me, reachable by camel, I would go to him. Shaqiq said: No sooner had Abdullah finished his sermon than I sat in the circles to hear what they said, and I heard no one objecting to that."
"8455","8428 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Mu'alla ibn Mahdi narrated to us, Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Wa'il, who said: When Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) ordered regarding the codices what he ordered, Abdullah stood, praised Allah, then said: O people, Allah Almighty says: And whoever defrauds will bring what he defrauded on the Day of Resurrection. So conceal the codices! Upon whose recitation do you order me to recite? Upon the recitation of Zayd ibn Thabit? By Him besides whom there is no god, I took from the mouth of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) over seventy surahs, while Zayd ibn Thabit had two forelocks and played with boys. By Him besides whom there is no god, if I knew anyone more knowledgeable in the Book of Allah than me, I would go to him."
"8456","8429 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Za'idah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Muslim, from Masruq, from Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: By Allah, no surah of the Qur'an was revealed but I know where it was revealed, and no verse of it was revealed but I know concerning what it was revealed. If I knew anyone more knowledgeable in the Book of Allah than me, reachable by camel, I would go to him."
"8457","8430 - Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Abbas al-Asbahani narrated to us, Sahl ibn Uthman narrated to us, Aththam ibn Ali narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Muslim, from Masruq, from Abdullah, who said: By Him besides whom there is no god, no surah of the Qur'an was revealed but I know where it was revealed, and no verse but I know concerning whom it was revealed. If I knew anyone more knowledgeable in the Book of Allah than me, reachable by camel, I would ride to him."
"8458","8431 - Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Bahram narrated to us, Su'ayr ibn al-Khums narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Abu al-Duha, from Masruq, from Abdullah, who said: There is no verse of the Book of Allah but I know where it was revealed and concerning whom it was revealed. If I knew anyone more knowledgeable in the Book of Allah than me, reachable by camel, I would journey to him."
"8459","8432 - Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Qattan narrated to us, Yahya ibn Adam narrated to us, Qutbah ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Ishaq, from Masruq, from Abdullah, who said: By Him besides whom there is no god, there is no surah in the Book of Allah but I know where it was revealed, and there is no verse in it but I know concerning what it was revealed."
"8460","A chapter. 8433 - Ahmad ibn Amr al-Qitrani al-Basri narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Tufayl al-Nakha'i narrated to us, Sharik narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Aswad, who said: It was said to Abdullah: Recite upon the recitation of Zayd. He said: Should I abandon my recitation for the recitation of Zayd, when I recited seventy surahs from the mouth of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was a boy with two forelocks?"
"8461","8434 - Uthman ibn Amr al-Dabbi narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Raja' narrated to us, Isra'il narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Khumayr ibn Malik, who said: When the codices were ordered to be altered, that grieved Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, and he said: Whoever of you is able to conceal a mushaf, let him do so, for whoever conceals anything will bring what he concealed on the Day of Resurrection. Then Abdullah said: I recited the Qur'an from the mouth of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), seventy surahs, while Zayd was a boy. Should I abandon what I took from the mouth of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)?"
"8462","8435 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Waki' narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Abu Ishaq, from Khumayr ibn Malik, from Abdullah, who said: I recited from the mouth of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seventy surahs, while Zayd ibn Thabit had two forelocks in the kuttab."
"8463","8436 - Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Mandah al-Asbahani narrated to us, Bishr ibn Khalid al-Askari narrated to us, Yahya ibn Adam narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Khumayr ibn Malik, who said: Abdullah said: I recited from the mouth of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seventy surahs, while Zayd ibn Thabit was a boy with a forelock playing with children."
"8464","8437 - Abu Sa'd Yahya ibn Mansur al-Harawi and Musa ibn Harun narrated to us, both saying: Ishaq ibn Rahawayh narrated to us, (ha) and Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Amr ibn Aban narrated to me, both saying: Abdah ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Ishaq, from Hubayrah ibn Yarim, from Abdullah, who said: Upon whose recitation do you order me to recite? I recited to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seventy surahs, while Zayd ibn Thabit was a boy with a forelock playing with lads."
"8465","8438 - Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna ibn Mu'adh narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Ibn Awn narrated to us, Amr ibn Qays narrated to me, from Amr ibn Shurahbil, or Ibn Sharahil, Abu Maysarah al-Hamdani, who said: A man came to me while I was praying and said: Do you not see that you are praying while the Book of Allah has been ordered to be torn up? I shortened my prayer - I was not one to be detained - and entered the house unhindered, climbed up unhindered, and there were al-Ash'ari, Hudhayfah, and Ibn Mas'ud disputing, Hudhayfah saying to Ibn Mas'ud: Hand over this mushaf to them. He said: No, by Allah, I shall not hand it over to them. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught me over seventy times, then should I hand it over to them? By Allah, I shall not hand it over to them."
"8466","8439 - Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Mudrik narrated to us, Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Sulayman ibn Qays, from Abu Sa'd al-Azdi, that he heard Abdullah ibn Mas'ud say: I received from the mouth of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seventy surahs, which I had mastered before Zayd ibn Thabit accepted Islam, when he had a forelock and played with boys."
"8467","8440 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal and Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, both saying: Sa'id ibn Abi al-Rabi' al-Samman narrated to us, al-Haysam ibn al-Shaddakh narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Yahya ibn Watthab, from Alqamah, from Abdullah, who said: I wonder at the people abandoning my recitation and taking the recitation of Zayd ibn Thabit, when I took from the mouth of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seventy surahs, while Zayd ibn Thabit was a boy with a forelock."
"8468","8441 - al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Ubaydah narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Razin, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, who said: Abdullah ibn Mas'ud said: I recited from the mouth of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) over seventy surahs, while Zayd ibn Thabit had two forelocks."
"8469","8442 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz and Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, both saying: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Asim, from Zirr, from Abdullah, who said: I took from the mouth of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seventy surahs, over which no one disputes with me."
"8470","8443 - Ahmad ibn Zuhayr al-Tustari narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Karamah narrated to us, Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, from Shayban, from al-A'mash, from Abu al-Duha, from Masruq, from Abdullah, who said: I recited from the mouth of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seventy surahs."
"8471","8444 - Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Yahya al-Umawi narrated to us, my father narrated to us, al-A'mash narrated to us, from Thuwayr ibn Abi Fakhitah, from his father, from Ibn Mas'ud, who said: I recited at the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) over seventy surahs, while Zayd ibn Thabit had two forelocks and played with boys."
"8472","8445 - Ahmad ibn Zuhayr narrated to us, Mu'ammar ibn Sahl narrated to us, Amir ibn Mudrik narrated to us, Isra'il narrated to us, Thuwayr narrated to us, from his father, from Abdullah, who said: I recited from the mouth of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seventy surahs, while Zayd ibn Thabit had not yet believed in Allah."
"8473","8446 - Ubayd ibn Kathir al-Tammar al-Kufi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Junayd narrated to us, Yahya ibn Salim narrated to us, from Hashim ibn al-Barid, from Bayan ibn Abi Bishr, from Zadham, from Abdullah, who said: I recited to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seventy surahs, and I completed the Qur'an under the best of people, Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"8474","8447 - al-Husayn ibn Ulwayh al-Qattan narrated to us, Ali ibn Siyabah narrated to us, Amr ibn Jarir al-Bajali narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Musa al-Ansari, from Abu Hashim al-Rummani, from Zadham Abu Imran, from Ibn Mas'ud, who said to him: O my brother, we have a gathering, so come to us. I came to him in his gathering and learned from him seventy surahs. Abdullah said to me: I took them from the mouth of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); Jibril (peace be upon him) brought them down from the Lord of the worlds, Almighty and Exalted."
"8475","8448 - Idris ibn Abd al-Karim al-Haddad narrated to us, Asim ibn Ali narrated to us, Abu Shihab narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Wa'il, from Abdullah, who said: How do you order me to recite upon the recitation of Zayd ibn Thabit? I recited from the mouth of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seventy surahs."
"8476","8449 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Za'idah narrated to us, from al-Hasan ibn Ubaydullah, from Ibrahim ibn Suwayd, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, that Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to them that the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Your permission regarding me is that you lift the curtain, and that you hear my private talk until I forbid you. Al-Hasan said: al-sawad means confidences."
"8477","8450 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Za'idah narrated to us, saying: Sulayman said: I heard them mention, from Ibrahim ibn Suwayd, from Alqamah, from Abdullah, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Your permission regarding me is to uncover the curtain."
"8478","8451 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, al-Mas'udi narrated to us, from Ayyash al-Amiri, from Abdullah ibn Shaddad ibn al-Had, that Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) was the keeper of the cushion, the siwak, and the sandals."
"8479","A chapter. 8452 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz and Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, both saying: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Asim, from Zirr, from Abdullah, who said: I used to gather siwak sticks of arak for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); the wind would bend him over, and there was thinness in his shanks, so the people laughed. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What are you laughing at, at the thinness of his shanks? By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, they are heavier in the Balance than Uhud."
"8480","8453 - Ahmad ibn Zuhayr al-Tustari narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Azdi narrated to us, Ja'far ibn Awn narrated to us, from al-Mu'alla ibn Irfan, from Abu Wa'il, from Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, the two shanks of Ibn Mas'ud on the Day of Resurrection will surely be weightier and greater than Uhud."
"8481","8454 - Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Ja'far ibn Musafir al-Tinnisi narrated to us, Ibn Abi Fudayk narrated to us, from Musa ibn Ya'qub, from Ibn Abi Harmalah, freedman of Huwaytib: that Sarah bint Abdullah ibn Mas'ud informed him, that her father informed her, saying: While he was walking behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his companions - or some of them - nudged him, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, Abdullah in the balances on the Day of Resurrection will surely be heavier than Uhud - as though they had marveled at his slightness."
"8482","A chapter. 8455 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz and Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, both saying: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Asim, from Zirr, from Abdullah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) came to me, having fled from the polytheists, while I was herding sheep of Ibn Abi Mu'ayt at Jiyad. He said: O boy, do you have milk to give us to drink? I said: I am entrusted, and I shall not give you drink. He said: Do you have a young she-goat over which no male has yet mounted? I said: Yes. I brought it to him; he wiped the udder, the udder filled, and Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) brought him a hollowed stone. He milked, drank, gave drink, and gave Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) drink, and gave me drink. And he said to the udder: Contract - and it contracted. Then I came to him afterwards and said: O Messenger of Allah, teach me from this speech - or from this Qur'an. He said: You are a taught boy. So I took from the mouth of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seventy surahs over which no one disputes with me."
"8483","8456 - Khalaf ibn Amr al-Ukbari narrated to us, Ya'la ibn Mahdi al-Mawsili narrated to us, Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Asim, from Zirr, from Abdullah, who said: I was among sheep of Uqbah ibn Abi Mu'ayt when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to me and said: O boy, do you have any milk with you? I said: Yes, but I am entrusted. He said: Bring me a ewe over which no male has mounted. I brought him a young goat or a young ewe; he kept wiping the udder and supplicating until it let down milk. Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) brought a stone and he milked into it, then handed it to Abu Bakr and he drank, then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) drank after him, then said to the udder: Contract, by Allah's permission - and it contracted and returned to what it was. Later I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Teach me from this Qur'an or from this speech. He wiped my head and said: You are a taught boy. I took from his mouth seventy surahs over which no human disputes with me."
"8484","8457 - Muhammad ibn al-Marzuban al-Adami al-Shirazi narrated to us, Sahl ibn Uthman narrated to us, Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to us, from Abu Ayyub al-Ifriqi, from Asim, from Zirr, from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by me while I was among sheep of Uqbah; he wiped my head and said: May Allah have mercy on you, you are a taught young boy."
"8485","8458 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Za'idah narrated to us, from Mansur, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, who said: I was told that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I am pleased for my Ummah with what Ibn Umm Abd is pleased with for it."
"8486","A chapter. 8459 - al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Husayn ibn Ali al-Ju'fi narrated to us, from Za'idah, from Asim, from Zirr, from Abdullah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Recite. So he began Surat al-Nisa' until he reached: So how will it be when We bring from every nation a witness and bring you against these as a witness - the verse - and his eyes shed tears, and he said: It suffices us."
"8487","8460 - Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from Abidah, from Abdullah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Recite. I said: O Messenger of Allah, shall I recite to you when it was revealed to you? He said: Yes. So I recited Surat al-Nisa' until I reached this verse: So how will it be when We bring from every nation a witness and bring you against these as a witness. He said: Enough now. I turned to him and his eyes were shedding tears."
"8488","8461 - al-Husayn ibn Ja'far al-Qattat al-Kufi narrated to us, Minjab ibn al-Harith narrated to us, Ali ibn Mushir narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from Abidah, from Abdullah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Recite Surat al-Nisa' to me - then he mentioned the like."
"8489","8462 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal and al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Ma'mari narrated to us, Abu Kamil al-Jahdari narrated to us, al-Mufaddal ibn Muhammad al-Kufi narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Muhajir, from Ibrahim, from Abidah, from Abdullah, that he recited to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until he came to this verse: So how will it be when We bring from every nation a witness and bring you against these as a witness. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wept, and said in the hadith: Whoever loves to recite the Qur'an fresh as it was revealed, let him recite it upon the recitation of Ibn Umm Abd."
"8490","8463 - Ahmad ibn Amr al-Bazzar narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Malik al-Tustari narrated to us, al-Mufaddal ibn Muhammad al-Nahwi narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Muhajir narrated to us, from al-A'mash and Mughirah, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Abdullah, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked me to recite Surat al-Nisa' while he was on the minbar. I recited it until I reached: So how will it be when We bring from every nation a witness and bring you against these as a witness - the eyes of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) filled with tears, and he said: Whoever wishes to recite the Qur'an as it was revealed, let him recite it upon the recitation of Ibn Umm Abd."
"8491","8464 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshi and Yusuf al-Qadi narrated to us, both saying: Amr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Shu'bah informed us, from Ibrahim ibn Muhajir, from Ibrahim al-Nakha'i, from Ibn Mas'ud, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Recite the Qur'an to me. Ibn Mas'ud said: O Messenger of Allah, shall I recite the Qur'an to you when it was revealed to you? He said: I love to hear it from other than myself. So he recited to him until he reached: So how will it be when We bring from every nation a witness and bring you against these as a witness - and the eyes of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) filled with tears, and he said: Whoever wishes to recite the Qur'an as it was revealed, let him recite it as the recitation of Ibn Umm Abd. Thus Amr ibn Marzuq and the companions of Shu'bah narrated it, and Sulayman ibn Harb joined it as continuous."
"8492","8465 - Bakr ibn Ahmad ibn Muqbil al-Basri narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn Zayd ibn Ibrahim al-Tustari narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Muhajir, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Abdullah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like."
"8493","8466 - Aslam ibn Sahl al-Wasiti narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Aban narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Abu Razin, who said: Ibn Mas'ud said: I recited Surat al-Nisa' to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); when I came to this verse: So how will it be when We bring from every nation a witness and bring you against these as a witness - his eyes overflowed."
"8494","8467 - Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah and Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, both saying: Hannad ibn al-Sari narrated to us, Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Abdullah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered me to recite to him while he was on the minbar. I recited Surat al-Nisa' to him until I reached: So how will it be when We bring from every nation a witness and bring you against these as a witness - the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) nudged me; I looked at him and his eyes were shedding tears, shedding tears."
"8495","8468 - Ahmad ibn Amr al-Qitrani narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Tufayl al-Nakha'i narrated to us, Sharik narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Ubaydah, that Abdullah struck Abu Jahl but accomplished nothing. He said: The little shepherd of yesterday? He said: That is he. So he took his sword and struck him until he killed him."
"8496","8469 - al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Yahya al-Himmani narrated to us, Sharik narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Ubaydah, from Abdullah, who said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Badr and said: I have killed Abu Jahl. He said: By Allah besides whom there is no god, did you kill him? I said: By Allah besides whom there is no god, I killed him. Joy made him light, and he said: Go, show him to me. I set out with him until I stood him over his head. He said: Praise be to Allah who disgraced you; this is the Pharaoh of this Ummah. Drag him to the well. He said: I had struck him with my sword and it made no mark on him, so I took his sword and struck him with it until I killed him. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave me his sword as spoils."
"8497","8470 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi al-Muqaddami narrated to me, Attham ibn Ali narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Ubaydah, from Abdullah, who said: I reached Abu Jahl while he was fallen, wearing his helmet, with a fine sword beside him, while I had a poor sword. I kept pecking at his head with my sword, recalling a pecking with which he used to peck my head in Makkah, until my hand weakened. So I took his sword; he raised his head and said: For whom was the turn, for us or against us? Are you not our little shepherd in Makkah? I killed him. Then I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I killed Abu Jahl. He said: By Allah besides whom there is no god? He made me swear three times, then stood with me to them, and supplicated against them."
"8498","8471 - al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Abu Kurayb narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Yusuf narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Ishaq, Abu Ubaydah narrated to me, from Abdullah, who said: When Allah routed the polytheists on the day of Badr, I passed by and there was Abu Jahl fallen, his leg struck. I said: O enemy of Allah, O Abu Jahl, Allah has disgraced the other. He said: I did not fear him at that. He said: Far removed is a man killed by his own people. I struck him with a sword of mine, of no avail, and it did me no good, until his sword fell from his hand. I took it and struck him until he grew cold. Then I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) hastening, to come as the fastest of Allah's creation in running, until I came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, Allah has killed Abu Jahl. He said: By Allah besides whom there is no god? I said: By Allah besides whom there is no god, I have killed him. He said: How? I narrated to him how it had happened and how I had found him. He said: By Allah besides whom there is no god? He said: I said: By Allah besides whom there is no god, I have killed him. He said takbir, then said: Praise be to Allah who fulfilled His promise and aided His servant. Then he set out until he came to him, then said: This is the Pharaoh of this Ummah."
"8499","8472 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, my father narrated to us, (ha) and Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, both saying: Umayyah ibn Khalid narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Ubaydah, from Abdullah, who said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, Allah Almighty has killed Abu Jahl. He said: Praise be to Allah who fulfilled His promise, aided His servant, and honored His religion."
"8500","8473 - Muhammad ibn Muhammad al-Tammar al-Basri narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Anbari narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Ubaydah, from Abdullah, who said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the head of Abu Jahl and said: This is the head of Abu Jahl. He said: By Allah besides whom there is no god? And such was his oath. I said: By Allah besides whom there is no god, this is indeed the head of Abu Jahl. He said: This is the Pharaoh of this Ummah."
"8501","8474 - al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Abu al-Mu'afa Muhammad ibn Wahb ibn Abi Karima al-Harrani narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Tamalah narrated to us, from Abu Abd al-Rahim, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr ibn Maymun, from Ibn Mas'ud, who said: I found Abu Jahl fallen on the day of Badr and said: O enemy of Allah, Allah has disgraced you. He said: And in what has Allah disgraced me, from a man you have killed? I had a sword of mine with me and kept striking him, but it made no mark on him; he had a fine sword of his. I struck his hand and the sword fell from his hand, so I took it, uncovered the helmet from his head, and struck his neck. Then I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him. He said: By Allah besides whom there is no god? I said: By Allah besides whom there is no god. He said: Go and verify. I set out, hastening like a bird, then returned, hastening like a bird, laughing, and informed him. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Go and show me. I set out with him and showed him. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood over him, he said: This is the Pharaoh of this Ummah. Thus Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah narrated it, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr ibn Maymun, and Abu Waki' followed him."
"8502","8475 - Muhammad ibn Dawud al-Tawwazi al-Basri narrated to us, Mahmud ibn Ghaylan narrated to us, Abu Dawud narrated to us, Abu Waki' narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr ibn Maymun, from Abdullah, who said: I came upon Abu Jahl on the day of Badr while he was fallen; I struck him with a blunt sword I had. His sword slipped from his hand, so I took it and killed him. Then I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a hot day as though I were being lifted from the ground, and said: O Messenger of Allah, did you not see that Allah has killed Abu Jahl? He said: By Allah? I said: By Allah - until he made me swear three times. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Go and show him to me. I set out to him; when he looked at him he said: This was the Pharaoh of this Ummah."
"8503","8476 - Ahmad ibn Zuhayr al-Tustari narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Mansur al-Tusi narrated to us, Shabbabah ibn Sawwar narrated to us, Abu Bakr al-Hudhali narrated to us, Abu al-Malih narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Mas'ud narrated to us, from his father, who said: On the day of Badr I reached Abu Jahl, having been held back; I took his sword and struck his head with it. He said: Our little shepherd in Makkah. I struck him with his sword until he grew cold. Then I came to the Prophet and said: O Messenger of Allah, I killed Abu Jahl. Aqil, who was a captive with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), said: You have lied; you did not kill him. I said: Rather you are the lying sinner, O enemy of Allah; by Allah, I have killed him. He said: What is his mark? I said: On his thigh is a ring-like scar like the ring of a close-fitting camel halter. He said: You have told the truth."
"8504","8477 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Amr narrated to me, Za'idah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Zayd ibn Wahb, who said: While we were sitting with Umar, Abdullah came, so short that sitting nearly matched him in height; Umar laughed when he saw him. He said: He kept speaking to Umar and jesting with him while standing over him, then turned away, and Umar followed him with his gaze until he disappeared. Then he said: A small vessel filled with understanding."
"8505","8478 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Harithah ibn Mudarrib, who said: Umar wrote to the people of Kufah: I have sent Ammar as commander, and Abdullah ibn Mas'ud as teacher and minister; they are among the eminent ones of the Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of the people of Badr and Uhud. So follow them, listen to their word; I have preferred you with Abdullah over myself."
"8506","8479 - Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, Khalaf ibn al-Walid narrated to us, Isra'il narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, that he was approached regarding the inheritance of two paternal cousins, one of whom was also a maternal brother. They said: Ibn Mas'ud gave him all the wealth. He said: May Allah have mercy on Ibn Mas'ud; he was indeed a jurist. But I shall give him the share of a maternal brother on account of his mother, then divide the wealth between them."
"8507","A chapter. 8480 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Za'idah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Shaqiq, who said: I was sitting with Hudhayfah when Abdullah ibn Mas'ud came forward. Hudhayfah said: The people most resembling the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in guidance, demeanor, purpose, and address, from when he leaves his house until he returns - and I do not know what he does with his family - is Abdullah ibn Mas'ud. By Allah, the fortunate ones among the Companions of Muhammad know that Abdullah is among the nearest of them in means on the Day of Resurrection."
"8508","8481 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Muhammad ibn Ja'far narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-A'mash, from Abu Wa'il, from Hudhayfah, who said: The fortunate ones among the Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) know that Ibn Umm Abd is the nearest of them to Allah in means on the Day of Resurrection."
"8509","8482 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz and Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, both saying: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, Mahdi ibn Maymun narrated to us, from Wasil al-Ahdab, from Abu Wa'il, from Hudhayfah, who said: The fortunate ones among the Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) know that Ibn Mas'ud is among the nearest of them to Allah in means on the Day of Resurrection."
"8510","8483 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz and Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, both saying: al-Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, Ghundar narrated to us, from Abu Wa'il, who said: Hudhayfah said: The fortunate ones among the Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) know that Ibn Umm Abd is among the nearest of them to Allah in means on the Day of Resurrection. He said: And he said: Among you is one who most resembles people in demeanor and character to your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from when he leaves his house until he returns to it. He said: It was said: Who is he? He said: Ibn Umm Abd."
"8511","8484 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Bashshar al-Ramadi narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, Jami' ibn Abi Rashid narrated to us, from Abu Wa'il, from Hudhayfah, who said: I have not seen a man more resembling the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in demeanor, bearing, or guidance, from when he leaves his house until he returns to it, than this one - meaning Abdullah ibn Mas'ud. And the fortunate ones among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) know that he is among the nearest of them in means to Allah on the Day of Resurrection."
"8512","8485 - Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn al-Hasan al-Tha'labi narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Bukayr al-Ghanawi narrated to us, from Hakim ibn Jubayr, from Abu Wa'il, who said: I was sitting with Hudhayfah when Ibn Mas'ud and Abu Musa passed by; Hudhayfah disliked leaving them, and said: The people most resembling the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he leaves his house until he returns to his family, is Abdullah ibn Mas'ud - and Allah knows best what he does."
"8513","8486 - Ahmad ibn Zuhayr narrated to us, Abu Shaybah ibn Abi Bakr narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ya'la narrated to us, Za'idah narrated to us, Abu Sinan al-Shaybani narrated to us, from Shaqiq ibn Salamah, who said: I was sitting with Hudhayfah when Abdullah ibn Mas'ud came. Hudhayfah said: The people most resembling the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in guidance and demeanor, and judgment, and address, from when he leaves his house until he returns - and I do not know what he does with his family - is Abdullah ibn Mas'ud. By Allah, the fortunate ones among the Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) know that Ibn Mas'ud is among the nearest of them in means."
"8514","8487 - Abu Khalifah al-Fadl ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi and Muhammad ibn Kathir al-Abdi narrated to us, both saying: Shu'bah narrated to us, (ha) and Uthman ibn Umar al-Dabbi and Muhammad ibn Hayyan al-Mazini narrated to us, both saying: Amr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Shu'bah informed us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, who said: We said to Hudhayfah: Inform us of a man close in bearing, guidance, and demeanor to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: I know no one closer in guidance, bearing, and demeanor to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), until he disappears behind the wall of his house, than Ibn Umm Abd. And he said: The fortunate ones among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) know that Ibn Umm Abd is the nearest of them to Allah in means on the Day of Resurrection."
"8515","8488 - Ahmad ibn Amr al-Qitrani al-Basri narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Tufayl narrated to us, Sharik narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, who said: We were with Hudhayfah opposite the house of Abdullah, and he said: I know no one more resembling the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in demeanor or bearing, from the master of this house, from when he leaves until he enters his dwelling; when he disappears, Allah knows best. The fortunate ones among the Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) know that he is among the nearest of them in means to Allah on the Day of Resurrection."
"8516","8489 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Yahya ibn Adam narrated to us, Isra'il narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from Hudhayfah, who said: Shall I not inform you of the people nearest in resemblance to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in guidance, demeanor, and bearing? We said: Yes. He said: Abdullah."
"8517","8490 - Ali ibn Sa'id al-Razi narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Dahir narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Abd al-Quddus narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Zayd ibn Wahb, who said: I heard Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman say: Whoever wishes to look at the people most resembling the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in guidance, bearing, and way, when he leaves his house until he returns, let him look at Abdullah ibn Mas'ud."
"8518","8491 - Aslam ibn Sahl al-Wasiti narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Aban al-Wasiti narrated to us, Yazid ibn Ata' narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from Zayd ibn Wahb, who said: I was sitting with Hudhayfah when Abdullah ibn Mas'ud and Abu Musa al-Ash'ari passed by. Hudhayfah said: I bear witness that the better of you two, and the more resembling of you two in guidance to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and in demeanor, is Abdullah ibn Mas'ud."
"8519","8492 - And Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Za'idah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Zayd ibn Wahb, who said: I was sitting with Hudhayfah and Abu Musa al-Ash'ari when one of them said to his companion: Did you hear the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say such-and-such hadith? He said: No. The other said to him: Did you hear it? He said: No. He said: The master of this house claims that he heard it. Abu Musa said: If he did so, he would enter when we were veiled, and witness when we were absent - Sulayman al-A'mash said: meaning Abdullah ibn Mas'ud."
"8520","8493 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Za'idah narrated to us, from Mughirah, who said: Ibrahim said: Abdullah came to Abu Musa and conversed with him. The prayer time came; when the iqamah was given, Abu Musa stepped back. Abdullah said to him: You know that it is of the Sunnah that the master of the house steps forward. Abu Musa refused until a freedman of one of them stepped forward."
"8521","8494 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Yahya ibn Adam narrated to us, Qutbah ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Malik ibn al-Harith, from Abu al-Ahwas, who said: We were in the house of Abu Musa with a group of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), looking into a mushaf. Abdullah stood up, and Abu Mas'ud said: I do not know that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left after him anyone more knowledgeable in what Allah Almighty revealed than this standing one. Abu Musa said: As for your saying that, he would witness when we were absent, and be admitted when we were veiled."
"8522","8495 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Za'idah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Malik ibn al-Harith, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Uqbah ibn Amr, who said: I see no man more knowledgeable in what was revealed to Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) than Abdullah. Abu Musa said: If that is said, he would hear when none was made to hear, and enter when none was made to enter."
"8523","8496 - Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Junayd narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Hisham narrated to us, Shayban narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Malik ibn al-Harith, from Abu al-Ahwas, who said: I came to Abu Musa al-Ash'ari, with him were Abdullah ibn Mas'ud and Abu Mas'ud al-Ansari, looking into a mushaf. We conversed a while, then Abdullah went out. Abu Mas'ud said: No, by Allah, I do not know that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left anyone more knowledgeable in the Book of Allah than this standing one."
"8524","8497 - al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Abu Kurayb narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Yusuf narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Aswad ibn Yazid, that he heard Abu Musa say: My brother and I came from Yemen and stayed a while seeing Abdullah ibn Mas'ud as of the household of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), because of his entering upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"8525","8498 - Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Jammal al-Asbahani narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Umar Rustah narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Aswad, from Abu Musa, who said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while I considered Ibn Mas'ud of the household."
"8526","8499 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, (ha) and Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, both from Sufyan al-Thawri, from Abu Husayn, from Abu Atiyyah al-Wadi'i, who said: A man came to Abu Musa and said: My wife's milk was retained in her breast, so I kept sucking it then spitting it out, and something of it entered my belly. He said: She has become unlawful to you. Then he came to Abdullah. Abu Atiyyah said: He stood and we stood with him. He said: Suckle the breast of this one, for suckling is only what grows flesh and blood. Abu Musa said: Do not ask me about anything while this scholar remains among you."
"8527","8500 - Umar ibn Hafs al-Sadusi narrated to us, Asim ibn Ali narrated to us, al-Mas'udi narrated to us, from Abu Husayn, from Abu Atiyyah, that Abu Musa was approached by a man who said: His wife's breast swelled, so he kept sucking it and spitting it out, and it entered his belly. He said: I do not see her as lawful for him. Then he came to Ibn Mas'ud and asked him about that. He said: She has not become unlawful to you; only that suckling makes unlawful which grows flesh and strengthens bone, and there is no suckling after weaning. It was said to Abu Musa, and he said: Do not ask us about anything while this scholar stands among you, of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"8528","8501 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Mis'ar narrated to us, from Zubayd, from Murrah, from Abdullah: Fear Allah as He should rightfully be feared - he said: That He be obeyed and not disobeyed, remembered and not forgotten, thanked and not denied."
"8529","8502 - Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Zubayd, from Murrah, from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, regarding His saying: Fear Allah as He should rightfully be feared - he said: That He be obeyed and not disobeyed, thanked and not denied, remembered and not forgotten."
"8530","8503 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from al-Thawri, from Zubayd, from Murrah, regarding His saying: And gives wealth in spite of love for it - he said: Ibn Mas'ud said: That you give it while you are healthy and miserly, hoping for life and fearing poverty."
"8531","8504 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Mis'ar narrated to us, from Asim, who said: Zirr said, from Abdullah: The major sins are what lies between the beginning of Surat al-Nisa' up to the thirtieth verse."
"8532","8505 - Umar ibn Hafs al-Sadusi narrated to us, Asim ibn Ali narrated to us, al-Mas'udi narrated to us, from Ali ibn Budhaymah, from Qays ibn Habtar, who said: Abdullah said: How beloved are the two disliked things: death and poverty. By Allah, as for wealth and poverty, I care not with which of them I am tried: if it is wealth, in it there is indeed kindness to others; and if it is poverty, in it there is indeed patience."
"8533","8506 - Umar ibn Hafs al-Sadusi narrated to us, Asim ibn Ali narrated to us, al-Mas'udi narrated to us, from Jami' ibn Shaddad, from Tamim ibn Salamah, from Abu Ma'mar, who said: When we heard something from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud that we disliked, we would remain silent until he explained it to us. One day Abdullah said to us: Sickness - no reward is recorded for its bearer. That grieved us and weighed heavily on us. He said: But Allah Almighty expiates sins through it."
"8534","8507 - Umar ibn Hafs al-Sadusi narrated to us, Asim ibn Ali narrated to us, al-Mas'udi narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Abu Wa'il, who said: Abdullah said: Shall I not warn you of excess speech? It suffices one of you to convey his need."
"8535","8508 - Umar ibn Hafs narrated to us, Asim ibn Ali narrated to us, al-Mas'udi narrated to us, from Jami' ibn Shaddad, from al-Qasim, from Abdullah, who said: That I remember Allah one day until night is dearer to me than that I ride choice horses one day until night."
"8536","8509 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Abu Sadiq, who said: Abdullah said: I know households that the Dajjal will frighten. They said: Who, O Abu Abd al-Rahman? He said: The houses of the people of Kufah."
"8537","8510 - Umar ibn Hafs al-Sadusi narrated to us, Asim ibn Ali narrated to us, al-Mas'udi narrated to us, from Ash'ath ibn Abi al-Sha'tha', from his father, who said: The Dajjal was mentioned in the presence of Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, and he said: Do not mention him much, for when the matter is decreed in heaven, the swiftest way for it to descend to earth is its appearing on people's tongues. He said: And how will it be with you when the people are safe and you are afraid? And how will it be with you when the people are in shade and you are in the sun?"
"8538","8511 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Khaythamah, who said: They mentioned the Dajjal in the presence of Abdullah, and some of them said: If he came out we would pelt him with stones. Abdullah said: If he were to appear at Babil in the morning, some of them in the morning would complain to him of sore feet from hurrying."
"8539","8512 - Umar ibn Hafs al-Sadusi narrated to us, Asim ibn Ali narrated to us, al-Mas'udi narrated to us, from Asim, from Abu Wa'il, from Abdullah, who said: Whoever shows off, Allah will expose him; whoever seeks repute, Allah will make him heard of; whoever stretches tall in arrogance, Allah will lower him; whoever humbles himself in awe, Allah will raise him. People are of kinds: one given ease in this world and stinted in the Hereafter; one stinted in this world and given ease in the Hereafter; one stinted in this world and the Hereafter; one given ease in this world and the Hereafter; and one at rest and one from whom rest is taken. We said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, who is the one at rest and the one from whom rest is taken? He said: As for the one at rest, it is the believer - when he dies he rests. As for the one from whom rest is taken, it is he who wrongs people and backbites them."
"8540","8513 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, al-Qasim ibn Ma'n narrated to us, from Mansur, from Muslim, from Masruq, who said: I surveyed the Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and found their knowledge ending with six: Umar, Ali, Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, Abu al-Darda', and Zayd ibn Thabit - then I surveyed the six and found their knowledge ending with Ali and Abdullah."
"8541","8514 - Bakr ibn Sahl al-Dimyati narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Salih narrated to me, from Rabi'ah ibn Yazid, from Abu Idris al-Khawlani, from Yazid ibn Umayrah, that when death approached Mu'adh ibn Jabal, we said to him: Advise us. He said: Seat me. Then he said: Knowledge and faith - their place: whoever seeks them finds them - he said that three times - and seek knowledge with four: Uwaymir Abu al-Darda', Salman al-Farisi, Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, and Abdullah ibn Salam."
"8542","8515 - Musa ibn Harun and Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, both saying: Yahya al-Himmani narrated to us, Yahya ibn Salamah ibn Kuhayl narrated to us, from his father, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, who said: None remained with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Uhud except four, one of them Abdullah ibn Mas'ud."
"8543","A chapter. 8516 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Jarir narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Umm Musa, who said: I heard Ali say: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered Ibn Mas'ud to climb a tree and bring him something from it. His companions looked at the thinness of his shanks and laughed. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What makes you laugh? The leg of Abdullah in the Balance will surely be heavier than Uhud."
"8544","8517 - Mahmud ibn Muhammad al-Wasiti narrated to us, Tamim ibn al-Muntasir narrated to us, Ishaq al-Azraq narrated to us, from Sharik, from Jabir, from Abu al-Duha, from al-Azhar ibn al-Aswad, from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, who said: I climbed an arak tree to gather arak sticks from it; my companions began marveling at my lightness. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What do you marvel at? By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, he will surely be heavier in the Balance on the Day of Resurrection than Uhud."
"8545","The sermon of Ibn Mas'ud and from his speech. 8518 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Ma'mar informed us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Ibn Mas'ud, who said: They are but two: guidance and speech. The finest speech is the speech of Allah, and the finest guidance is the guidance of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Beware of newly-invented matters and innovations, for the worst of matters are their newly-invented ones, and every newly-invented matter is misguidance. Let not the span of time grow long upon you lest your hearts harden. Hope: what is coming is near. The distant is what is not coming. The wretched is he who is wretched in his mother's womb, and the fortunate is he who takes heed from others. The worst of narrations are the narrations of lies. Lying is not proper, neither seriously nor in jest. Lying leads to wickedness, wickedness leads to the Fire, truthfulness leads to righteousness, and righteousness leads to Paradise. It is said to the truthful one: He told the truth and was righteous; and it is said to the liar: He lied and acted wickedly. And I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The servant keeps lying until he is recorded as a liar, and keeps telling the truth until he is recorded as truthful - then he said - Beware of al-adhah. Do you know what al-adhah is? Tale-carrying and conveying reports."
"8546","8519 - Ahmad ibn Ishaq ibn Wadih al-Assal al-Basri narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ja'far informed us, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah, the like."
"8547","8520 - al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Jarir narrated to us, from Idris al-Awdi, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, raising the hadith to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he used to address on Thursdays, standing, saying: O people, they are but two: guidance and speech - then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq."
"8548","8521 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Za'idah narrated to us, from Ibrahim al-Hajari, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah, who said: They are but two: guidance and speech. The truest speech is the speech of Allah, the finest guidance is the guidance of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the worst of matters are their newly-invented ones, every newly-invented matter is an innovation, every innovation is misguidance, and every misguidance is in the Fire."
"8549","8522 - Mahmud ibn Muhammad al-Wasiti narrated to us, Wahb ibn Baqiyyah narrated to us, Khalid informed us, from Isma'il ibn Hammad ibn Abi Sulayman, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Ubaydah, from Ibn Mas'ud, that he would come every Thursday, standing, never sitting, saying: Do not put people to trial, for among them are the weak, the elderly, and the needy. He would say: They are two: the finest guidance is the guidance of Muhammad, the truest speech is the Book of Allah, the worst of matters are their newly-invented ones, and every newly-invented matter is misguidance. The wretched is he who is wretched in his mother's womb, and the fortunate is he who takes heed from others. Let not the span of time grow long upon you, nor let hope distract you, for all that is coming is near, and only distant is what is not coming. Among the worst of people is the idler by day, the corpse by night. Killing a believer is disbelief, reviling him is transgression, and it is not lawful for a Muslim to forsake his brother for more than three days. The worst of narrations is lying: no lying is proper, neither seriously nor in jest, nor that a man promise his child and then not fulfill it. Lying leads to wickedness, wickedness leads to the Fire, truthfulness leads to righteousness, righteousness leads to Paradise. The truthful one is told: He told the truth and was righteous; the liar is told: He lied and acted wickedly. And I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The servant keeps telling the truth and is recorded with Allah as truthful, and he keeps lying until he is recorded with Allah as a liar. Do you know what al-adhah is? It is tale-carrying that corrupts between people."
"8550","8523 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ma'mar, from Ja'far Barqan, who said: Abdullah ibn Mas'ud said: All that is coming is near. The distant is what is not coming. Allah does not hasten for anyone's haste, nor act lightly for people's affairs. What Allah wills, not what people will. Allah wills a matter and people will a matter; what Allah wills comes to be even if people dislike it. None draws near what Allah has set far, none sets far what Allah has drawn near, and nothing comes to be except by Allah's permission. The truest speech is the Book of Allah, the finest guidance is the guidance of Muhammad, the worst of matters are their newly-invented ones, every newly-invented matter is an innovation, and every innovation is misguidance. Ma'mar said: And others besides Ja'far ibn Barqan said, from Ibn Mas'ud: The best of what is cast into the heart is certainty, the best wealth is wealth of the soul, the best knowledge is what benefits, the best guidance is what is followed, and what is little and suffices is better than what is much and distracts. One of you will only end up in a place of four cubits, so do not bore people or weary them. Every soul has a devil, and an eagerness and turning; it also has a weariness and turning away. The worst of narrations is lying. Lying leads to wickedness, wickedness leads to the Fire. Uphold truthfulness: truthfulness leads to righteousness, righteousness leads to Paradise. Consider that: they are two companions that meet. It is said to the truthful one: He told the truth, until he is recorded as truthful. The liar keeps lying until he is recorded as a liar. Lying is not proper, neither seriously nor in jest, nor that a man of you promise his child then not fulfill it to him. Do not ask the People of the Book about anything, for the span of time has grown long upon them, their hearts have hardened, and they have innovated in their religion. If you must ask them, take what agrees with your Book, leave what contradicts it, and remain silent."
"8551","8524 - Yusuf al-Qadi narrated to us, Amr ibn Marzuq informed us, (ha) and Yusuf al-Qadi narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Harb informed us, Shu'bah informed us, from Amr ibn Murrah, he heard Murrah narrating from Abdullah, who said: The truest speech is the Book of Allah, the finest guidance is the guidance of Muhammad, the worst of matters are their newly-invented ones. What you are promised will surely come, and you cannot escape. Distant is what is not coming. Uphold truthfulness, for it leads to Paradise. A man keeps telling the truth until he is recorded with Allah as truthful, and righteousness becomes firm in his heart, with no place for wickedness in his heart, not even a needle's point to settle in. Beware of lying, for it leads to the Fire. A man keeps lying until he is recorded with Allah as a liar, and wickedness settles in his heart, leaving no needle's point in his heart to settle in."
"8552","8525 - Muhammad ibn Ali al-Sa'igh al-Makki narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Mujahid, from Abu Ma'mar, from Ibn Mas'ud, who said: No lying is proper, neither in jest nor seriously, nor that one of you promise his child something then not fulfill it to him."
"8553","8526 - Muhammad ibn Ali al-Sa'igh narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, who said: Abdullah said: No lying is proper, neither seriously nor in jest."
"8554","8527 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Ansari narrated to us, (ha) and Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, both saying: Ibn Awn narrated to us, saying: We came to Abu Wa'il, with him a slave girl called Buraydah. We said: O Buraydah, tell Abu Wa'il to narrate to us. She said: Abu Wa'il, narrate to the people what you heard from Abdullah. He said: I heard Abdullah say: O people, you will be gathered on a single plain; the caller will make you hear, and sight will penetrate you. The wretched is he who is wretched in his mother's womb - he said: and I think he follows it with - and the fortunate is he who takes heed from others."
"8555","8528 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Kulthum ibn Jabr narrated to us, saying: I heard Abu al-Tufayl, saying: I heard Abdullah ibn Mas'ud say: The wretched is he who is wretched in his mother's womb, and the fortunate is he who takes heed from others."
"8556","8556 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, Rabiah ibn Kulthum ibn Jabr narrated to us, my father Kulthum ibn Jabr narrated to me, he said: I heard Abu al-Tufayl say: I heard Abd Allah ibn Masud say: The wretched is he who is wretched in his mother's womb, and the fortunate is he who takes admonition from others."
"8557","8557 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, Rabiah ibn Kulthum ibn Jabr narrated to us, my father Kulthum ibn Jabr narrated to me, from Abu al-Tufayl Amr ibn Wathilah, he said: Ibn Masud, when he addressed us in Kufa, used to say: The wretched is he who is wretched in his mother's womb, and the fortunate is he who is fortunate in his mother's womb."
"8558","8558 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Abu Hamzah, from Rabah al-Nakhai, he said: Abd Allah ibn Masud used to address us every Thursday and say: The truest speech is the Book of Allah, the best guidance is the guidance of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), every invented matter is an innovation, every innovation is misguidance, and the worst of matters are their invented ones; you will be gathered on a single plain, sight will encompass you and the caller will make you hear; verily the wretched is he who is wretched in his mother's womb, and the fortunate is he who takes admonition from others."
"8559","8559 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Arim Abu al-Numan narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Murrah, from Abd Allah, he said: O people, hold to truthfulness, for it leads to righteousness and righteousness leads to Paradise; beware of lying, for it leads to wickedness and wickedness leads to Hell; it is said of the truthful one: he spoke truth and did righteousness, and of the liar: he lied and acted wickedly; verily the angel has a touch and Satan has a touch: the angel's touch is a promise of good, Satan's touch a promise of evil; whoever finds the angel's touch let him praise Allah, whoever finds Satan's touch let him seek refuge from it, for Allah says: Satan promises you poverty and commands you - to the end of the verse; he said: Verily Allah laughs at two men: a man who rises on a cold night from his bed, blanket and covering, performs ablution then stands for prayer, so Allah says to His angels: What carried My servant to do this? They say: Our Lord, hope for what is with You and fear of what is with You; He says: I have given him what he hoped and secured him from what he feared; and a man in a company who knows what flight entails and what is with Allah, so he fights until killed, and He says to the angels: What carried My servant to do this? They say: Our Lord, hope for what is with You and fear of what is with You; He says: I call you to witness that I have given him what he hoped and secured him from what he feared - or a phrase similar to it."
"8560","8560 - Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Qurrah ibn Khalid narrated to us, from al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim, he said: Abd Allah ibn Masud said: There is none of you but a guest and a loan; the guest departs, and the loan is returned to its owners."
"8561","8561 - Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Qurrah ibn Khalid narrated to us, Awn narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah said: Knowledge is not from abundance of hadith, but knowledge is from fear of Allah."
"8562","8562 - Muhammad ibn Ali al-Saigh narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, al-Sari ibn Yahya narrated to us, from al-Hasan, he said: Abd Allah ibn Masud said: If I stood between Paradise and Hell and were told: choose - we give you choice which of the two you wish to be in, or to be ashes - I would love to be ashes."
"8563","8563 - Muhammad ibn Ali al-Saigh narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, he said: Ibn Masud advised his son Abu Ubaydah with three phrases: O my son, I advise you to fear Allah, let your house contain you, weep over your sin, and withhold your tongue."
"8564","8564 - Muhammad ibn Ali al-Saigh narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Misar, from Man ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: A man said to Abd Allah: Advise me with comprehensive beneficial words; Abd Allah said to him: Worship Allah and associate nothing with Him, stay with the Quran wherever it turns, whoever brings you truth accept it from him even if distant, whoever brings you falsehood reject it even if a beloved close one."
"8565","8565 - Muhammad ibn Ali al-Saigh narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Abu Awanah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Yahya ibn Waththab, he said: Ibn Masud said: I dislike seeing a man idle, neither in work of this world nor the Hereafter."
"8566","8566 - Muhammad ibn Ali al-Saigh narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, al-Amash narrated to us, from al-Musayyab ibn Rafi, from one who informed him, from Ibn Masud, he said: I detest seeing a man idle, neither in work of this world nor the Hereafter."
"8567","8567 - Muhammad ibn Ali al-Saigh narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Shaqiq, he said: Abd Allah said: Whoever emigrates seeking something, it is his; he said: A man emigrated to marry a woman called Umm Qays, and he was called the emigrant of Umm Qays."
"8568","8568 - Muhammad ibn al-Saigh narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Hudayj ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd Allah, from Abd Allah ibn Masud, he said: No man says: I seek Allah's forgiveness, besides whom there is no god, the Living, the Sustainer, and I repent to Him, three times, except he is forgiven even if he fled from battle."
"8569","8569 - Muhammad ibn Ali al-Saigh narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Misar, from Awn ibn Abd Allah, from Ibn Masud, he said: A mountain calls to a mountain by name: O so-and-so, did anyone pass by you today remembering Allah? If it says yes, it rejoices; Awn said: They listen to evil and do not listen to good; they are more hearing for good; and he recited: And they say: The Most Merciful has taken a son; you have brought a terrible thing; the heavens almost rupture from it, the earth splits, and the mountains collapse in devastation, that they invoke a son for the Most Merciful; it is not befitting the Most Merciful to take a son."
"8570","8570 - Muhammad ibn Ali al-Saigh narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Malik ibn al-Harith, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, he said: Abd Allah said: Whoever is not commanded to good by his prayer nor forbidden from evil by it only increases in distance from Allah."
"8571","8571 - Muhammad ibn Ali al-Saigh narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, al-Amash narrated to us, from Abu Zibyan, from Alqamah, he said: Abd Allah said: Patience is half of faith, and certainty is faith."
"8572","8572 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Abu Asim narrated to us, from Abd al-Jabbar, al-Thaqafi narrated to us, from Zarah, from Bilad ibn Ismah, he said: While I was with Abd Allah, I saw a crowd that surged then returned; he said: Beware the crush of the market, for it is Satan's crush."
"8573","8573 - Muhammad ibn Ali al-Saigh narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Salih ibn Hubab, from Husayn ibn Uqbah, he said: Abd Allah said: Paradise is surrounded by hardships and Hell is surrounded by desires; whoever lifts the veil falls into what is beyond it."
"8574","8574 - Muhammad ibn Ali al-Saigh narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Salih ibn Hubab, from Husayn ibn Uqbah, he said: Abd Allah said: The people with most sins on the Day of Resurrection are those who indulge most in falsehood."
"8575","8575 - Muhammad ibn Ali al-Saigh narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq informed us, from Muharib ibn Dithar, from his uncle, he said: I used to pass by the house of Abd Allah ibn Masud at dawn and hear him say: O Allah, You called me and I answered, You commanded me and I obeyed, and this is dawn so forgive me; I met him and said: words I heard you say at dawn, and informed him of them; he said: Yaqub delayed his sons until dawn."
"8576","8576 - Muhammad ibn Ali al-Saigh al-Makki narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Sharik narrated to us, from Hilal al-Warraq, from Abd Allah ibn Ukaym, that Ibn Masud used to supplicate: O Allah, increase me in faith, certainty, understanding - or he said - and knowledge."
"8577","8577 - Muhammad ibn Ali al-Saigh narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Khalaf ibn Khalifah narrated to us, Abu Yazid narrated to us, from al-Shabi, he said: Ibn Masud said: Beware of what-do-you-think questions, for those before you perished only because of such; do not analogize one thing to another lest a foot slip after being firm; when one of you is asked about what he does not know let him say: I do not know, for it is a third of knowledge."
"8578","8578 - Muhammad ibn Ali al-Saigh narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Mujalid, from al-Shabi, from Masruq, he said: Abd Allah said: No year but the one after it is worse than it, no year is better than a year, no nation better than a nation; rather your best and your scholars pass away, and a people arise who judge matters by their opinion, so Islam collapses and is breached."
"8579","8579 - Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Aswad, from Abd Allah, he said: The righteous pass away predecessors, and remain the people of doubt who know no good nor deny evil."
"8580","8580 - Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated to us, from Said ibn Abi Ayyub, Abd Allah ibn al-Walid narrated to us, he said: I heard Abd al-Rahman ibn Hujayrah narrate, from his father, from Abd Allah ibn Masud, that when he sat he used to say: You are in the passage of night, with shortened lifespans and preserved deeds, death comes suddenly; whoever sows good will soon harvest desire, whoever sows evil will soon harvest regret; for each there is a sower; the slow is not preceded in his share, nor does the greedy attain what was not decreed for him; whoever is given good, Allah gave him, whoever is shielded from evil, Allah shielded him; the God-fearing are masters, the jurists leaders, and sitting with them is increase."
"8581","8581 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from al-Thawri, and Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abd Allah, he said: Do not contend with this night, for you cannot bear it; when one of you dozes let him go to his bed, for it is safer for him."
"8582","8582 - Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Sawti narrated to us, Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abd Allah, he said: Yusuf and his mother were given two-thirds of beauty."
"8583","8583 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abd Allah, he said: Yusuf and his mother were given two-thirds of beauty."
"8584","8584 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zuhayr narrated to us, Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abd Allah, he said: Yusuf and his mother were given two-thirds of people's beauty, in face, fairness and other than that; when a woman came to him he would cover his face fearing she would be tempted."
"8585","8585 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abd Allah, he said: Enough lying for a believer is to narrate everything he hears."
"8586","8586 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abd Allah, he said: When you invite a man you have given him permission."
"8587","8587 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abd Allah, he said: The most painful thing in the believer is obscenity."
"8588","8588 - Muhammad ibn Hayyan al-Mazini narrated to us, Amr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Shubah informed us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abd Allah, he said: The meanest of the believer's character traits is obscenity."
"8589","8589 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Qays ibn Muslim, from Tariq ibn Shihab, he said: Abd Allah said: A man goes out with his religion with him, then returns with none of it with him; he comes to a man who owns for him nor for himself neither benefit nor harm, then swears to him by Allah that you lied and sinned, then returns having obtained nothing of his need, while having angered Allah against him."
"8590","8590 - Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Abu al-Walid narrated to us, and Muhammad ibn Abdus narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Jad narrated to us, both said: Shubah narrated to us, from Qays ibn Muslim, from Tariq ibn Shihab, from Abd Allah, similar to it."
"8591","8591 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Qays ibn Muslim, from Tariq ibn Shihab, he said: Atris ibn Urqub al-Shaybani came to Abd Allah and said: Perished is he who does not command good and forbid evil; he said: Rather perished is he whose heart does not know good nor whose heart denies evil."
"8592","8592 - Muhammad ibn Abdus ibn Kamil narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Jad narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Qays ibn Muslim, from Tariq ibn Shihab, from Abd Allah, the like of it."
"8593","8593 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Qays al-Awdi, from Huzayl ibn Shurahbil, he said: Abd Allah said: Whoever desires the Hereafter harms the worldly life, whoever desires the worldly harms the Hereafter; and he ordered them to harm the perishable for the lasting; and he said: You are in a time whose scholars are many and speakers few, whose givers are many and beggars few, so lengthen prayer and shorten the sermon; verily some eloquence is magic; after you will be a time whose speakers are many and scholars few, whose beggars are many and givers few."
"8594","8594 - Muhammad ibn Habban al-Mazini narrated to us, Amr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Shubah informed us, from Abu Ishaq, he said: I heard Abu al-Ahwas and Abu al-Kindi, from Abd Allah, he said: You are in a time when prayer is long and the sermon short, its scholars many and speakers few; there will come upon you a time when prayer is short and the sermon long, its speakers many and scholars few, they delay prayer, the Isha prayer, until the east of the dead; whoever of you reaches that, let him pray the prayer at its time and make it with them voluntary; you are in a time when a man is envied for much wealth and many dependents, and there will come upon you a time when a man is envied for few dependents and light load; there is none I leave behind among my family more beloved to me in death than they, nor a household of dung beetles, and I love them as you love your families."
"8595","8595 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays, he said: Abd Allah saw some playing boys of his children before him and said: These are more insignificant to me than their number of dung beetles."
"8596","8596 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud, from Wail ibn Rabiah, from Abd Allah, he said: False testimony is equated with associating partners with Allah, and he recited: And avoid false speech."
"8597","8597 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn al-Saib, from Abd Allah ibn Qatadah al-Muharibi, from Abd Allah, he said: Charity falls in Allah's Hand before it falls in the beggar's hand; then Abd Allah recited: And He is the One who accepts repentance from His servants - the verse."
"8598","8598 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, and Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, both from Sufyan al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Abd Allah al-Taymi, from Abu Majid al-Hanafi, he said: A man brought his brother's son to Abd Allah drunk and said: I found him drunk; Abd Allah said: Shake him, move him, and smell him; so he was shaken, moved, and smelled, and the smell of drink was found from him; Abd Allah ordered him to prison, then brought him out the next day, then ordered a whip whose tip was beaten until it softened into a lash for him, then said to the executioner: Strike and lift your hand, and give every limb its right; so he struck him a non-severe beating, and put him in a cloak and trousers - or shirt and trousers; then he said: Evil, by Allah, is the guardian of Taym; you neither disciplined well nor covered the disgrace. He said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, he is my brother's son, I feel for him the anguish I feel for my child. Abd Allah said: Allah loves pardon, and no governor brought an ordained punishment should fail to establish it; then he began to narrate from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The first man of the Muslims to have his hand cut among the Ansar - or in the Ansar - it was said: O Messenger of Allah, he stole, and it was as if ashes were strewn on the face of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); some said: O Messenger of Allah, did it grieve you? He said: What prevents me, while you are helpers of Satan against your companion; he said: Allah is Pardoning, loves pardon, and no governor brought an ordained punishment should fail to establish it; then he recited: Let them pardon and overlook; do you not love that Allah forgive you, and Allah is Forgiving, Merciful; and the wording is Abu Nuaym's."
"8599","8599 - Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Jarir narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Hassan Abu al-Ashras, from Abu Ubaydah, from Abd Allah, he said: Abd Allah said: When one of you incurs an ordained punishment, do not supplicate against him aiding Satan against him, but ask Allah to turn to him and have mercy on him."
"8600","8600 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Ubaydah, from Ibn Masud, he said: When you see your brother commit a sin, do not be helpers of Satan against him saying: O Allah disgrace him, O Allah curse him, but ask Allah for well-being; for we, the companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), used to say nothing about anyone until we knew upon what he dies; if he is sealed with good we knew he attained good, if sealed with evil we feared for him his deed."
"8601","8601 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Amr ibn Salamah, from his father, from Abd Allah, he said: To enjoy a whip in Allah's path is more beloved to me than performing Hajj after Hajj."
"8602","8602 - Yusuf al-Qadi narrated to us, Amr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, and Muhammad ibn Abdus ibn Kamil narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Jad narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Farwah al-Hamdani, he said: I heard Abu Amr al-Shaybani say: Abd Allah had permitted currency exchange, and a man marrying a woman who died before consummation then marrying her mother; then he came to Madinah and as if he met Umar and returned; he came to the money changers and forbade them from that."
"8603","8603 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Umar ibn Aban narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Sad ibn Iyas al-Bajali, he said: Abd Allah used to permit a dirham for two dirhams and a dinar for two dinars; then he returned to Madinah and came to Umar and Ali and the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and they forbade him from that; when he returned I saw him circling among the money changers saying: Woe to you, O people, do not consume usury, do not buy a dirham for two dirhams nor a dinar for two dinars."
"8604","8604 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Abd al-Salam ibn Harb narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, that Abd Allah ibn Masud used to give fatwa permitting currency exchange, until he came to Umar and asked him and he disliked it, so Abd Allah retracted his opinion."
"8605","8605 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from al-Thawri, from Abu Farwah, from Abu Amr al-Shaybani, from Ibn Masud, that a man of Banu Shamkh ibn Fazarah married a woman, then saw her mother and she pleased him, so he sought Ibn Masud's fatwa and he ordered him to separate from her then marry her mother; he married her and she bore children; then Ibn Masud came to Madinah and asked about that and was informed she is not lawful for him; when he returned to Kufa he said to the man: She is forbidden to you, so separate from her."
"8606","8606 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Abi Muawiyah narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ali ibn al-Aqmar, from Abu Atiyah, he said: Abd Allah said: When you meet the wicked, meet him with a frowning face."
"8607","8607 - Mahmud ibn Muhammad al-Wasiti narrated to us, Zakariya ibn Yahya Zahmawayh narrated to us, Sharik narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn al-Muntashir, from his father, and Masruq, from Abd Allah, he said: When you see the wicked and cannot change him, then frown in his face."
"8608","8608 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Asim, from Zirr, from Abd Allah, he said: Allah looked into the hearts of servants and found the heart of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the best of servants' hearts, so He chose him for Himself and singled him out - or he said: sent him with His message - then He looked into servants' hearts after his heart and found the hearts of his companions the best of servants' hearts, so He made them ministers of His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), fighting for His religion."
"8609","8609 - Umar ibn Hafs al-Sadusi narrated to us, Asim ibn Ali narrated to us, al-Masudi narrated to us, from Asim, from Abu Wail, from Abd Allah ibn Masud, he said: Allah looked into the hearts of servants and chose Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), sending him with His message and selecting him with His knowledge; then He looked into people's hearts and chose his companions, making them ministers of His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and supporters of His religion; whatever the believers see as good is good with Allah, whatever the believers see as ugly is ugly with Allah."
"8610","8610 - Umar ibn Hafs al-Sadusi narrated to us, Asim ibn Ali narrated to us, al-Masudi narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Wail, he said: Abd Allah heard a man say: Where are the ascetics in this world and desirers of the Hereafter? Abd Allah said: Those are the companions of al-Jabiyah; five hundred of the Muslims stipulated not to return until killed; they shaved their heads, met the enemy, and were killed except one informing of them."
"8611","8611 - Muhammad ibn Abdus ibn Kamil narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Jad narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Qays al-Awdi, from Huzayl ibn Shurahbil, from Abd Allah ibn Masud, he said: The Hour will not come except upon the worst of people, who know no good nor deny evil, copulating as beasts copulate on the road; a woman passes a man on the road, he rises to her and fulfills his need from her, then returns to his companions laughing to them and they laughing to him, like the worthless pressed dates that are not eaten."
"8612","8612 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, al-Masudi narrated to us, from Abu Qays, from Huzayl ibn Shurahbil, he said: Abd Allah said: The Hour will come upon the worst of people, upon folk who command no good nor forbid evil, copulating as donkeys copulate; a man takes a woman's hand, isolates with her, fulfills his need from her, then returns laughing to them and they laughing to him, like the worthless pressed dates that are not eaten."
"8613","8613 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Sad ibn Wahb, from Abd Allah, he said: Among the greatest of sins is for a man to say to his brother: Fear Allah, and he says: Mind yourself, do you command me?"
"8614","8614 - Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, from Shubah, from Abu Ishaq, from Zayd ibn Wahb, from Abd Allah, he said: Enough sin for a man is that when it is said to him: Fear Allah, he becomes angry."
"8615","8615 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Said ibn Wahb, from Abd Allah, he said: People remain in good as long as knowledge comes to them from the companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and from their elders; when knowledge comes from their young ones, that is when they perish."
"8616","8616 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Mamar informed us, from Abu Ishaq, from Said ibn Wahb, he said: I heard Ibn Masud say: People remain righteous and holding together as long as knowledge comes to them from the companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and from their elders; when it comes to them from their young ones they perish."
"8617","8617 - Abu Khalifah al-Fadl ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, from Shubah, from Abu Ishaq, from Zayd ibn Wahb, from Abd Allah, he said: People will remain in good as long as knowledge comes to them from their elders and those of their predecessors; when it comes to them from their young ones they perish; thus Shubah narrated it from Abu Ishaq from Zayd ibn Wahb, and Zayd ibn Hibban followed him."
"8618","8618 - Muhammad ibn al-Sari ibn Mihran al-Naqid narrated to us, al-Hakam ibn Musa narrated to us, Mamar ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Hibban, from Abu Ishaq, from Zayd ibn Wahb, from Ibn Masud, he said: People remain in good as long as knowledge comes to them from the companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and their elders; when it comes to them from their young ones they perish."
"8619","8619 - al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati narrated to us, Ubayd ibn Yaish narrated to us, Ali ibn Qadim narrated to us, from Abd al-Salam ibn Harb, from al-Amash, from Abu Wail, from Abd Allah, he said: Allah looked into the hearts of servants and found the heart of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the best of servants' hearts; then he mentioned similar to Asim's hadith."
"8620","8620 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, and Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Raja narrated to us, and Umar ibn Hafs al-Sadusi narrated to us, Asim ibn Ali narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, al-Masudi narrated to us - Ibn Raja said: al-Masudi informed us - from Awn ibn Abd Allah, from Abu Fakhitah, from al-Aswad ibn Yazid, he said: Abd Allah said: When you send blessings upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), beautify the blessing upon him, for you do not know, perhaps that is presented to him; he said: So teach us; he said: Say: O Allah, place Your blessings, mercy and benedictions upon the master of messengers, leader of the God-fearing, seal of prophets, Muhammad Your servant and messenger, leader of good, guide to good, messenger of mercy; O Allah, raise him to a praised station envied by the first and the last; O Allah, bless Muhammad and the family of Muhammad as You blessed Ibrahim and the family of Ibrahim, You are Praiseworthy, Glorious."
"8621","8621 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from al-Thawri, from Abu Salamah, from Awn ibn Abd Allah, from a man, from al-Aswad ibn Yazid, from Ibn Masud, that he used to say: O Allah, place Your blessings and mercy upon the master of messengers, leader of the God-fearing, seal of prophets, Muhammad Your servant and messenger, leader of good, guide to good, messenger of mercy; O Allah, raise him to a praised station envied by the first and the last; O Allah, bless Muhammad and the family of Muhammad as You blessed the family of Ibrahim, You are Praiseworthy; O Allah, bless Muhammad and the family of Muhammad as You blessed the family of Ibrahim, You are Praiseworthy, Glorious; and this Abu Salamah from whom al-Thawri narrated this hadith is Misar ibn Kidam."
"8622","8622 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from al-Thawri, from Ibrahim al-Hajari, from Abu al-Ahwas, he said: Abd Allah ibn Masud said: Whoever is pleased to meet Allah tomorrow as a Muslim, let him preserve these prescribed prayers where they are called; for they are from the ways of guidance, and Allah legislated for your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the ways of guidance; by my life, I do not think any of you but has taken a mosque from his house; if you prayed in your houses as this absentee prays in his house you would abandon the way of your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and if you abandoned the way of your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) you would go astray; we saw none absented from it except a known hypocrite, or one known for hypocrisy; I saw a man supported between two men until stood in the row; no man purifies himself excellently then takes a step intending a mosque except Allah writes for him a good deed, raises him a degree, and removes from him a sin, so much that we used to shorten our steps."
"8623","8623 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, Ibrahim al-Hajari informed me, and Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Amr ibn Hakam narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Ibrahim al-Hajari, from Abu al-Ahwas, he said: Abd Allah said: Whoever is pleased to meet Allah as a Muslim servant, let him preserve these five prayers where they are called; for they are from the ways of guidance, and Allah legislated for your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the ways of guidance; by my life, if all of you prayed in your houses as the one in his house prays you would abandon the way of your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and if you abandoned the way of your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) you would go astray; we saw none absented from it except a hypocrite of known hypocrisy; we saw a man supported between two men until brought into the row; no Muslim servant performs ablution excellently intending a congregational mosque and prays in it, taking a step, except Allah raises him a degree or removes from him a sin, so much that we used to shorten steps; and the wording is Hajjaj's hadith."
"8624","8624 - Ishaq ibn Dawud al-Sawwaf al-Tustari narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ghaylan narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Bazigh narrated to us, from Rawh ibn al-Qasim, from Ibrahim al-Hajari, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abd Allah ibn Masud, he said: Whoever is pleased to meet Allah tomorrow as a Muslim, let him preserve these prayers where they are called in congregation; for they are from the ways of guidance, and Allah imposed for your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the ways of guidance; if you prayed in your houses as this absentee prays in his house you would abandon the way of your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and if you abandoned the way of your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) you would go astray; I saw a man supported between two men until brought and stood in the row; a time came upon us when none absented from it except a hypocrite of known hypocrisy, and we used to shorten steps."
"8625","8625 - Abu Yazid al-Qaratis i narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Yazid ibn Ata narrated to us, from Ibrahim al-Hajari, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abd Allah, he said: Whoever is pleased to meet Allah tomorrow as a Muslim, let him preserve the prescribed prayers where they are called; for Allah legislated for your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the ways of guidance, and they are from the ways of guidance; if you prayed in your houses as this absentee prays in his house you would abandon the way of your Prophet, and if you abandoned the way of your Prophet you would go astray; a time came upon us when none absented from them except a hypocrite of evident hypocrisy; I saw a man supported between two men until he stands in the row."
"8626","8626 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from Ibrahim al-Hajari, he said: I heard Abu al-Ahwas narrate from Abd Allah, he said: Whoever is pleased to meet Allah tomorrow as a Muslim, let him preserve these prescribed prayers where they are called; for Allah legislated for your Prophet the ways of guidance, and they are from the ways of guidance; by my life, if you prayed in your houses as this absentee prays in his house you would abandon the way of your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and if you abandoned the way of your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) you would go astray; a time came upon us when none absented from them except a hypocrite of known hypocrisy; I saw a man supported between two men until stood in the row; no man purifies himself excellently then intends a mosque to pray in it, taking a step, except Allah writes for him a good deed, raises him a degree, and removes from him a sin, so much that we used to shorten steps."
"8627","8627 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani narrated to us, my father narrated to us, and Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, both said: Zuhayr narrated to us, Ibrahim al-Hajari narrated to us, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abd Allah, he said: Whoever is pleased to meet Allah tomorrow as a Muslim, let him preserve these prescribed prayers where they are called; for Allah legislated for your Prophet the ways of guidance, and they are from the ways of guidance; by my life, if you prayed in your houses as this absentee prays in his house you would abandon the way of your Prophet, and if you abandoned the way of your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) you would go astray; we saw none absented from it except a hypocrite of known hypocrisy."
"8628","8628 - Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Mamar ibn Sahl narrated to us, Amir ibn Mudrik narrated to us, Khallad al-Saffar narrated to us, from al-Hajari, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abd Allah, the like of it."
"8629","8629 - Fudayl ibn Muhammad al-Malti narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Abu al-Umays narrated to us, he said: I heard Ali ibn al-Aqmar mention from Abu al-Ahwas, he said: Abd Allah said: Whoever is pleased to meet Allah tomorrow as a Muslim, let him preserve these prayers where they are called; for Allah legislated for His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the ways of guidance, for they are from the ways of guidance; if you prayed in your houses as this absentee prayed in his house you would abandon the way of your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and if you abandoned the way of your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) you would go astray; no man purifies himself excellently then intends a mosque except Allah writes for him with every step a good deed, raises him a degree, and removes from him an evil deed; we saw none absented from it except a hypocrite of known hypocrisy, and a man would be supported between two men until stood in the row."
"8630","8630 - Umar ibn Hafs al-Sadusi narrated to us, Asim ibn Ali narrated to us, al-Masudi narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Aqmar narrated to us, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abd Allah, he said: Whoever is pleased to meet Allah tomorrow as a Muslim, let him preserve these prayers where they are called; for Allah legislated for your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the ways of guidance, and they are from the ways of guidance; I think every one of you has a mosque he prays in; if you abandoned the way of your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) you would go astray; no Muslim servant performs ablution excellently then proceeds to prayer except Allah writes for him with every step a good deed, raises him a degree, and removes from him a sin."
"8631","8631 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, Sharik narrated to us, from Ali ibn al-Aqmar and Ibrahim ibn Muslim al-Hajari, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abd Allah, he said: Whoever is pleased to meet Allah tomorrow as a Muslim, let him preserve these five prayers when they are called; for they are from the ways of guidance; then he mentioned similar to al-Masudi's hadith."
"8632","8632 - Ahmad ibn Zuhayr narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Husayn al-Dirhami narrated to us, Ali ibn Khalid narrated to us, al-Masudi narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abd Allah, he said: Whoever is pleased to meet Allah tomorrow as a Muslim, let him preserve these five prayers when they are called; for Allah legislated for your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the ways of guidance, and they are from the ways of guidance; every man of you has a mosque in his house; if you abandoned your mosques you would abandon the way of your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and if you abandoned the way of your Prophet you would go astray."
"8633","8633 - Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Mamar ibn Sahl narrated to us, Amir ibn Mudrik narrated to us, Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq narrated to us, Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abd Allah, he said: Whoever is pleased to meet Allah tomorrow as a Muslim, let him preserve these prescribed prayers where they are called; for Allah legislated for your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the ways of guidance, and they are from the ways of guidance; if you prayed in your houses as this absentee prays you would abandon the way of your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and if you abandoned the way of your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) you would go astray; we saw none absented from it except a hypocrite of known hypocrisy; I saw a man of us supported between two men until stood in the row; no Muslim servant performs ablution in his house excellently then intends a mosque and comes to it, lifting a foot and putting a foot, except Allah writes for him ten good deeds, erases ten evil deeds, and raises him ten degrees, so much that we used to shorten steps."
"8634","8634 - Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Abbas al-Asbahani narrated to us, Sahl ibn Uthman narrated to us, Yahya ibn Zakariya ibn Abi Zaidah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abd Allah ibn Masud, he said: We saw none absented from prayer except a hypocrite of known hypocrisy, or a sick person; the sick would walk between two men coming to prayer; and he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught us the ways of guidance, and among the ways of guidance is prayer in the mosque in which the call is made."
"8635","8635 - Ibrahim ibn Nailah al-Asbahani narrated to us, Ismail ibn Amr al-Bajali narrated to us, Sharik narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abd Allah, he said: We saw none absented from prayer except a hypocrite of known hypocrisy; the sick would walk between two men coming to prayer; then he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught us the ways of guidance, and among the ways of guidance is to pray in the mosque in which the call is made."
"8636","8636 - Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami narrated to me, Muammal ibn Ismail narrated to us, from Sufyan, from al-Amash, from Umarah ibn Umayr, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abd Allah, he said: Hold to the five prayers in the mosque, for they are from the matter of guidance and the way of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"8637","8637 - Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Dahir ibn Nuh narrated to us, Amr ibn al-Walid al-Aghdf narrated to us, he said: I heard Kahmas ibn al-Hasan narrate from Harun al-Asamm, he said: Ibn Masud used to say: Whoever is pleased to meet Allah as a Muslim servant, let him pray the prayers where they are called, for they are the guidance of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); if every man prayed in his house you would abandon the guidance of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and if you abandoned the guidance of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) you would go astray; we were, with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) among us, prayer not established until rows were straightened; today prayer is about to be established while one of them calls: O slave girl, bring ablution water, uncovering his forearms; we were, with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) among us, relating that when a man performs ablution in his house then goes out to prayer, he does not lift a foot except raised a degree for it, nor puts down another except Allah removes a sin for it, so we used to shorten steps."
"8638","8638 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, al-Masudi narrated to us, Muslim al-Batin narrated to me, from Amr ibn Maymun, he said: I sat with Abd Allah - I think he said: a year - and we did not hear him narrate in it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), except that one day he spoke and it ran on his tongue: The Messenger of Allah said, and distress overcame him until I saw sweat pour from him; he said: If Allah wills, above that, or near that, or below that."
"8639","8639 - Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Ali ibn Hakim al-Awdi narrated to us, Sharik narrated to us, from Abu al-Umays, from Muslim al-Batin, from Abu Amr al-Shaybani, he said: I used to sit with Ibn Masud for a year and he would not say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said; when he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, trembling seized him, and he says: Thus, or similar to this, or near this, or what Allah wills."
"8640","8640 - Muhammad ibn Said ibn Kisa al-Wasiti narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Yahya al-Hallab narrated to us, Yahya ibn al-Sakan narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, Utbah Abu al-Umays narrated to us, from Muslim al-Batin, from Abu Ubaydah, he said: A year would pass over Abd Allah without narrating from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); one day he narrated while we were with him, and said: Similar to this, or near this."
"8641","8641 - Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Abdah ibn Abd Allah al-Saffar narrated to us, Yahya ibn Kathir al-Anbari narrated to us, from Shabbah ibn Muslim al-Batin, from his father, from Amr ibn Maymun, he said: I sat with Abd Allah for a year and did not hear him narrate from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); one day he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, then his face changed, then he said: Similar to this, or above this, or below this."
"8642","8642 - Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn Mukram narrated to us, Surayj ibn Yunus narrated to us, and Ahmad ibn Zuhayr al-Tustari narrated to us, Hawtharah ibn Muhammad al-Muqri narrated to us, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Ammar al-Duhni, from Muslim ibn Abi Imran al-Batin, from Amr ibn Maymun, he said: I accompanied Abd Allah ibn Masud for eighteen months and did not hear him narrate from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except one hadith, then he sweated and said: This, or similar to this, or like this."
"8643","8643 - Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Ismail ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Ibn Awn, from Muslim ibn Abi Imran, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from Amr ibn Maymun, he said: No Thursday evening missed me but I came to Abd Allah ibn Masud in it, and I never heard him say with anything: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), until one evening he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he bowed his head and raised it, and I saw him with shirt buttons loosened, his neck veins swollen, his eyes tearful, and he said: Or above that, or below that, or near that, or like that."
"8644","8644 - Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Abu Dawud al-Hafari narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Muhajir, from Muslim al-Batin, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, from Abd Allah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, then his face changed, then he said: Similar to this or near this."
"8645","8645 - Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Dirar ibn Surad narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Asim, from Zirr, from Abd Allah, that one day he narrated a hadith from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Similar to that, or like it, and he was not seen narrating after this hadith afterwards."
"8646","8646 - Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Mujahid ibn Musa narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Numayr narrated to us, from Malik ibn Mighwal, from al-Shabi, from Alqamah, from Abd Allah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, then trembling seized him, and he said: Similar to this or like this."
"8647","8647 - Ahmad ibn Amr al-Qatrani narrated to us, Abu Kamil al-Jahdari narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Mukhtar narrated to us, Mansur ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, al-Shabi narrated to us, from Alqamah, that Abd Allah ibn Masud used to stand every Thursday evening and I never heard him in any evening say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, except once; he said: I looked at him leaning on a staff, and I saw the staff shaking."
"8648","8648 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Ghassan Malik ibn Ismail narrated to us, Israil narrated to us, from Abu Husayn, from al-Shabi, from Masruq, from Abd Allah, that one day he narrated and said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and his garments trembled; he said: This, or similar to it, or like this."
"8649","8649 - Ahmad ibn Zuhayr al-Tustari narrated to us, al-Fadl ibn Sahl al-Araj narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ghaylan narrated to us, Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Firas, from al-Shabi, from Masruq, from Abd Allah, he said: Sometimes he would narrate a hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his color would change, and he would say: This, or near this."
"8650","8650 - Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn Usayd al-Asbahani narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Asim narrated to us, Abu Dawud narrated to us, Shayban narrated to us, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, from his uncle, he said: I sat with Ibn Masud and did not hear him narrate from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except one hadith I heard him narrate, then he shook as a palm frond shakes, then he said: This, or similar to it."
"8651","8651 - Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Shami al-Kufi narrated to us, Abu Kurayb narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Hisham narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, from his uncle Qays ibn Abd, he said: I sat with Ibn Masud for a year and never heard him narrate a hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at all, except once; I saw him shake as a palm frond shakes, then he said: Near this, or similar to this."
"8652","8652 - Ahmad ibn Zuhayr al-Tustari narrated to us, Ismail ibn Hibban al-Wasiti narrated to us, Zakariya ibn Adi narrated to us, Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, he said: Abd Allah would remain a year without saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said; when he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, trembling seized him and his face changed; he said: Thus, or thus, or thus."
"8653","8653 - al-Haytham ibn Khalaf al-Duri narrated to us, Abu Kurayb narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Fudayl ibn Ghazwan narrated to us, from Bayan Abu Bishr, from Amir, and he mentioned Qays, he said: Ibn Masud would narrate for a month without saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); when he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: This, or similar to this, or near this, while trembling."
"8654","8654 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Abu al-Zara, he said: al-Musayyab ibn Najabah came to Abd Allah and said: I left folk in the mosque saying: whoever glorifies such and such has such and such; he said: Stand, O Alqamah; when he saw them he said: O Alqamah, divert the people's eyes from me; when he heard them and what they say he said: You are holding to the tail of misguidance, or you are better guided than the companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"8655","8655 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn Uyaynah, from Bayan, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, he said: A storyteller sitting at night telling people: say such was mentioned to Ibn Masud; he said: When you see him inform me; he said: They informed him, and Abd Allah came veiled and said: Whoever knows me has known me, whoever does not know me, I am Abd Allah ibn Masud; you know you are better guided than Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his companions, or you are attached to the tail of misguidance."
"8656","8656 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Jafar ibn Sulayman, Ata ibn al-Saib informed us, I know it only from Abu al-Bakhtari, he said: It reached Abd Allah ibn Masud that folk sit from Maghrib to Isha glorifying, saying: say such and say such; Abd Allah said: If they sit, inform me; when they sat they came to him, so he went and entered with them and sat wearing a cloak; they engaged in their glorification, so Abd Allah removed the cloak from his head and said: I am Abd Allah ibn Masud; the folk fell silent, and he said: You have brought a dark innovation, or you have surpassed the companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in knowledge; a man of Banu Tamim said: We brought no dark innovation, nor surpassed the companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in knowledge; Amr ibn Utbah ibn Farqad said: I seek Allah's forgiveness, O Ibn Masud, and repent to Him; he ordered them to disperse; he said: Ibn Masud saw two circles in the mosque of Kufa and stood from them and said: Which of you was before its companion? One said: We were; he said to the other: Rise to it; so he made them one."
"8657","8657 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Abd al-Salam ibn Harb narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Abu al-Bakhtari, and sometimes he said: Amir, he said: He entered the mosque and there were two circles; he said to the boy: Go and see, are these sitting before or these? He came and said: These; he said: One narrator suffices the mosque; those before you perished only through mutual rivalry."
"8658","8658 - Uthman ibn Umar al-Dabbi narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Raja narrated to us, Zaidah informed us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Abu al-Bakhtari, he said: It was mentioned to Abd Allah that a man is gathered to, and he mentioned Abu Nuaym's hadith."
"8659","8659 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Abu Umar al-Darir narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah informed us, that Ata ibn al-Saib informed them from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, he said: Amr ibn Utbah ibn Farqad al-Sulami and Muaddad with some of their companions took a mosque in which they glorified between Maghrib and Isha such, declared oneness such, and praised such; Abd Allah ibn Masud was informed of that and said to the one who informed him: When they sit, inform me; when they sat he informed him, and Abd Allah came wearing a cloak until he entered upon them and removed the cloak from his head, then said: I am Ibn Umm Abd; by Allah you have brought a dark innovation, or you have surpassed the companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in knowledge; Muaddad, an eloquent man, said: By Allah we brought no dark innovation nor surpassed the companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); Abd Allah said: If you follow the folk you have been clearly preceded, and if you turn right and left you have gone far astray."
"8660","8660 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zuhayr narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abd Allah, he said: Do not bore people lest they tire of remembrance."
"8661","8661 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Mamar informed us, from al-Amash, from Ibn Masud, he passed a man reminding folk and said: O reminder, do not make people despair."
"8662","8662 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu al-Numan Arim narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Mujalid ibn Said, from Amr ibn Salamah, he said: We were sitting at Ibn Masud's door between Maghrib and Isha, and Abu Musa came and said: Has Abu Abd al-Rahman come out to you? Ibn Masud came out, and Abu Musa said: What brought you at this hour? He said: By Allah, only that I saw a matter that frightened me, though it is good; it frightened me though it is good: folk sitting in the mosque and a man saying to them: Glorify such and such, praise such and such; Abd Allah went and we went with him until he came to them and said: How quickly you went astray while the companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) are alive, his wives young, his clothes and vessels unchanged; count your evil deeds, and I guarantee upon Allah to count your good deeds."
"8663","8663 - Ahmad ibn Zuhayr al-Tustari narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Bistam narrated to us, Abu Ahmad al-Zubayri narrated to us, Sharik narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr ibn Zurarah, he said: Abd Allah stood over me while I was storytelling in the mosque and said: O Amr, you have invented an innovation of misguidance, or you are better guided than Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his companions; I saw them disperse from me until I saw my place with none in it."
"8664","8664 - al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Ali ibn Nasr narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Dawud narrated to us, Ali ibn Salih narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abd Allah ibn Agharr, he said: It reached Ibn Masud that Amr ibn Zurarah with companions of his remind them; Abd Allah came to them and said: Are you better guided or the companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? You are holding to an edge of misguidance."
"8665","8665 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Ghassan Malik ibn Ismail narrated to us, Israil narrated to us, from Ashath ibn Abi al-Shatha, from al-Aswad ibn Hilal, from Abd Allah, he said: A storytelling man was mentioned to him, so he came and sat among the folk and heard him say: Glory be to Allah such and such; when he heard that he stood and said: Do you not listen? When they looked at him he said: You are better guided than Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his companions? You are holding to an edge of misguidance."
"8666","8666 - Muhammad ibn Hayyan al-Mazini narrated to us, Amr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Shubah informed us, from Abu Ishaq, he said: I heard Abu al-Ahwas, from Abd Allah, he said: When people fall into evil, say: I have no example in evil."
"8667","8667 - Muhammad ibn Ali al-Saigh al-Makki narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Hudayj ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Said ibn Wahb, from Ibn Masud, he said: When people fall into tribulation and they say: Go out, you have an example in people, then say: I have no example in evil."
"8668","8668 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, he said: Ibn Masud said: This Quran is Allah's banquet; whoever of you can learn something from it, let him do so; the smallest of houses in good is that in which there is nothing of Allah's Book; the house in which there is nothing of Allah's Book is like a ruined house with no inhabitant; Satan exits the house in which Surat al-Baqarah is heard."
"8669","8669 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from Asim, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abd Allah, he said: Recite Surat al-Baqarah in your houses, for Satan does not enter a house in which Surat al-Baqarah is recited."
"8670","8670 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Arim Abu al-Numan narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Asim, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Everything has a hump, and the hump of the Quran is al-Baqarah; everything has a core, and the core of the Quran is al-Mufassal; devils exit the house in which Surat al-Baqarah is recited; the smallest of houses is the interior in which there is nothing of Allah's Book."
"8671","8671 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Arim Abu al-Numan narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Asim, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Everything has a hump, and the hump of the Quran is al-Baqarah; everything has a core, and the core of the Quran is al-Mufassal; devils exit the house in which Surat al-Baqarah is recited; the smallest of houses is the interior in which there is nothing of Allah's Book."
"8672","8672 - Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi and Muhammad ibn Bishr narrated to us, both said: Shubah narrated to us, and Muhammad ibn Hayyan al-Mazini narrated to us, Amr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Shubah informed us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, he said: Abd Allah used to say: The smallest of houses is that in which there is no Book of Allah; the house in which there is no Book of Allah is ruined like the ruin of a house with no inhabitant."
"8673","8673 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibrahim al-Hajari, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abd Allah ibn Masud, he said: This Quran is Allah's banquet, so learn from His banquet what you can; this Quran is Allah's rope which He commanded, the clear light and beneficial healing, protection for whoever holds to it, salvation for whoever clings to it; it does not deviate to be straightened, does not stray to be blamed, its wonders do not end, it does not wear from repetition; recite it, for Allah rewards you with ten good deeds for every letter of it; I do not say to you: Alif-Lam-Mim is a letter, but Alif is a letter, Lam a letter, Mim a letter."
"8674","8674 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Mamar informed us, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Abu Ubaydah, from Ibn Masud, he said: Whoever recites the Quran has for every letter ten good deeds; I do not say: Alif-Lam-Mim is ten, but Alif, Lam, Mim are thirty good deeds."
"8675","8675 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Arim Abu al-Numan narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Ibn Masud, he said: Learn the Quran and recite it, for you are rewarded for it with a good deed for every letter; I do not say: Alif-Lam-Mim is a good deed, but Alif, Lam, Mim are thirty good deeds; that is because Allah says: Whoever brings a good deed has ten like it."
"8676","8676 - Muhammad ibn Abdus ibn Kamil narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Jad narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abd Allah ibn Masud, he said: Learn the Quran and recite it, you are rewarded with ten good deeds for every letter; I do not say: Alif-Lam-Mim, but I say: Alif, Lam, Mim."
"8677","8677 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Mamar informed us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abd Allah ibn Masud, he said: A man died and angels of punishment came and sat at his head; it said: You have no way to him, he used to recite Surat al-Mulk; they sat at his feet, and it said: You have no way to him, he used to stand over us with Surat al-Mulk; they sat at his belly and it said: You have no way to him, he retained Surat al-Mulk in me; so it was named the Preventer."
"8678","8678 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from al-Thawri, from Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, from Ibn Masud, he said: A man is approached in his grave; his feet are approached and they say: You have no way over what is before us, he used to recite Surat al-Mulk over us; then his belly is approached and it says: You have no way over me, he retained Surat al-Mulk in me; then he is approached from his head and it says: You have no way over what is before me, he used to recite Surat al-Mulk in me; Abd Allah said: It is the Preventer, preventing the punishment of the grave; it is in the Torah: this Surat al-Mulk, whoever recites it in a night has done much and well."
"8679","8679 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud narrated to us, from Zirr, from Abd Allah, he said: When a man recites Surat al-Mulk every night and is entered into his grave, he is approached in his grave, beginning with his feet, and his feet say: You have no way over what is before me; then he mentioned the like of it."
"8680","8680 - Yusuf ibn Yaqub al-Qadi narrated to us, Amr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Shubah informed us, from Asim, from Zirr, from Abd Allah, the like of it."
"8681","8681 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Arim Abu al-Numan narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Asim, from Zirr, from Abd Allah, the like of it."
"8682","8682 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq and others, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, he said: Abd Allah said: The Quran is an intercessor accepted, and a prosecutor believed; whoever puts it before him it leads him to Paradise, whoever puts it behind him it drives him to Hell."
"8683","8683 - Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from Abd Allah, he said: Whoever loves the Quran and is pleased with it is in good."
"8684","8684 - Muhammad ibn Hayyan al-Mazini narrated to us, Amr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Shubah informed us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from Abd Allah, he said: Whoever loves to know that he loves Allah and His Messenger, let him see: if he loves the Quran, he loves Allah and His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"8685","8685 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, Mutamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: I heard Mansur, from Amir, he said: Shutayr ibn Shakl and Masruq ibn al-Ajda sat; Shutayr said to Masruq: Narrate what you heard from Abd Allah and I will confirm you, or I narrate and you confirm me; he said: I heard Abd Allah say: The most comprehensive verse in the Quran for good and evil is a verse in Surat al-Nahl: Allah commands justice, excellence, giving to relatives, and forbids immorality, wrongdoing and transgression; He admonishes you that you may remember; he said: You spoke truth; he said: I heard Abd Allah say: The greatest verse in the Quran in joy is a verse in Surat al-Ghuraf: Say: O My servants who transgressed against themselves, do not despair of Allah's mercy; Allah forgives all sins, if He wills; he said: You spoke truth."
"8686","8686 - Muhammad ibn Ali al-Saigh al-Makki narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Said ibn Masruq, from al-Shabi, he said: Masruq and Shutayr ibn Shakl sat in the grand mosque, and people saw them and turned to them; Masruq said to Shutayr: These have turned to us only to narrate to them; either you narrate and I confirm you, or I narrate and you confirm me; Masruq said: Narrate and I will confirm you; Shutayr said: Abd Allah ibn Masud narrated to us that the greatest verse in Allah's Book is: Allah, there is no god but He, the Living, the Sustainer - to the end of the verse; Masruq said: You spoke truth. Abd Allah narrated to us that the most comprehensive verse in Allah's Book is: Allah commands justice and excellence - to the end of the verse; Masruq said: You spoke truth; and he narrated to us that the greatest - or most - verse in Allah's Book in joy is: O My servants who transgressed against themselves, do not despair of Allah's mercy - to the end of the verse; Masruq said: You spoke truth; and he narrated to us that the severest verse in Allah's Book in reliance is: Whoever fears Allah, He makes for him a way out and provides for him whence he does not expect - to the end of the verse; Masruq said: You spoke truth."
"8687","8687 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from al-Thawri, from Jabir and others, from al-Shabi, from Masruq and Shutayr ibn Shakl al-Absi, he said: We sat in the mosque and people rose to them; one of them said to his companion: They stood to us only to narrate to them; either I narrate to them and you confirm me, or you narrate to them and I confirm you; one said: I heard Abd Allah say: The greatest verse in the Quran is Ayat al-Kursi; the other said: You spoke truth; the other said: I heard Abd Allah say: The most comprehensive verse in the Quran is: Allah commands justice and excellence; he said: You spoke truth; he said: And I heard him say: The severest verse in the Quran in reliance is: Whoever fears Allah, He makes for him a way out; he said: You spoke truth; the other said: And I heard him say: The greatest verse in the Quran in joy is: O My servants who transgressed against themselves; he said: You spoke truth."
"8688","8688 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, and Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Arim Abu al-Numan narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Asim ibn Bahdalah, from Abu al-Duha, he said: Masruq and Shutayr ibn Shakl gathered in the mosque, and the mosque circles turned to them; Masruq said: I do not see these sitting to us except to hear good from us; either you narrate from Abd Allah and I confirm you, or I narrate from Abd Allah and you confirm me; he said: Narrate to us, O Abu Aishah; Masruq said: I heard Abd Allah ibn Masud say: The two eyes commit adultery, the two feet commit adultery, the two hands commit adultery, and the private part confirms that or denies it; he said: Yes, and I heard it; he said: Did you hear Abd Allah ibn Masud say: The most comprehensive verse in the Quran in lawful, unlawful, command and prohibition is: Allah commands justice, excellence, giving to relatives, and forbids immorality - to the end of the verse? He said: Yes; he said: And I heard it; he said: Did you hear Abd Allah ibn Masud say: The greatest verse in the Quran in reliance is: Whoever fears Allah, He makes for him a way out and provides for him whence he does not expect? He said: Yes; he said: And I heard it; he said: Did you hear Abd Allah ibn Masud say: The severest verse in the Quran in joy is: O My servants who transgressed against themselves, do not despair of Allah's mercy - to the end of the verse? He said: Yes; he said: And I heard it; and the wording is Hammad ibn Zayd's hadith."
"8689","8689 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from Abu Ishaq, he said: Abd Allah ibn Masud said: If it were said to one of you: If you went early to the village you would have four hundred young she-camels, he would say: It is time for me to go early; if one of you went early and learned a verse from Allah's Book it would be better for him than four and four and four, until he counted much; Abu Ishaq said: Abu Ubaydah informed me that Ibn Masud, when morning came and he went out, people came to his house, and he would say: In your places; then he would pass those he taught the Quran and say: O father of so-and-so, which surah are you on? He informs him, and he says: Which verse? He informs him, and he opens for him the next verse, then says: Learn it, for it is better for you than what is between heaven and earth; the man would think there is no verse in the Quran better than it, then he passes the next and says the like to him, until he says that to all of them."
"8690","8690 - Muhammad ibn Hayyan al-Mazini narrated to us, Amr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Shubah informed us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abd Allah, that he used to recite a verse to a man then say: It is better than what the sun rose upon - or than what is on earth - until he would say that about the whole Quran."
"8691","8691 - Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, Khalaf ibn al-Walid narrated to us, Israil informed us, from Abu Ishaq, from Murrah, from Ibn Masud, he said: Whoever desires the good of the first and the last, let him stir up the Quran, for in it is the good of the first and the last."
"8692","8692 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zuhayr narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Murrah, he said: Abd Allah said: Whoever desires knowledge, let him stir up the Quran, for it is the good of the first and the good of the last."
"8693","8693 - Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Murrah, from Abd Allah, he said: Whoever desires knowledge, let him stir up the Quran, for in it is the knowledge of the first and the last."
"8694","8694 - Yusuf al-Qadi narrated to us, Amr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Shubah informed us, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abd Allah, he said: For every letter there is a limit, and for every limit an outlook."
"8695","8695 - Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abd Allah, he said: There is no letter of the Quran but has a limit, and every limit has an outlook."
"8696","8696 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from Asim, from Zirr, from Abd Allah, he said: Whoever recites: Say: He is Allah, One, it is as if he recited a third of the Quran."
"8697","8697 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, al-Masudi narrated to us, from al-Qasim, he said: Abd Allah said: The likeness of one who recites the Quran and does not act upon it is like sweet basil, its scent pleasant with no taste; the likeness of one who acts upon the Quran and does not recite it is like a date, its taste pleasant with no scent; the likeness of one who learns the Quran and teaches it is like a citron, its taste pleasant and scent pleasant; the likeness of one who neither recites the Quran nor acts upon it is like colocynth, its taste foul and scent foul."
"8698","8698 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, al-Masudi narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Amr ibn Salamah, from his father, from Abd Allah, he said: Whoever recites Surat al-Baqarah in a night has done much and well."
"8699","8699 - Abu Khalifah al-Fadl ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, Abu al-Walid narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, Yahya ibn Amr ibn Salamah informed me, he said: I heard my father say: Abd Allah said: Whoever recites three verses from Surat al-Baqarah in a night has done much and well."
"8700","8700 - Fudayl ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Abu al-Umays narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Shabi say: Abd Allah said: Whoever recites ten verses from Surat al-Baqarah in a house, no devil enters that house that night until morning: four verses from its beginning, Ayat al-Kursi, two verses after it, and its endings."
"8701","Muhammad ibn Ali al-Saigh narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, from Tamim ibn Hadlam, from Ibn Masud, that he used to recite: And all came to Him humbled."
"8702","Muhammad ibn Ali al-Saigh narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Jarir narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, from Tamim ibn Hadlam, who said: I recited the Quran to Abdullah and he did not correct me except on two wordings. I said: And all came to Him humbled, and: And all shall come to Him humbled. He said: and I said: Until when the messengers despaired and thought they had been denied. He said: And thought they had been denied."
"8703","Muhammad ibn Ali al-Saigh narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, from Dawud, from al-Shabi, from Alqamah, from Ibn Masud, that he used to recite: This is nothing but the custom of the ancients, saying: Something they fabricated."
"8704","Muhammad ibn Ali al-Saigh narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Shihab ibn Khirash narrated to us, Musa ibn Yazid al-Kindi narrated to me, saying: Ibn Masud used to teach a man the Quran, and the man recited: Alms are only for the poor and the needy, without prolongation. Ibn Masud said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, did not teach it to me like this; he taught it to me as: Alms are only for the poor and the needy, and he prolonged it."
"8705","Al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Yahya al-Himmani narrated to us, Waki narrated to us, from Sufyan, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Abdullah, that he recited: Wherever he turns, he brings no good."
"8706","Al-Husayn ibn Ishaq narrated to us, Yahya al-Himmani narrated to us, Waki narrated to us, from Sufyan, from al-Amash, who said: Abdullah ibn Masud used to recite: And your Lord has enjoined that you worship none but Him."
"8707","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Shaqiq, who said: Abdullah said: I have heard the reciters and heard them close to one another, so recite as you were taught, and beware of affectation and disagreement, for it is only like one of you saying: Come here and come along."
"8708","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Shaqiq, who said: We said in the presence of Abdullah: Hayta. Abdullah said: Not hayta; we already asked about that, and that I recite as I was taught is dearer to me."
"8709","Muhammad ibn Ali al-Saigh narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Tamim ibn Salamah, from Arfajah, from Abdullah, that he used to say: Majraha wa mursaha, its sailing and its anchoring."
"8710","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, who said: Abdullah said: The mistake is not that part of it is recited into part of it, but the mistake is that you attach to it what is not from it."
"8711","Ibrahim ibn Ahmad al-Wakii narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us, from Layth, from Talhah ibn Musarrif, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Abdullah, in a raised report, who said: Recite the Quran with correct Arabic grammar, for it is Arabic."
"8712","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from Layth, from Talhah ibn Musarrif, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Abdullah, who said: Recite the Quran with correct grammar."
"8713","Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from Sabr Abu al-Hakam, from Ibn Masud, who said: Recite the Quran with correct Arabic, for it is Arabic; there will come a people who will master its wording, yet they are not the best of you."
"8714","Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Asim, from Zirr, from Abdullah, who said: Keep looking into the mushaf continually."
"8715","Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi narrated to us, Malik ibn Mighwal narrated to us, from Zubayd, from Murrah, from Abdullah, who said: Take care of the mushafs, for it slips away from the chests of men more swiftly than camels from their halters."
"8716","Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, al-Firyabi narrated to us, from Sufyan, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Abdullah, that he recited it as: Nay, you wondered while they mock."
"8717","Muhammad ibn Ali al-Saigh narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, Abu Ishaq al-Kufi told us, from Abu Khalid al-Kinani, from Ibn Masud, that he used to recite it as: The Living, the Ever-Sustaining."
"8718","Muhammad ibn Ali al-Saigh al-Makki narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, from Ibn Masud, that he used to recite: Until the camel passes through the eye of the needle. He said: The male mate of the she-camel."
"8719","Muhammad ibn Ali al-Saigh narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Amr ibn Thabit narrated to us, from his father, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The male mate of the she-camel."
"8720","Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn Uyaynah, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, who said: Abdullah said: When one of you asks his companion how he recites such-and-such verse, let him ask him about what comes before it."
"8721","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, who said: Abdullah said: When one of you asks his brother about a verse, let him not say: such-and-such, for he bears no duty by that; rather let him recite what is before it, then let him come between him and his need."
"8722","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, who said: I recited to Abdullah and my voice pleased him, so he said: Recite slowly and distinctly; may my father and mother be ransom for you."
"8723","Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq told us, al-Thawri told us, from Asim ibn Bahdalah, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, who said: Ibn Masud said: Keep looking into the mushaf continually; and when you differ over ya and ta, make them ya; keep the Quran in remembrance."
"8724","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah ibn Qudamah narrated to us, Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud narrated to us, from Abdullah, who said: When you dispute over ya or ta, make them ya, and keep the Quran masculine in form, for it is masculine."
"8725","Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from al-Thawri, from his father, from al-Musayyab ibn Rafi, from Shaddad ibn Maqil; al-Thawri said: And Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay narrated it to me, from Shaddad, that Ibn Masud said: This Quran will surely be taken away from among you. I said: Abu Abd al-Rahman, how will it be taken when we have fixed it in our mushafs? He said: It will be taken up in one night, so nothing of it will remain in the heart of any servant nor in any mushaf, and people will wake up poor like cattle. Then Abdullah recited: And if We willed, We could surely take away what We revealed to you, then you would find no guardian for it against Us."
"8726","Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay, from Shaddad ibn Maqil, who said: Abdullah said: The first thing you will lose of your religion is trustworthiness, and the last that remains is prayer; and a people will surely pray who have no faith."
"8727","Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Israil, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay, from Shaddad ibn Maqil, who said: I heard Ibn Masud say: The first thing you will lose of your religion is trustworthiness, and the last that remains of your religion is prayer; and a people will surely pray who have no religion; and the Quran will surely be taken away from among you. They said: Abu Abd al-Rahman, do we not recite the Quran and have fixed it in our mushafs? He said: The Quran will be taken up by night, and it will be removed from the hearts of men, so nothing of it will remain on earth."
"8728","Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Ibn Masud, who said: Whoever recites the Quran in less than three is a rajiz, one who rattles it off."
"8729","Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Ali ibn Badhimah, who said: I heard Abu Ubaydah ibn Abdullah, from his father, who said: Whoever recites the Quran in less than three is a rajiz."
"8730","Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Misar narrated to us, from Ali ibn Badhimah, from Abu Ubaydah, from Abdullah, who said: Whoever recites the Quran in less than three is a rajiz."
"8731","Ishaq narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from al-Thawri, from Ali ibn Badhimah, from Abu Ubaydah, from Abdullah, the like of it."
"8732","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from Hisham, from al-Hasan, that it reached him from Ibn Masud, who said: Whoever recites the Quran in less than three is a rajiz."
"8733","Muhammad ibn Abdus ibn Kamil narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Jad narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Dhakwan, who said: I heard Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Masud narrating from Abdullah, that he used to recite the Quran through in a week, and recite it through thrice in Ramadan."
"8734","Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman, from al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Umarah ibn Umayr, from Abu al-Ahwas, who said: Abdullah said: The Quran is not to be recited in less than three; recite it in seven, and let a man keep to his portion every day and night."
"8735","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Umarah, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah, the like of it."
"8736","Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj, from al-Amash, from Umarah ibn Umayr, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah, the like of it."
"8737","Ishaq narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from al-Thawri, from Husayn, from Abdullah ibn Utbah, who said: Ibn Masud used to recite the Quran through in three, and would take little help from the daytime."
"8738","Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, al-Masudi narrated to us, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Abu Ubaydah, who said: Abdullah used to recite the Quran through every three days, and would seldom take any of it in the daytime."
"8739","Ishaq narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq told us, Mamar told us, from Abu Ishaq, who said: I heard him say: Ibn Masud said: When the prostration is at the end of the surah, then bow if you wish or prostrate, for the prostration goes with the bowing. I said: Who narrated this to you, Abu Ishaq? He said: Our companions: Alqamah, al-Aswad, and al-Rabi ibn Khuthaym."
"8740","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zuhayr narrated to us, Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from Alqamah, Amr ibn Shurahbil, and Masruq, from Abdullah, the like of the hadith of Mamar."
"8741","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, who said: Abu Ishaq was asked, and it was said to him: Do you remember, from al-Aswad, that Abdullah said: When you recite a surah whose end is a prostration, if you wish then bow, for the bowing is part of the prostration, and if you wish then prostrate, then recite another surah after it? He said: Yes."
"8742","Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Aswad, from Abdullah, who said: When the prostration is the seal of the surah, then if you wish bow and if you wish prostrate."
"8743","Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Abu al-Walid narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Aswad, from Abdullah, the like of it."
"8744","Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Abu al-Duha, from Masruq, who said: Abdullah ibn Masud said: It is only the repentance of a prophet that is mentioned, so he would not prostrate in it, meaning Sad."
"8745","Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Misar narrated to us, from Asim, from Zirr, that Abdullah would not prostrate in Sad."
"8746","Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Asim, from Zirr, from Ibn Masud, that he would not prostrate in Sad, and he said: It is only the repentance of a prophet."
"8747","Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Arim Abu al-Numan narrated to us, and Muhammad ibn Ali al-Saigh narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us; both said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Asim, from Zirr, from Abdullah, that he would not prostrate in Sad, saying: It is only the repentance of a prophet."
"8748","Muhammad ibn Ali al-Saigh narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Abdah ibn Abi Lubabah, from Zirr, that Abdullah would not prostrate in Sad."
"8749","Muhammad ibn Ali al-Saigh narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, Mughirah told us, from Ibrahim, and Dawud told us, from al-Shabi; both said: Abdullah would not prostrate in Sad, saying: It is only the repentance of a prophet."
"8750","Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from Ata al-Khurasani, who said: Ibn Masud used to prompt a man while he recited; then he stood and urinated, so the man stopped reciting. Ibn Masud said to him: Recite. He said: You have urinated. He said: Recite. So he kept prompting him while he recited."
"8751","Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, that Ibn Masud was teaching a man; when he reached the bank of the Euphrates he urinated, so the man fell silent from him. He said: What is wrong with you? He said: You have broken your ablution. He said: Recite. So the man kept reciting and he kept prompting him."
"8752","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from Mughirah, who said: Ibrahim said: Abdullah relieved himself, then a man asked him to prompt him, so he prompted him before touching water."
"8753","Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from Jabir, who said: On the signet ring of Ibn Masud there was a tree, or something between two flies of a sword."
"8754","Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, Khallad ibn Yahya narrated to us, Fitr ibn Khalifah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah ibn Masud, who said: Whoever recites five verses in a night will never be written among the heedless that night; whoever recites a hundred verses will be written among the devout; whoever recites three hundred, a qintar will be written for him; and whoever recites seven hundred will succeed."
"8755","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from Layth ibn Abi Sulaym, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad, from his father, who said: Abdullah used to prostrate in: When the sky is rent asunder."
"8756","Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Hamzah al-Zayyat narrated to us, from Talhah ibn Musarrif, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, who said: I prostrated with Umar and with Abdullah in: When the sky is rent asunder. Al-Aswad said: As for one of them, I have no doubt about him."
"8757","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from Mansur ibn al-Mutamir, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, who said: I saw Abdullah and Umar, or one of them, prostrating in: When the sky is rent asunder."
"8758","Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from al-Shabi, I think from Alqamah, that Abdullah used to prostrate in al-Najm and: Read in the name of your Lord who created."
"8759","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, who said: Abdullah said: Whoever recites Surah al-Araf, or al-Najm, or: When the sky is rent asunder, or Bani Israil, or: Read in the name of your Lord who created, and wishes to bow with the end of them, let him bow; the bowing prostration suffices him; and if he prostrates, let him add another surah to it."
"8760","Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, from Ibn Masud, who said: Whoever recites al-Araf, al-Najm, and: Read in the name of your Lord who created, and wishes to bow with the end of them, let him bow; the bowing prostration suffices him; and if he prostrates, let him add another surah to it."
"8761","Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, from Ibn Masud, who said: Whoever recites al-Araf, al-Najm, and: Read in the name of your Lord who created, then if he wishes he bows with them and that suffices him, and if he wishes he prostrates, then stands, recites the surah, bows, and prostrates."
"8762","Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Arim narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Said ibn Abi Sadaqah, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, that Ibn Masud, when he recited al-Najm to the people, would prostrate with it; and when he recited it in prayer, he would bow with it and prostrate."
"8763","Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Abu Jafar al-Razi narrated to us, from al-Rabi ibn Anas, from a man, from Abdullah ibn Masud, who said: There is nothing due on him for smiling in prayer."
"8764","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zuhayr narrated to us, Abu Ishaq narrated to us, who said: I heard Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid and Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad mention that Abdullah ibn Masud used to prostrate at the first verse of: Ha Mim, a revelation from the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate."
"8765","Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Abbas ibn Qirtas narrated to us, al-Musayyab ibn Rafi narrated to me, I know him only from Zirr, who said: Abdullah said: The prayers are the good deeds; and the expiation for what is between the first prayer and Asr is the Asr prayer, and the expiation for what is between Asr and Maghrib is the Maghrib prayer, and the expiation for what is between Maghrib and Isha is the Isha prayer; then the Muslim retires to his bed with no sin upon him, so long as he avoids major sins. Then he recited: Indeed good deeds remove evil deeds."
"8766","Umar ibn Hafs al-Sadusi narrated to us, Asim narrated to us, al-Masudi narrated to us, from al-Qasim, from Laqit ibn Qabisah, who said: Abdullah ibn Masud said: You burn up, until when you pray Fajr it is washed off; then you burn up, until when you pray Zuhr it is washed off; then you burn up, until when you pray Asr it is washed off; until he counted all the prayers like this."
"8767","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Shaqiq, from Abdullah, who said: These established prayers are expiations for what is between them, so long as major sins are avoided."
"8768","Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Abu Wail, from Ibn Masud, who said: The prayers are expiations for what is between them, so long as major sins are avoided."
"8769","Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Abd al-Salam ibn Harb narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, who said: We used to recite to Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami while he walked; when we passed a prostration he would say takbir and we would say takbir, and he would prostrate and we would prostrate by gesture; he would raise his head and say: Peace be upon you, and we would say: And upon you be peace. And Abu Abd al-Rahman claimed that Abdullah used to do that with them."
"8770","Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Ubaydah, from Abdullah ibn Masud, who said: Whoever among you recites the Quran, let him learn the laws of inheritance; if a Bedouin meets him and says: O Muhajir, do you recite the Quran? And he says: Yes; then the Bedouin says: And I recite; then the Bedouin says: Do you know the faraid, O Muhajir? If he says: Yes, he says: An increase and a good thing; and if he says: I do not know them well, he says: What merit have you over me, O Muhajir?"
"8771","Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz and Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, both saying: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, al-Amash narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Hayyan, from Anbasah ibn Uqbah, who said: Abdullah said: By Allah, besides whom there is no god, there is nothing on the face of the earth more in need of a long imprisonment than the tongue."
"8772","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Yazid ibn Hayyan, from Anbasah ibn Uqbah, who said: Abdullah said: By Allah, besides whom there is no god, there is nothing on the back of the earth more in need of a long imprisonment than the tongue."
"8773","Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Shuayb al-Simsar narrated to us, Khalid ibn Khirash narrated to us, Fudayl ibn Iyad narrated to us, from Sulayman al-Amash, from Yazid ibn Hayyan, from Anbasah ibn Uqbah, who said: Abdullah said: There is nothing on earth more deserving of a long imprisonment than the tongue."
"8774","Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Hayyan, from Anbasah ibn Uqbah, who said: I heard Abdullah ibn Masud say: There is nothing more deserving of a long imprisonment than the tongue."
"8775","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from Mansur, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from his father, from Abdullah, who said: Sin is the scratching of hearts; whatever scratches in the heart of one of you, let him leave it."
"8776","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah, who said: No glance occurs but Satan has a craving in it; and sin is the scratching of hearts."
"8777","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, who said: Abdullah said: Beware of the gnawings of chests."
"8778","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud, from Abu Razin, from Abdullah ibn Masud, who said: Whoever makes a show will have Allah make a show of him; whoever shows off, Allah will show off through him on the Day of Resurrection; and whoever humbles himself for Allah in humility, Allah will raise him on the Day of Resurrection."
"8779","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Abdullah, who said: Go out in the morning as a scholar or a learner, and do not go out between that; and if you do not do so, then love the scholars and do not hate them."
"8780","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, the family of Abdullah narrated to me that Abdullah advised his son Abd al-Rahman, saying: I enjoin upon you the fear of Allah; let your house contain you, weep over your sin, and hold your tongue."
"8781","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abdullah ibn Murrah, from Masruq, from Abdullah, who said: A hoarding man will be branded, so dirham will touch dirham and no dinar dinar; his skin will be stretched until every dinar and dirham is placed each on its own spot."
"8782","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Umarah, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah, who said: Accustom yourselves to good, for good comes by habit; and guard your intentions in prayer."
"8783","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Tamim ibn Salamah, from Abu Ubaydah, from Abdullah, who said: Whomever Allah intends good for, He gives him understanding in religion."
"8784","Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Qays, from Huzayl ibn Shurahbil, from Abdullah, who said: Whoever wants the Hereafter harms his world, and whoever wants the world harms his Hereafter; so he commanded them to harm the perishing for the lasting."
"8785","Al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Muslim al-Batin, from Adasah al-Tai, who said: I was at Shiraf, and Abdullah alighted with us; my family sent me to him with some things, and some slave boys of ours who were with the camels came from a journey of four nights with birds. I took them to him, and when I took them to him he asked me: From where did you bring me these birds? He said: I said: Some slave boys of ours who were with the camels brought them, from a journey of four nights. Abdullah said: I wish I were where it was hunted, not speaking to any human about anything, nor anyone speaking to me, until I meet Allah."
"8786","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Khaythamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from al-Aswad, from Abdullah, who said: By Allah, besides whom there is no god, there is no living soul but death is better for it if he is righteous; indeed Allah Almighty says: And what is with Allah is better for the righteous. And if he is wicked, indeed Allah Almighty says: And let not those who disbelieve think that Our giving them respite is good for themselves; We only give them respite that they may increase in sin."
"8787","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Tamim ibn Salamah, from Abu Ubaydah, from Abdullah, who said: O people, learn; whoever knows, let him act."
"8788","Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Fadalah ibn al-Fadl narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Abu Abd al-Rahman, who said: Abdullah said: There will come a people who will drink knowledge like drink."
"8789","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from al-Amash, who said: Khaythamah claimed, saying: Abdullah said one day, when they pressed him, and he said: O Jar ibn Qays, to al-Harith, what do you think they want from what they ask about? He said: That they may know it then abandon it. He said: You spoke the truth, by Him besides whom there is no god."
"8790","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Khaythamah, who said: Abdullah said: Let me not find one of you a corpse of the night and a gadfly of the day."
"8791","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah, who said: Let none of you entrust his religion to a man blindly; if he believes he believes, and if he disbelieves he disbelieves. And if you must follow an example, then follow the dead, for the living is not safe from trial."
"8792","Umar ibn Hafs al-Sadusi narrated to us, Asim ibn Ali narrated to us, al-Masudi narrated to us, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, who said: Abdullah said: Let none of you be an immaah, a yes-man. They said: What is the immaah, Abu Abd al-Rahman? He said: One who says: I am only with the people; if they are guided I am guided, and if they stray I stray. Rather, let each of you settle himself that if people disbelieve, he will not disbelieve."
"8793","Muhammad ibn al-Abbas al-Akhram al-Asbahani narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Ziyad al-Kufi narrated to us, Amr ibn Abd al-Ghaffar narrated to us, al-Amash narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah, who said: In the pre-Islamic age we used to call the immaah the one who comes to food though not invited; and verily the immaah among you today is the one who loads his religion upon others."
"8794","Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Amr ibn Hakam narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Ibrahim al-Hajari, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah, who said: We used to call the immaah in the pre-Islamic age the man invited to food whom another man follows uninvited; and today it is the one who loads the people with his religion. And we used to call adhah sorcery, and today it is gossip."
"8795","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Umarah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from Abdullah, who said: You pray more and strive more than the companions of Muhammad, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, yet they were better than you. They said: How is that, Abu Abd al-Rahman? He said: They were more detached from the world and more desirous of the Hereafter."
"8796","Muadh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Yahya ibn Mansur narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Sulayman al-Amash, from Malik ibn al-Harith, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from Abdullah, the like of the hadith of Zaidah."
"8797","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, from Abdullah, who said: Follow and do not innovate, for you have been sufficed; every innovation is misguidance."
"8798","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Khaythamah, from Abdullah, who said: The whole earth will be fire on the Day of Resurrection, and Paradise will be beyond it; they will see its maidens and its goblets. By Him in whose hand is the soul of Abdullah, a man will overflow with sweat until it flows over the earth his height, then it will rise until it reaches his nose, though the reckoning has not touched him. They said: Why is that? He said: From what he sees people suffering."
"8799","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Khaythamah, who said: Abdullah said: By Him besides whom there is no god, no servant thinks well of Allah but He will grant him his expectation, and that is because all good is in His hand."
"8800","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Khaythamah, who said: Abdullah said to his wife: Is today better or yesterday? She said: I do not know. He said: But I know; yesterday is better than today, and today is better than tomorrow, and so it will be until the Hour comes."
"8801","Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Abd al-Salam ibn Harb narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Abu Juhayfah, who said: Abdullah came out to us one day, dejected, and we said: What is wrong with you? He said: The pure part of the world has gone, and only the dregs remain; so death today is a gift for every Muslim."
"8802","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Abu Juhayfah, from Abdullah, who said: The pure part of the world has gone, and only the dregs remain; so death today is a gift for every Muslim."
"8803","Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Abu al-Kanud, from Abdullah, who said: The likeness of the world is like a thaghab. We said: What is thaghab? He said: A pond whose clear water has gone, and its murk remains; so death is the gift of every believer."
"8804","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from a man of al-Nakha, from Ibn Masud, who said: The people of affliction will wish on the Day of Resurrection, when they see the reward, that their skins had been clipped with shears."
"8805","Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Abd al-Salam ibn Harb narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, one who heard Ibn Masud narrated to me, saying: The people of affliction will wish, when they see the reward, that their bodies had been clipped with shears."
"8806","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from Ibrahim al-Hajari, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah, who said: The disbeliever will be bridled with his sweat on the Day of Resurrection from the length of that day, until he says: My Lord, relieve me, even to the Fire."
"8807","Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Ubaydah ibn Abdullah, from his father, who said: When one of you wants to ask, let him begin with praise and extolling Allah as He deserves, then let him send blessings upon the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, then let him ask after that, for that is more likely to succeed."
"8808","Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq told us, Mamar told us, from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Abu Mamar, from Ibn Masud, who said: When a man mounts a beast and does not remember Allah, Satan rides behind him and says to him: Sing; and if he does not do well, he says to him: Wish."
"8809","Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq told us, Mamar told us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Ibn Masud, who said: The Satan of the believer meets the Satan of the disbeliever, and sees the believer's Satan pale, dusty, and emaciated. The disbeliever's Satan says: What is wrong with you? Woe to you, you have perished. The believer's Satan says: No, by Allah, I get nothing from him; when he eats he mentions the name of Allah, when he drinks he mentions the name of Allah, and when he enters his house he mentions the name of Allah. The other says: But I eat of his food, drink of his drink, and sleep on his bed. So this one is sleek, and this one emaciated."
"8810","Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Misar narrated to us, from Wabarah, from Abd al-Malik ibn Maysarah, from Abu al-Tufayl, who said: Abdullah said: The major sins are: associating partners with Allah, despairing of the relief of Allah, desponding of the mercy of Allah, and feeling safe from the plot of Allah."
"8811","Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, Mamar told us, from Abu Ishaq, from Wabarah, from Amir, from Abu al-Tufayl, from Ibn Masud, that he said: The greatest of major sins are: associating partners with Allah, feeling safe from the plot of Allah, desponding of the mercy of Allah, and despairing of the relief of Allah."
"8812","Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Arim Abu al-Numan narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud narrated to us, from Abu Wail, from Abdullah, who said: The greatest of major sins are: associating partners with Allah, despairing of the relief of Allah, desponding of the mercy of Allah, and feeling safe toward the plot of Allah."
"8813","Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from Abu Ishaq, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from Ibn Masud, who said: A son of his came to him wearing a shirt of silk, the boy admiring his shirt; when he drew near Abdullah he tore it, then said: Go to your mother and tell her to dress you in a shirt other than this."
"8814","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Abu Ghassan Malik ibn Ismail narrated to us, Qays ibn al-Rabi narrated to us, Abu Ishaq told us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, who said: We were with Abdullah when a son of his came wearing a silk shirt. He said: Who dressed you in this? He said: My mother. He said: So he tore it. He said: Tell your mother to dress you in other than this."
"8815","Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ibn Masud, that he said: A man will never taste the taste of faith until he believes in the decree, and knows that he will die, and that he will be raised."
"8816","Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, Khalaf ibn al-Walid narrated to us, Israil narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, who said: I saw Ibn Masud wet the tip of his finger in his mouth, then say: By Allah, no servant tastes the taste of faith until he believes in the decree, and knows that he will die, then be raised after death."
"8817","Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from Qatadah, that Ibn Masud said: Three things: whoever has them in him will find their sweetness of faith: abandoning disputation over truth, and lying in jest, and knowing that what befell him was never going to miss him, and what missed him was never going to befall him."
"8818","Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq told us, Mamar told us, from al-Amash, from Shaqiq, who said: We were with Ibn Masud on a journey when he met a company of riders. We said: Who are the people? They said: We are the believers. Ibn Masud said: Did they then say: We are the people of Paradise?"
"8819","Yusuf al-Qadi narrated to us, Amr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Shubah told us, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, who said: A man said in the presence of Abdullah: I am a believer. Abdullah said: Say: I am in Paradise; but we have believed in Allah, His angels, and His messengers."
"8820","Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, that Ibn Masud said: I adjure by Allah anyone who severs kinship to stand away from us, for we want to supplicate our Lord, and the gates of heaven are shut before one who severs kinship."
"8821","Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, that Ibn Masud said: A man of the Children of Israil, when he sinned, would wake to find written on his door: You sinned such-and-such, and its expiation is such-and-such deed; perhaps he would deem it too much to do. Ibn Masud said: I would not love that Allah Almighty gave us that in place of this verse: And whoever does evil or wrongs himself, then seeks forgiveness of Allah, will find Allah Forgiving, Merciful."
"8822","Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Ubaydah, from Ibn Masud, that a man passed by a man while he was prostrating and stepped on his neck. He said: Do you step on my neck while I am prostrating? By Allah, Allah will never forgive you. Allah said: Do you swear upon Me? Verily I have forgiven him."
"8823","Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq told us, Mamar told us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from Ibn Masud: The likeness of belittled sins is like traveling people who alight in a barren land, with food that nothing will cook for them but fire; so they scatter, and this one brings dung, and this one brings a bone, and this one brings a stick, until they gather enough to cook their food. So likewise the owner of belittled sins tells a lie, commits a sin, and gathers of that what may cause Allah to cast him on his face into the fire of Hell."
"8824","Yusuf al-Qadi narrated to us, Amr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Shubah told us, from Simak ibn Harb, from a man, that he said: We sought permission from Abdullah after the dawn prayer, and he gave us permission, and threw a blanket over his wife, saying: I disliked holding you back."
"8825","Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Ubaydah, from Ibn Masud, who said: Two men at whom Allah smiles: a man with a horse among the best horses of his companions; the enemy meets them and they flee, while the other stands firm; if he is killed he is killed a martyr, so Allah smiles at him. And a man who stands at night, unknown to anyone, perfects his ablution, sends blessings upon Muhammad, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, praises Allah, and begins recitation; so Allah smiles at him, saying: Look at My servant; none sees him but Me."
"8826","Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Ubaydah, from Ibn Masud, who said: Three things I swear upon, and a fourth, if I swore upon it I would fulfill my oath: Allah does not make one who has a share in Islam like one who has no share; no servant takes Allah as guardian in the world but He entrusts him to another on the Day of Resurrection; no man loves a people but he will come with them; and the fourth, which if I swore upon it I would fulfill my oath: Allah does not conceal for a servant in the world but Allah will conceal for him in the Hereafter."
"8827","Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, al-Masudi narrated to us, from al-Qasim, who said: Abdullah said: Three things I swear upon, and a fourth, if I swore upon it I hope I would not sin, and I would fulfill my oath: that Allah does not make one with a share in Islam like one with no share; no Muslim servant takes Allah as guardian but He entrusts him to another on the Day of Resurrection; no man loves a people but Allah will raise him with them. And the shares of Islam are three: prayer, zakat, and fasting. And the fourth, which if I swore upon it I hope I would not sin: Allah does not conceal for a servant in the world but Allah will conceal for him in the Hereafter."
"8828","A chapter. Yusuf al-Qadi narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Zayd ibn Wahb, who said: I and a man went to Abdullah, and he was standing in prayer with two men around him. When he gave salam they asked him about a verse. He said to one of them: Who taught you? He said: Umar. He said to the other: Who taught you? He said: Abu Hakim, or Abu Amrah. He said: Recite as Umar taught you. Then he wept until his tears wet the pebbles, then said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, was a strong fortress for Islam; they would enter Islam and not leave it. When Umar was struck, the fortress was breached."
"8829","Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, Khallad ibn Yahya narrated to us, Misar ibn Kidam narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Maysarah, from Zayd ibn Wahb, who said: Two men disputed over a verse of the Quran and came to Abdullah ibn Masud. One of them said: Abu Amrah taught it to me; the other said: Umar taught it to me. When Umar was mentioned, Abdullah wept while standing, wiped his eyes, shook his hand over the pebbles, then said: It is clearer than the road of al-Saylahayn. Then he said: Recite it as Umar taught you; indeed no Muslim household in which sorrow for Umar did not enter on the day he was struck is but a household of evil. Umar was the most God-fearing of us and the best reciter of the Book of Allah."
"8830","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Zayd ibn Wahb, who said: Two men disputed over a verse; while we were so, Abdullah came from the direction of his in-laws. They stood to him, and I stood to him with them. They said: We disputed over a verse. Abdullah said to one of them: Recite; so he recited. He said: Who taught it to you? He said: Abu Amrah Maqil ibn Muqarrin. Then he said to the other: Recite; so he recited. He said: Who taught it to you? He said: Umar. His eyes filled with four tears and he wept until I saw him take some of his tears in his palm; he did so like this, and I saw two marks in the pebbles from the tears of Abdullah. Then Abdullah said: I do not think any Muslim household in which the sorrow of Umar did not enter on the day he was struck is but a household of evil. Umar was the most knowledgeable of us of Allah, the best reciter of the Book of Allah, and the most understanding of the religion of Allah; and recite it as Umar taught you, for by Allah it is clearer than the road of al-Saylahayn."
"8831","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Zayd ibn Wahb, who said: Two men came to Abdullah while we were with him, and said: Abu Abd al-Rahman, how do we recite this verse? Abdullah recited it to him. The man said: Abu Hakim taught it to me so-and-so. The other recited, and Abdullah said: Who taught it to you? He said: Umar. Abdullah said: Recite as Umar taught you. Then Abdullah wept until I saw his tears falling into the pebbles, then said: Umar was a strong fortress for Islam; people would enter into it and not leave. The fortress has now been breached, so people leave from it and do not enter."
"8832","Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn Uyaynah, from al-Amash, from Zayd ibn Wahb, who said: We came to Ibn Masud and found him praying; we waited for him until he finished his prayer. Two men came to him differing over a verse, and one of them recited it to him. Abdullah said: You did well; who taught you? He said: Abu Hakim al-Muzani taught me. He asked the other to recite, and said: Who taught you? He said: Umar ibn al-Khattab taught me. Abdullah wept until his tears moistened the pebbles, then said: Recite as Umar taught you. Then he drew a circle with his hand, then said: Umar was a strong fortress for Islam; people would enter through it and not leave it. When Umar died, the fortress was breached, so people leave from it and do not enter."
"8833","Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, al-Masudi narrated to us, from al-Qasim, who said: Abdullah said: The Islam of Umar was a conquest, his emigration was a victory, and his rule was a mercy. By Allah, we could not pray openly by the Kabah until Umar embraced Islam."
"8834","Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from Qatadah and Hammad, both saying: I heard them say; he heard them both say: Ibn Masud used to say: Umar ibn al-Khattab was a strong fortress for Islam; Islam would be entered and not left. When Umar died, a breach opened in the fortress, so it is left and not entered. When he took a road, we found it easy; and when the righteous are mentioned, then welcome to Umar. He was the divider between increase and decrease. By Allah, I wish I could serve the like of him until I die."
"8835","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, al-Amash narrated to us, from Shaqiq, who said: Abdullah said: By Allah, I think the knowledge of Umar, if placed in one pan of the scale and all other living people of the earth placed in the other pan, the knowledge of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, would outweigh them. Sulayman said: I mentioned it, and he said: Abdullah said better than that; he said: I think Umar went with nine-tenths of knowledge."
"8836","Abu Yazid al-Qaratis narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Waki narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Wail, who said: Abdullah said: If the knowledge of Umar were placed in one pan of a scale, and the knowledge of the people of the earth placed in a pan, his knowledge would outweigh theirs. Waki said: Al-Amash said: I found that objectionable, so I came to Ibrahim and mentioned it to him. He said: What did you find objectionable in that? By Allah, Abdullah said better than that; he said: I think nine-tenths of knowledge went on the day Umar went, may Allah be pleased with him."
"8837","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, Mansur narrated to us, who said: Abdullah said: I think Umar was raised on the day he died with nine-tenths of knowledge; and I think the knowledge of Umar, if placed in one pan of the scale, and the knowledge of those after him, the knowledge of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, would outweigh it."
"8838","A chapter. Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Abu Ubaydah, from Ibn Masud, that Said ibn Zayd said: Abu Abd al-Rahman, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, has passed away, so where is he? He said: He is in Paradise. He said: Abu Bakr died, so where is he? He said: That tender-hearted one, at every good sought. He said: Umar died, so where is he? He said: When the righteous are mentioned, then welcome to Umar."
"8839","Abu Yazid al-Qaratis narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Qays ibn Muslim, from Tariq ibn Shihab, who said: Abdullah said: When the righteous are mentioned, then welcome to Umar."
"8840","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from Asim, from Zirr, from Abdullah, who said: When the righteous are mentioned, then welcome to Umar. His Islam was a victory, and his rule was a conquest. By Allah, I know nothing on earth but it felt the loss of Umar, even the thorny trees. By Allah, I think there was an angel between his eyes guiding him aright. By Allah, I think Satan feared him, lest he introduce an event into Islam and Umar refute him. By Allah, if I knew a dog that loved Umar, I would love it."
"8841","Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Umar ibn Aban narrated to me, Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad al-Muharibi narrated to us, from Raqabah ibn Masqalah al-Abdi, from Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, from Abdullah ibn Masud, who said: I loved Umar so much that I feared Allah; and if I knew a dog loved Umar, I would love it. I wished I were a servant to Umar, may Allah be pleased with him. Everything felt his loss, even the thorny trees. His emigration was a victory, and his authority was a mercy."
"8842","Yusuf al-Qadi narrated to us, Amr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Shubah told us, from Asim, from Abdullah, who said: If Umar loved a dog, it would be the most beloved of dogs to me."
"8843","Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, al-Masudi narrated to us, from al-Qasim, who said: Abdullah said: I feared Allah on account of my love for Umar, may Allah be pleased with him."
"8844","Abu Yazid al-Qaratis narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, who said: Abdullah said: When the righteous are mentioned, then welcome to Umar."
"8845","Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from Mansur, who said: Abdullah used to say: When the righteous are mentioned, then welcome to Umar; I wish I were a servant to the like of Umar until I die."
"8846","8819 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, al-Masudi narrated, from al-Qasim, he said: Abdullah said: When the righteous are mentioned, then hail Umar."
"8847","8820 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, Misar narrated, from al-Qasim, he said: Abdullah said: The Islam of Umar was indeed a victory, and his rule was indeed a mercy. By Allah, we were not able to pray at the House until Umar became Muslim; when Umar became Muslim, he confronted them until they left us alone, so we prayed."
"8848","Chapter. 8821 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr narrated to us, Muawiyah narrated, Zaidah narrated, from Ismail, from Qays, from Abdullah ibn Masud, he said: We remained mighty ever since Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, became Muslim."
"8849","8822 - Abu Yazid al-Qaratisiyy narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated, Marwan ibn Muawiyah narrated, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, he said: I heard Ibn Masud saying: We remained mighty ever since Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, became Muslim."
"8850","8823 - Aslam ibn Sahl al-Wasitiyy narrated to us, Zakariyya ibn Yahya Zahmawayh narrated, Ali ibn Hashim narrated, from al-Amash, from Shaqiq, from Abdullah, he said: We remained mighty ever since Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, became Muslim."
"8851","8824 - Abu Yazid al-Qaratisiyy narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated, al-Masudi narrated, Asim narrated, from Shaqiq, he said: Abdullah said: Satan met a man from among the companions of Muhammad, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and wrestled him; the Muslim threw him down and pinned him with his thumb. He said: Let me go and I will teach you a verse that none of us hears except that he turns away. So he released him, but he refused to teach him. He returned and wrestled him, and the Muslim threw him down and pinned him with his thumb. He said: Inform me of it, but he refused to teach him. When he returned the third time, he said: The verse in Surah al-Baqarah: {Allah, there is no god but He, the Ever-Living, the Sustainer} to its end. It was said to Abdullah: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, who was that man? He said: Who could it be but Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him."
"8852","8825 - Abu Yazid al-Qaratisiyy narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated, (H) and Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, al-Masudi narrated, from al-Qasim, he said: Abdullah said: I think Satan flees from the very presence of Umar."
"8853","8826 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, Abu Asim al-Thaqafiyy narrated, al-Shabiyy narrated to me, he said: Abdullah ibn Masud said: A man from among the companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, met a man from among the jinn and wrestled him, and the human threw him down. The jinni said to him: Wrestle me again. He wrestled him again and threw him down. The human said to him: I see you frail and emaciated, as if your forearms were the forearms of a dog; is that how you all are, O company of jinn - or are you among them like that? He said: No, by Allah, among them I am indeed sturdy, but wrestle me a third time, and if you throw me down I will teach you something that will benefit you. He wrestled him again and threw him down. He said: Come, teach me. He said: Do you recite Ayat al-Kursi? He said: Yes. He said: You will never recite it in a house except that Satan leaves it with a sound like the braying of a donkey, and he will not enter it until morning. A man from the people said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, who was that man from among the companions of Muhammad, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him? Abdullah frowned, turned to him, and said: Who could he be but Umar, may Allah be pleased with him?"
"8854","8827 - Ahmad ibn Zuhayr al-Tustariyy narrated to us, Mamar ibn Sahl narrated, Abu Ahmad al-Zubayriyy narrated, Sharik narrated, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Ubaydah, from Abdullah, he said: We would not think it unlikely that tranquility spoke upon the tongue of Umar."
"8855","8828 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdiyy narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated, al-Masudi narrated, from Abu Nahshal, from Abu Wail, he said: Abdullah said: Umar excelled people in four matters: by his mention of the captives on the Day of Badr, when he commanded that they be killed, so Allah Almighty revealed: {Had it not been for a prior decree from Allah, a great punishment would have touched you for what you took}; and by his mention of the hijab, when Zaynab said: You are jealous of us while revelation descends in our houses, so Allah Almighty revealed: {And when you ask them for anything, ask them from behind a hijab}; and by the supplication of the Prophet of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: O Allah, support Islam with Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him; and by his view concerning Abu Bakr, for he was the first of the people to pledge allegiance to him."
"8856","Chapter. 8829 - Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated, Sufyan narrated, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah, he said: The most perspicacious of people were three: the companion of Musa who said: {O my father, hire him; indeed the best you can hire is the strong, the trustworthy}; he said: And what did you see of his strength? She said: He came to the well over which was a rock that so-and-so could not move, and he lifted it. He said: And what did you see of his trustworthiness? She said: I was walking ahead of him and he placed me behind him; and the companion of Yusuf when he said: {Honor his stay; perhaps he will benefit us or we will take him as a son}; and Abu Bakr when he appointed Umar as successor."
"8857","8830 - Muhammad ibn Ali al-Saigh al-Makkiyy narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated, al-Ahwas narrated, from Abu Ishaq, some of the companions of Abdullah narrated to us, they said: Abdullah said: Who are the three most perspicacious? Then he mentioned similar to it."
"8858","Chapter. 8831 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, al-Masudi narrated, from al-Qasim, he said: Abdullah said: I think there is an angel between the eyes of Umar guiding him rightly."
"8859","8832 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, Sufyan narrated, from Wasil, from Abu Wail, he said: I never saw Umar except as if there were an angel between his eyes guiding him rightly."
"8860","8833 - Salamah ibn Ibrahim ibn Ismail ibn Yahya ibn Salamah ibn Kuhayl narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from his father, from his grandfather, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Abu Wail, from Abdullah, he said: I never saw Umar ibn al-Khattab except that it appeared to me that between his eyes there was an angel guiding him rightly."
"8861","8834 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdiyy narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated, Zaidah narrated, from al-Amash, from Shaqiq, from Abdullah, he said: Umar disliked prayer after Asr, and I dislike what Umar disliked."
"8862","Chapter. 8835 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdiyy narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated, Zaidah narrated, Asim ibn Abi al-Najud narrated, from Shaqiq, he said: When Umar was killed, Abdullah ibn Masud traveled to us for seven nights and addressed us. He said: The Commander of the Believers, Umar, was struck by Abu Luluah, the slave of al-Mughirah ibn Shubah, while he was in the Fajr prayer, and he killed him. Then he wept, and the people wept. Then he said: We, the companions of Muhammad, gathered and appointed the best of us, the foremost in merit."
"8863","8836 - Abu Yazid al-Qaratisiyy narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, from Asim ibn Bahdalah, from Abu Wail, that Ibn Masud traveled from Medina to Kufa when Uthman ibn Affan was made caliph. He praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: To proceed: The Commander of the Believers, Umar ibn al-Khattab, has died, and we never saw more sobbing than on that day. We, the companions of Muhammad, gathered and did not fall short of choosing the best of us, the foremost in merit, and we pledged allegiance to him. So pledge allegiance to the Commander of the Believers, Uthman ibn Affan."
"8864","8837 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshiyy narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Harb narrated, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated, from Abdullah ibn al-Mukhtar, from Asim, from Abu Wail, from Abdullah, similar to it."
"8865","8838 - Muadh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musaddad narrated, Yahya ibn Said narrated, from Misar, from Imran ibn Umayr, from Kulthum al-Khuzaiyy, he said: Abdullah said: It would not please me that I shot an arrow at Uthman and missed him - I think he said: intending to kill him - even if I had the like of Uhud in gold."
"8866","8839 - Ibrahim ibn Nailah al-Asbahaniyy narrated to us, Ismail ibn Amr al-Bajaliyy narrated, Misar narrated, from Abd al-Malik ibn Maysarah, from al-Nazzal ibn Sabrah, from Abdullah ibn Masud, similar to it."
"8867","8840 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdiyy narrated to us, Muawiyah narrated, Zaidah narrated, Sulayman narrated, from Abdullah ibn Sinan, he said: Abdullah came announcing the death of Umar and the succession of Uthman, and Abdullah said: By Allah, we did not fall short of choosing the highest of them in merit."
"8868","8841 - Abu Yazid al-Qaratisiyy narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated, Abu Yahya al-Himmaniyy narrated, from al-Amash, from Abdullah ibn Sinan, from Abdullah ibn Masud, he said: When Abdullah pledged allegiance to Uthman, Abdullah said: We did not fall short of choosing the highest of them in merit."
"8869","8842 - Ibrahim ibn Nailah al-Asbahaniyy narrated to us, Ismail ibn Amr al-Bajaliyy narrated, Misar ibn Kidam narrated, from Abd al-Malik ibn Maysarah, from al-Nazzal ibn Sabrah, he said: When Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, was made caliph, Abdullah ibn Masud said: We appointed the best of those who remained and did not fall short."
"8870","8843 - Abu Yazid al-Qaratisiyy narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated, Abu Yahya al-Himmaniyy narrated, from Misar, from Abd al-Malik ibn Maysarah, from al-Nazzal ibn Sabrah, he said: When Uthman was made caliph, Abdullah ibn Masud said: We appointed the best of those who remained and did not fall short."
"8871","8844 - Abu Yazid al-Qaratisiyy narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated, Muhammad ibn Talhah narrated, from al-Walid ibn Qays, that Ibn Masud ascended the minbar intending to announce the death of Umar, but sobbing choked him once - or twice - then he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab was a fortified fortress for Islam, entered into but not left; so the fortress has collapsed. Then he announced his death, then said: And Uthman ibn Affan was made successor, and we did not fall short of choosing the best of them in merit."
"8872","8845 - Al-Dabariyy narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Ibn Masud, he said: Hold fast to knowledge before it is taken away, and its taking away is the departure of its people. Hold fast to knowledge, for none of you knows when he will need what he has. Hold fast to knowledge, and beware of extremism and deep speculation. Hold fast to the ancient way, for there will come a people who recite the Book of Allah casting it behind their backs."
"8873","8846 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from al-Thawriyy, from al-Amash, from Abu Wail, from Ibn Masud, he said: O people, learn, for none of you knows when he will need it. A man came to him and said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, what do you think of a man who recites the Quran in reverse order? He said: That is an inverted heart. And a mushaf was brought that had been adorned and gilded, so Abdullah said: The best adornment of the mushaf is its recitation in truth."
"8874","8847 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abu Qilabah, from Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, that he used to say: O Allah, if You have written me among the wretched, then erase me and establish me among the fortunate."
"8875","8848 - Ishaq narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from Qatadah, that Ibn Masud would sometimes cite a verse of poetry from the accounts of the Arabs."
"8876","8849 - Muadh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musaddad narrated, Yahya ibn Said narrated, from Abu Hayyan al-Taymiyy, my father narrated to me, from al-Harith ibn Suwayd, he said: I heard Abdullah saying: There is no speech I utter before a ruler, warding off from myself with it two lashes of the whip, except that I would utter it."
"8877","8850 - Ishaq narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from al-Zuhriyy, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abdullah, he said: Ibn Masud said: A man narrates a hadith and one whose mind cannot grasp that hadith hears it, so it becomes a trial for him."
"8878","8851 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Mamar informed us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Ibn Masud, he said: There were two towns, one righteous and the other unjust. A man left the unjust town intending the righteous town, and death came to him where Allah willed. The angel and Satan disputed over him. Satan said: By Allah, he never disobeyed me at all. The angel said: He set out intending repentance. It was judged between them that they look to which of the two he was nearer; they found him nearer to the righteous town by a span, so he was forgiven. Mamar said: And I heard one saying: Allah brought the righteous town nearer to him."
"8879","8852 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from Abu Ishaq, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from Ibn Masud, he said: One who forbids the lawful is like one who permits the unlawful."
"8880","8853 - Uthman ibn Umar al-Dabbiyy narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Raja narrated, Israil narrated, from Abu Ishaq, he said: I was sitting with Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah when a man came asking him about his son al-Qasim. He said: He went early to the rubbish heap seeking lizards. He said: Do you eat it? Abd al-Rahman said: And who forbade it? I heard Abdullah ibn Masud saying: One who forbids the lawful is like one who permits the unlawful."
"8881","8854 - Ishaq narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah ibn Masud, he said: I leave nothing after me more beloved to me than camels that I give water to."
"8882","8855 - Ishaq narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from Qatadah, that Ibn Masud said: Allah Almighty will question every shepherd regarding what He entrusted to him: did he uphold the command of Allah concerning them or neglect it? Until a man will be questioned about the people of his house."
"8883","8856 - Ishaq narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from al-Amash, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: The Euphrates was complained of to Ibn Masud. They said: We fear it will burst upon us; if only you would send to it one who will dam it. Abdullah said: Do not dam it. By Allah, there will come upon people a time when, if they sought a basin of water, they would not find it. Every water will return to its origin, and water and the Muslims will be in Sham."
"8884","8857 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, al-Masudi narrated, from al-Qasim, he said: The Euphrates overflowed in the time of Abdullah and the people disliked that. Abdullah said: Do not dislike it, for soon there will come upon people a time when a basin of water will be sought and not found; that is when every water returns to its origin, and the remainder of water and the believers will be in Sham."
"8885","8858 - Ishaq narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from Abu Ishaq, from a man, from Ibn Masud, he said: When the year thirty-five comes, a grave matter will occur; if they perish, then it is likely, and if they are saved, then perhaps. And when the year seventy comes, you will see what you disapprove of."
"8886","8859 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, that Ibn Masud said: It is as if I see the Turks coming upon you on mules with bound ears, until they tie them at the bank of the Euphrates."
"8887","8860 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq narrated, Mamar informed us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Ibn Masud, he said: Part of faith is that a man loves his brother, loving him only for Allah and in Him."
"8888","8861 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazariyy, from Ziyad ibn Abi Maryam, or from Abu Ubaydah - Mamar doubted - he said: Ibn Masud saw beads on the neck of his wife that she had hung against redness, so he cut it and said: The family of Abdullah is free of any need for shirk."
"8889","8862 - Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, Musa ibn Dawud al-Dabbiyy narrated, Abu Israil al-Mulaiyy narrated, from Maysarah ibn Habib, from al-Minhal ibn Amr, from Abu Ubaydah, that Ibn Masud entered upon one of the mothers of his children and saw an amulet on her neck. He twisted the cord until he cut it and said: Is there shirk in my houses? Then he said: Amulets, incantations, and love-spells are shirk, or a part of shirk."
"8890","8863 - Umar ibn Hafs al-Sadusiyy narrated to us, Asim ibn Ali narrated, al-Masudi narrated, from al-Minhal ibn Amr, from Abu Ubaydah, from Abdullah, that he saw on the neck of a woman of his household a cord containing amulets. He pulled it with a strong pull until he cut the cord and said: The family of Abdullah is free of any need for shirk. Then he said: Love-spells, amulets, and incantations are indeed shirk. A woman said: One of us complains of her head and seeks incantation, and when she is treated she thinks it benefited her. Abdullah said: Satan comes to one of you and prods her head; when she seeks incantation he withdraws, and when she does not seek incantation he pricks. If one of you would call for water and sprinkle it on her head and face, then say: In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful, then recite: {Say: He is Allah, the One}, and {Say: I seek refuge with the Lord of the daybreak}, and {Say: I seek refuge with the Lord of mankind}, it would benefit her, if Allah wills."
"8891","8864 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Mamar informed us, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr ibn Maymun al-Awdiyy, from Ibn Masud, he said: The marrow of the shin of a woman from the houris will be seen from behind flesh and bone, from beneath seventy garments, as a red drink is seen in a white glass vessel."
"8892","8865 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Mamar informed us, from al-Amash, from a man, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Ibn Masud, he said: Sudden death is a relief for the believer and a regret for the disbeliever."
"8893","8866 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Arim Abu al-Numan narrated, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated, from Asim, from Abu Wail, from Abdullah, he said: The soul of the believer leaves with sweat, and the soul of the disbeliever leaves in his cheek as the soul of a donkey leaves."
"8894","8867 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: When Ibn Masud wanted to enter a town, he said: O Allah, Lord of the heavens and what they shade, Lord of the devils and what they mislead, Lord of the winds and what they scatter, I ask You for its good and the good within it, and I seek refuge with You from its evil and the evil within it."
"8895","8868 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated, Zaidah narrated, from al-Amash, from Shaqiq, he said: Abdullah rarely fasted. That was mentioned to him, and he said: When I fast I become weak for recitation, and recitation of the Quran is more beloved to me."
"8896","8869 - Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisiyy and Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated, they said: Shubah narrated, from Abu Ishaq, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from Abdullah, that he hardly ever fasted. He said: When I fast I become weak for prayer, and prayer is more beloved to me than fasting. And if he fasted, he fasted three days of the month."
"8897","8870 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from al-Thawriyy, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, he said: Abdullah rarely fasted. We said to him: You rarely fast. He said: When I fast I become weak for prayer, and prayer is more beloved to me than fasting."
"8898","8871 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, Bukayr ibn Amir narrated, from al-Shabiyy, from Ibn Masud, he said: Prayer is more beloved to me than fasting, and he would not pray Duha."
"8899","8872 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, from Abu Hamzah, from Ibrahim, from Ibn Masud, that it was said to him: You rarely fast. He said: Yes; when I fast I become weak for prayer, and prayer is more beloved to me than fasting."
"8900","8873 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn Uyaynah, a shaykh from Bajilah informed me, he said: I heard al-Shabiyy saying: Abdullah ibn Masud would not pray Duha, and he would pray between Zuhr and Asr along with a long portion of the night."
"8901","8874 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from al-Thawriyy, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazariyy, from Abu Ubaydah, from his mother, she said: I never saw Abdullah ibn Masud fasting at all except Ramadan. He said: I do not know what is the matter with those two days."
"8902","8875 - Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Abu al-Walid and Ibn Kathir narrated, from Shubah, from Abu Ishaq, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from Abdullah, that he hardly ever fasted. He said: When I fast I become weak for prayer, and prayer is more beloved to me than fasting; and if he fasted, he fasted three days of the month."
"8903","8876 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshiyy narrated to us, Ismah ibn Sulayman al-Khazzaz narrated, Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated, from Mujalid, from al-Shabiyy, from his uncle, he said: I frequented Ibn Masud for a year and never saw him fasting a single day except in Ramadan, and he would drink strong nabidh from a green jar."
"8904","8877 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Arim Abu al-Numan narrated, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated, from Mujalid, from al-Shabiyy, from Qays ibn Abd, he said: I frequented Ibn Masud for a year and never saw him praying Duha, and never saw him fasting voluntarily except the day of Ashura."
"8905","8878 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn Uyaynah, from Mujalid, from al-Shabiyy, from Qays ibn Abd, he said: I frequented Ibn Masud, then he mentioned similar to the hadith of Hammad ibn Zayd."
"8906","8879 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from al-Thawriyy, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, he said: We were with Abdullah when a drink was brought. He said: Hand it to the people. They said: We are fasting. He said: But I am not fasting. Then he drank and recited: {They fear a Day in which hearts and eyes will turn about}."
"8907","8880 - Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Abu al-Walid narrated, Shubah narrated, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Aswad, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn Masud saying: The righteous go away one after another, and there remain the people of doubt who neither recognize good nor denounce evil."
"8908","8881 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, al-Amash narrated, from Abdullah ibn Dirar al-Asadiyy, from his father, from Abdullah, he said: Allah Almighty divided good and made it ten parts; He placed nine parts in Sham and the remainder in the rest of the earth. And He divided evil and made it ten parts; He placed one part of it in Sham and the remainder in the rest of the earth."
"8909","8882 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz and Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, they said: Abu Nuaym narrated, al-Amash narrated, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah, he said: Whoever loves meeting Allah, Allah loves meeting him; whoever hates meeting Allah, Allah hates meeting him; and death is before meeting Allah."
"8910","8883 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, al-Amash narrated, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah, he said: When one of you seeks a need, let him seek it secretly, for he will only have what was decreed for him. And let none of you come to his companion and praise him, breaking his back."
"8911","8884 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, al-Amash narrated, from Malik ibn al-Harith, from Abdullah ibn Rabiah, he said: We were sitting with Abdullah when the people mentioned a man and mentioned his character. Abdullah said: Tell me, if you cut off his head, could you restore it? They said: No. He said: Then his hand? They said: No. He said: Then his leg? They said: No. He said: You will not be able to change his character until you change his creation. The nutfah settles in the womb for forty nights, then descends as blood, then becomes a clot, then becomes a lump of flesh; then the angel is sent to him and writes his provision, his character, and whether wretched or fortunate."
"8912","8885 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr narrated to us, Muawiyah narrated, Zaidah narrated, from al-Amash, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Abdullah ibn Rabiah, he said: We were with Abdullah. He said: They mentioned a man and mentioned his character. The people said: Does he have no one to restrain him? Abdullah said: Tell me, if his head were cut off, could you make a head for him? Or if his hands were cut off, could you make hands for him? Or if his leg were cut off, could you make a leg for him? They said: No. Abdullah said: The nutfah, when it falls into the woman, remains forty days, then descends as blood, then becomes a clot, then becomes a lump of flesh likewise; then Allah sends the angel and says: Write his term, his deed, his provision, his trace, and whether wretched or fortunate. You will not be able to change his character until you change his creation."
"8913","8886 - Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ishaq al-Saylahiniyy narrated, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, from Abu Abd al-Salam, from Abdullah ibn Mikraz, or Ubayd Allah ibn Mikraz, he said: Abdullah ibn Masud said: Your Lord, the Exalted, has with Him neither night nor day. The light of the heavens and the earth is from the light of His Face. The measure of each day of your days with Him is twelve hours. Your deeds of yesterday are presented to Him at the beginning of today. He looks into them for three hours and sees therein what He dislikes, and that angers Him. The first to know His anger are the bearers of the Throne, praising Him, and it weighs upon them; so the bearers of the Throne, those around the Throne, the angels brought near, and the rest of the angels glorify Him. Then Jibril, peace be upon him, blows the horn, and nothing remains except that it hears his voice, and they glorify the Most Gracious Almighty for three hours, until the Most Gracious fills with mercy; that is six hours. Then the wombs are brought and He looks into them for three hours; that is His saying in His Book: {It is He who forms you in the wombs as He wills} {He grants to whom He wills females and grants to whom He wills males * or He makes them both males and females, and makes whom He wills barren; indeed He is Knowing, Powerful}. That is nine hours. Then provisions are brought and He looks into them for three hours; His saying in His Book: {He extends provision to whom He wills and restricts} {Every day He is engaged in a matter}. He said: This is part of your affair and the affair of your Lord."
"8914","8887 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshiyy narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Raja narrated, al-Masudi informed us, from Awn, that Abdullah used to say: Servants remain under the respite of the cover of Allah Almighty so long as they uphold worship and do not shed unlawful blood."
"8915","8888 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshiyy narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Raja narrated, al-Masudi informed us, from Awn, he said: I said to Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz: Ibn Masud used to say: There will be ambiguous matters; whoever is pleased with them among those absent from them is like one who witnessed them, and whoever hates them among those who witnessed them is like one who was absent from them. So it pleased him."
"8916","8889 - Abu Muslim narrated to us, Abdullah narrated, al-Masudi narrated, from Awn, he said: When Abdullah left his house, he said: In the name of Allah, I place my trust in Allah; there is no power nor strength except with Allah. Muhammad ibn Kab al-Quraziyy said: This is in the Quran: {Embark therein in the name of Allah} and He said: {Upon Allah we have placed our trust}."
"8917","8890 - Abu Muslim narrated to us, Abdullah narrated, al-Masudi narrated, from Awn, he said: Abdullah said: Do not swear by the oath of Satan, that one of you says: By the might of Allah; but say as Allah said: Allah, Lord of might."
"8918","8891 - Abu Muslim narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Raja narrated, al-Masudi narrated, from Awn, he said: A man said to Abdullah: I fear I am a hypocrite. He said: Had you been a hypocrite, you would not have feared that."
"8919","8892 - Abu Muslim narrated to us, Abdullah narrated, al-Masudi narrated, from Awn, he said: When news of the death of Utbah came to Abdullah, he wept. It was said to him: You weep? He said: He was my brother by lineage and my companion with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him; yet with that I would not like to have been before him, for him to die so that I count him as reward is more beloved to me than that I die so that he counts me as reward."
"8920","8893 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshiyy narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Raja narrated, al-Masudi informed us, from Awn, that Ibn Masud used to say: O Originator with no beginning for You, O Eternal with no end for You, O Living One who gives life to the dead, You are the Maintainer of every soul for what it earned."
"8921","8894 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated, al-Masudi narrated, from Awn, he said: Abdullah said: Whoever has a handsome appearance, is in a position that does not disgrace him, is given ample provision, then humbles himself, is among the sincerely devoted of Allah."
"8922","8895 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, Sufyan narrated, from Abu Sinan, from Abdullah ibn Abi al-Hudhayl ibn Sulaym ibn Hanzalah, that Abdullah came to the entrance of the market and said: O Allah, I ask You for its good and the good of its people, and I seek refuge with You from its evil and the evil of its people."
"8923","8896 - Abu Khalifah al-Fadl ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated, Husayn ibn Mani al-Sadusiyy narrated, he said: I heard Abu Muhammad al-Nahdiyy saying: Abdullah ibn Masud said: People are of three types, and besides them there is no good: a man who sees a group fighting in the path of Allah and strives with himself and his wealth; a man who strives with his tongue, enjoins good and forbids evil; and a man who knows the truth in his heart."
"8924","8897 - Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated, Husayn ibn Mani al-Sadusiyy narrated, he said: I heard Abu Muhammad al-Nahdiyy saying: Ibn Masud said: Guarantee for me deliberate acts and I will guarantee for you mistakes."
"8925","8898 - Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, Yazid ibn Mihran Abu Khalid al-Khabbaz narrated, Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated, from Ibn Husayn, from al-Mustawrid ibn al-Ahnaf, from Maqil ibn Amir al-Asadiyy, he said: Abdullah passed by me while I was selling goods of mine and swearing over them. He began raising over my head something in his hand, saying: He says: Do not swear, for an oath promotes the goods but erases blessing."
"8926","8899 - Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ishaq al-Saylahiniyy narrated, Abu Awanah narrated, from Hilal al-Wazzan, from Abdullah ibn Ukaym, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn Masud in this mosque beginning with an oath before speech. He said: There is none among you except that his Lord will be alone with him as one of you is alone with the moon on the night of full moon. He will say: Son of Adam, what deceived you concerning Me? Son of Adam, what deceived you concerning Me? Son of Adam, what answer did you give the messengers? Son of Adam, what did you do with what you knew? Son of Adam, what did you do with what you knew? Son of Adam, what did you do with what you knew?"
"8927","8900 - Abu Yazid al-Qaratisiyy narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated, Sharik narrated, from Hilal al-Wazzan, from Abdullah ibn Ukaym, he said: I heard Ibn Masud beginning with an oath before the hadith. He said: By Allah, there is none among you except that Allah will be alone with him on the Day of Resurrection as one of you is alone with the moon on the night of full moon. He will say: What deceived you concerning Me, son of Adam? What deceived you concerning Me, son of Adam? What deceived you concerning Me, son of Adam? What did you do with what you knew? Son of Adam, what answer did you give the messengers?"
"8928","8901 - Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ishaq al-Saylahiniyy narrated, Mahdi ibn Maymun narrated, from Wasil al-Ahdab, from Abu Wail, he said: I asked Ibn Masud one day after we finished the morning prayer, and we sought permission to enter upon him. He said: Enter. We said: We will wait a while, perhaps someone of the household has a need. He turned to glorifying Allah. He said: You thought the family of Abdullah heedless. Then he said: O maidservant, see whether the sun has risen? She said: No. Then he said to her a second time: See whether the sun has risen? She said: No. Then he said to her a third time: See whether the sun has risen? She said: Yes. He said: Praise be to Allah who has granted us this day and pardoned our slips therein - and I think he said: and did not punish us with the Fire."
"8929","8902 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, (H) and Abu Muslim al-Kashshiyy narrated to us, al-Hakam ibn Marwan al-Darir narrated, they said: Misar ibn Kidam narrated, from Wabarah ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: Abdullah said: That I swear by Allah falsely is more beloved to me than that I swear by other than Him while truthful."
"8930","8903 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshiyy narrated to us, Mamar ibn Abdullah al-Ansariyy narrated, al-Masudi narrated, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abdullah ibn Masud, he said: The height of Musa, peace be upon him, was twelve cubits, his staff was twelve cubits, and his leap was twelve; then he struck Uj ibn Anaq, and struck nothing of him except his ankle."
"8931","8904 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshiyy narrated to us, Ismah ibn Sulayman al-Khazzaz al-Kufiyy narrated, Muhammad ibn Talhah ibn Musarrif narrated, from his father, from Yazid ibn Wahb, he said: Abdullah ibn Masud said: Two men who have entered Islam do not boycott each other except that one of them leaves it until he returns; and his return is that he comes to him and greets him with peace."
"8932","8905 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshiyy narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Khattab narrated, Qays ibn al-Rabi narrated, from Layth, from Abu Qays, from Huzayl ibn Shurahbil, from Abdullah ibn Masud, he said: The souls of the martyrs are in the bellies of green birds, roaming in Paradise wherever they wish, then sheltering in lamps suspended from the Throne."
"8933","8906 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshiyy narrated to us, Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated, Abu Bakr al-Hudhaliyy narrated, from al-Shabiyy, from Masruq, he said: Ibn Masud went out to the people of the house and said to them: Whoever among you comes seeking a ruling, let him sit until we give him a ruling; whoever among you comes disputing, let him stay with his opponent until we judge between them; whoever among you comes revealing to us a private sin that Allah has covered, let him cover himself with the cover of Allah and confide it to the One who owns its forgiveness, for we do not own its forgiveness, and we will enforce a hadd upon him and its consequences."
"8934","8907 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, Sufyan narrated, from Mansur, from Abu al-Duha, from Masruq, he said: An udder was brought to Abdullah and a man withdrew. He said: I have forbidden it to myself. Abdullah said: {O you who believe, do not forbid the good things that Allah has made lawful for you}. Eat and expiate."
"8935","8908 - Muhammad ibn Ali al-Saigh narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated, Jarir narrated, from Mansur, from Abu al-Duha, from Masruq, he said: An udder was brought to Abdullah and he began eating from it. He said to the people: Come near. The people came near and a man among them withdrew. Abdullah said: What is the matter with you? He said: I have forbidden the udder to myself. He said: This is from the footsteps of Satan. Come near, eat, and expiate your oath. Then he recited: {O you who believe, do not forbid the good things that Allah has made lawful for you}."
"8936","8909 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, Sufyan narrated, from Mansur, from Malik ibn al-Harith, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from Abdullah, he said: Every trait the believer may be folded upon except treachery and lying."
"8937","8910 - Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisiyy and Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated, they said: Shubah narrated, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, that a man came to Abdullah and said: My brother is ill, complaining of his stomach, and wine was prescribed for him; shall I give him to drink? Abdullah said: Glory be to Allah; Allah has not placed healing in filth. Healing is only in two things: honey is a healing for people, and the Quran is a healing for what is in the breasts."
"8938","8911 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, Sufyan narrated, from Man ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from Abdullah ibn Masud, he said: People are two who go out in the morning: one selling himself and destroying it, and one ransoming it and freeing it. Charity is a proof, fasting is a shield, prayer is a light, tranquility is a gain and abandoning it is a loss."
"8939","8912 - Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated, Shubah narrated, from Abu Ishaq, from Murrah, from Abdullah, he said: Souls are marshalled troops; those of them that recognize one another unite, and those that do not recognize one another differ."
"8940","8913 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, Sufyan narrated, from Asim, from Zirr, from Abdullah, he said: Allah indeed supports the religion with a wicked man."
"8941","8914 - Muhammad ibn Hayyan al-Maziniyy narrated to us, Amr ibn Marzuq narrated, Shubah informed us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah, he said: Women are but private parts; a woman leaves her house with nothing wrong in her, yet Satan looks out for her and says: You will not pass by anyone except that you please him. A woman puts on her clothes and it is said: Where do you intend? She says: I visit a sick person, or attend a funeral, or pray in a mosque; yet a woman has never worshipped her Lord like worshipping Him in her house."
"8942","8915 - Muhammad ibn Hayyan al-Maziniyy narrated to us, Amr ibn Marzuq narrated, Shubah informed us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah, he said: When you are three on a journey, appoint one of you over you, and two should not converse privately excluding their companion."
"8943","8916 - Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Abu al-Walid and Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated, they said: Shubah narrated, (H) and Muhammad ibn Hayyan al-Maziniyy narrated to us, Amr ibn Marzuq narrated, Shubah informed us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, he said: Asma ibn Kharijah boasted against a man, saying: I am the son of noble elders. Abdullah said: That is Yusuf ibn Yaqub ibn Ishaq, the sacrificed of Allah, son of Ibrahim, the intimate friend of Allah."
"8944","8917 - Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisiyy narrated, Shubah narrated, he said: Abu Ishaq informed us, he said: I heard Abu al-Ahwas saying: I heard Abdullah supplicating with this supplication: O Allah, I ask You by Your ample blessing with which You blessed me, by Your trial with which You tried me, and by Your bounty with which You favored me, to admit me into Paradise. O Allah, admit me into Paradise by Your bounty, Your favor, and Your mercy."
"8945","8918 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshiyy narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Raja narrated, al-Masudi narrated, (H) and Umar ibn Hafs al-Sadusiyy narrated to us, Asim ibn Ali narrated, al-Masudi narrated, from Awn ibn Abdullah, from Abu Fakhita, from al-Aswad ibn Yazid, he said: Abdullah recited: {Except one who has taken with the Most Gracious a covenant}. He said: Allah will say on the Day of Resurrection: Whoever has a covenant with Me, let him stand. They said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, teach us. He said: Say: O Allah, Originator of the heavens and the earth, Knower of the unseen and the witnessed, I entrust to You in this worldly life that if You leave me to myself, You bring me near evil and distance me from good, and I trust in nothing but Your mercy; so make it for me a covenant with You that You will fulfill to me on the Day of Resurrection, for You never break the promise. He said: And Zakariyya Abu Yahya added in it, from al-Qasim: Fearful, seeking refuge, seeking forgiveness, desiring You."
"8946","8919 - Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated, Sufyan narrated, from Abu Ishaq, from Hubayrah ibn Yarim, from Abdullah, he said: Judge people by their close companions."
"8947","8920 - Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated, Sufyan narrated, from al-Amash, from Umarah ibn Umayr, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, and sometimes he said: from Hurayth ibn Zuhayr, he said: Abdullah ibn Masud said: O people, a time has passed over us when we did not judge, nor were we in such a position, and Allah has brought us to what you see. Whoever among you is presented with a judgment after today, let him judge therein by what is in the Book of Allah. If there comes to him a matter not in the Book of Allah, let him judge therein by what the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, judged. If there comes to him a matter not in the Book of Allah and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, did not judge concerning it, let him judge by what the righteous judged. If there comes to him a matter not in the Book of Allah, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, did not judge concerning it, and the righteous did not judge concerning it, let him strive, and let none of you say: I fear, and I think; for the lawful is clear and the unlawful is clear, and between them are ambiguous matters, so leave what makes you doubt for what does not make you doubt."
"8948","8921 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, al-Masudi narrated, from al-Qasim, he said: Abdullah said: When a matter you must decide comes to you, look at what is in the Book of Allah and judge by it; if you are unable, then at what the Messenger, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, judged; if you are unable, then at what the righteous judged; if you are unable, then take counsel and do not fall short; if you are unable, then acknowledge, and do not be ashamed."
"8949","8922 - Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated, Sufyan narrated, from al-Amash, from Umarah ibn Umayr, from Abu Mamar, from Amr ibn Shurahbil, from Abdullah, he said: Pain is not recorded as reward; reward is only in deeds, but Allah expiates sins by it."
"8950","8923 - Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated, Sufyan narrated, from al-Amash, from Abu Wail, from Abdullah, he said: One who gives people rulings in everything they seek rulings about is mad."
"8951","8924 - Yusuf al-Qadi narrated to us, Hafs ibn Umar al-Hawdiyy narrated, (H) and Ahmad ibn Amr al-Qatiraniyy narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Harb narrated, they said: Shubah narrated, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit and Sulayman al-Amash, from Abu Wail, he said: Abdullah said: Whoever gives people rulings in everything they ask him about is mad. This is the wording of Sulayman ibn Harb, and al-Hawdiyy said in his hadith: In everything they seek his ruling about."
"8952","8925 - Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated, Qurrah ibn Khalid narrated, Awn ibn Abdullah narrated, he said: Abdullah ibn Masud said: What a fine gathering is the gathering in which wisdom is spread."
"8953","8926 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, al-Masudi narrated, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: Abdullah ibn Masud said: Learn the shares of inheritance, for soon a man may need knowledge he once knew, or remain among a people who do not know."
"8954","8927 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, al-Masudi narrated, from al-Qasim, he said: Abdullah said: Fear of Allah suffices as knowledge, and being deceived by Allah suffices as ignorance."
"8955","8928 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, al-Masudi narrated, from al-Qasim, he said: Abdullah said: When a ruler is appointed, he has two inner circles from his people: an inner circle commanding him to obedience of Allah, and an inner circle commanding him to disobedience; and he is with whichever of them he obeys."
"8956","8929 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, al-Masudi narrated, from al-Qasim, he said: Abdullah said: Do not hasten to praise people nor to blame them, for you - or perhaps - may see from your brother today something that pleases you, yet it may displease you tomorrow; and perhaps you see from him today something that displeases you, yet it may please you tomorrow. People reproach, while only Allah forgives sins on the Day of Resurrection. Allah is more merciful to His servant on the day he meets Him than a single mother who spreads for him on shaded ground, then feels him: if there were a thorn, it would be in her before him, and if there were a sting, it would be in her before him."
"8957","8930 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, al-Masudi narrated, from al-Qasim, Abdullah said: I think a man forgets knowledge he knows because of a sin he commits."
"8958","8931 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, al-Masudi narrated, from al-Qasim, he said: Abdullah said: Let none of you say: O Allah, I seek refuge with You from trial, for there is none among you except that he encompasses trial; but whoever seeks refuge, let him seek refuge from its perplexing trials, for Allah Almighty says: {Your wealth and your children are but a trial}."
"8959","8932 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, al-Masudi narrated, from al-Qasim, he said: Abdullah said: Let none of you say: I am one who has never performed Hajj, for a Muslim is not one who has never performed Hajj; and let none of you say: I am a pilgrim, for the pilgrim is only the one in ihram; but let him say: I intend Makkah."
"8960","8933 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from al-Thawriyy, from Jabir, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Ibn Masud, he said: There is no hadd except in two cases: a man denied from his father, or one who accuses a chaste woman."
"8961","8934 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah, from al-Qasim, from Ibn Masud, similar to it."
"8962","8935 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, al-Masudi narrated, from al-Qasim, he said: Abdullah said: There is no hadd except in two cases: that he accuses a chaste woman, or a man is denied from his father."
"8963","8936 - Fudayl ibn Muhammad al-Malatiyy narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, Abu al-Umays narrated, from al-Qasim, he said: Abdullah said: There is no hadd except in two cases. Then he mentioned similar to it."
"8964","8937 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, al-Masudi narrated, from al-Qasim, he said: Abdullah said: Repentance is offered to the son of Adam if he accepts it, so long as one of three has not emerged: so long as the sun has not risen from its west, or the Beast has not emerged, or Yajuj and Majuj have not emerged."
"8965","8938 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, al-Masudi narrated, from al-Qasim, he said: It was said to Abdullah: Allah Almighty mentions prayer abundantly in the Quran: {Those who are constant in their prayer} {And those who guard their prayer}. Abdullah said: That is concerning its appointed times. They said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, we thought that meant abandoning it. Abdullah said: Abandoning it is disbelief."
"8966","8939 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, from Abd al-Rahman al-Masudi, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Ibn Masud, he said: Whoever abandons prayer has disbelieved."
"8967","8940 - Abu Yazid al-Qaratisiyy narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated, al-Masudi narrated, from al-Qasim, from Abdullah, and al-Hasan ibn Sad, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah, he said: It was said to Abdullah: Allah Almighty mentions prayer abundantly: {Those who are constant in their prayer} {And those who guard their prayer}. Abdullah said: That is concerning its appointed times. We said: We thought it meant only abandoning it. He said: Abandoning it is disbelief."
"8968","8941 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, Sufyan narrated, from Asim, from Zirr, from Abdullah, he said: Whoever does not pray has no religion."
"8969","8942 - Abu Yazid al-Qaratisiyy narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated, Shayban Abu Muawiyah narrated, from Asim, from Zirr, from Abdullah, he said: Whoever does not pray has no religion."
"8970","8943 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, al-Masudi narrated, from al-Qasim, or from Amr ibn Murrah, that Abdullah went out to the dawn prayer while people had leaned their backs against the qiblah of the mosque. He said: Move back like this, away from the faces of the angels."
"8971","8944 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Hajjaj narrated, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, from Abd al-Rahman al-Masudi, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Ibn Masud, that he saw people who had leaned their backs against the qiblah between the Fajr adhan and the iqamah. He said: Do not come between the angels and their prayer."
"8972","8945 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from al-Thawriyy and Mamar, from al-Amash, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, he said: Abdullah came at dawn while people were leaning their backs against the qiblah. He said: Move back from the qiblah; do not come between the angels and the qiblah, for it is the prayer of the angels."
"8973","8946 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdiyy narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated, Zaidah narrated, from al-Amash, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, he said: Abdullah came while people were leaning their backs toward the qiblah, then he mentioned similar to the hadith of al-Thawriyy and Mamar."
"8974","8947 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, al-Masudi narrated, from al-Qasim, he said: Abdullah said: Ward off flogging and killing from the servants of Allah as much as you can."
"8975","8948 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, al-Masudi narrated, from al-Qasim, he said: Abdullah said: Two months are fifty-nine days."
"8976","8949 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, al-Masudi narrated, from al-Qasim, he said: Abdullah arrived while Sad had built the palace and taken a mosque among the sellers of dates. He used to go out to it for the prayers. When Abdullah took charge of the treasury, the treasury was broken into and he seized the man. Abdullah wrote to Umar, and Umar wrote: Do not cut him; move the mosque and place the treasury toward the qiblah, for there will always be in the mosque one who prays. So Abdullah moved it and laid out this plan. The palace that Sad built had a terrace upon which the imam would stand. So Abdullah ordered it demolished until the standing place of the imam became level with the people."
"8977","8950 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, al-Masudi narrated, from al-Qasim, that Abdullah lent money to maternal uncles of his from Banu Sad. When their grants came out, they chose for him their wealth. When it was brought, Abdullah said: This is better than our wealth which we gave you; so gather your grants and give us from their goods."
"8978","8951 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, al-Masudi narrated, from al-Qasim, he said: Abdullah said: By the One besides whom there is no god, Allah distributed this fay on the tongue of Muhammad, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, before Persia and Rome were conquered."
"8979","8952 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, al-Masudi narrated, from al-Qasim, he said: Abdullah said: Four matters have been settled: creation, character, provision, and term."
"8980","8953 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated, Mutamir narrated, he said: I heard Isa ibn al-Musayyab narrating that he heard al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Masud narrating from his father, from his grandfather Abdullah ibn Masud, he said: Four matters have been settled: creation, character, provision, and term; none of us earns on behalf of another. And he said: Charity is valid whether possessed or not possessed."
"8981","8954 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, al-Masudi narrated, from al-Qasim, he said: Abdullah had a Christian scribe. Abdullah entered the palace, the Christian turned back, and Abdullah followed him with greetings of peace."
"8982","8955 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, al-Masudi narrated, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Tamim ibn Salamah, he said: Some people of shirk walked with Abdullah; when he reached the gate of the palace, he greeted them with peace."
"8983","8956 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn Uyaynah, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, he said: A man came to Ibn Masud and said: I passed by a mosque of Banu Hanifah and heard them reciting something Allah did not reveal: The grinders grinding, the kneaders kneading, the bakers baking, the servers serving. Ibn Masud brought forward Ibn al-Nawwahah, their imam, and killed him, and deemed the rest numerous. He said: I will not let Satan slaughter them today; dispatch them to Sham until Allah grants them repentance or the plague annihilates them. He said: And Ismail informed me, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, that Ibn Masud said: This one - meaning Ibn al-Nawwahah - came to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and Musaylamah sent him to him. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Had I been one to kill an envoy, I would have killed him."
"8984","8957 - Al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfatiyy and Abu Khalifah narrated to us, they said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated, Sufyan narrated, from Abu Ishaq, from Harithah ibn Mudarrib, that he came to Abdullah ibn Masud and said: There is no enmity between me and any of the Arabs, yet I passed by a mosque of Banu Hanifah and they believe in Musaylamah. Abdullah sent to them and they were brought. He asked them to repent except Ibn al-Nawwahah. He said to him: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying: Were it not that you are an envoy, I would strike your neck; today you are not an envoy. He ordered Qarazah ibn Kab and he struck his neck in the market. Then he said: Whoever wishes to look at Ibn al-Nawwahah slain in the market."
"8985","8958 - Bakr ibn Sahl al-Dimyatiyy narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated, Abu Muawiyah narrated, from al-Amash, from Harithah ibn Mudarrib, he said: Abdullah said to Ibn al-Nawwahah: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying: Were it not that you are an envoy, I would have killed you; as for today, you are not an envoy. O Harashah, stand and strike his neck. So he stood to him and struck his neck."
"8986","8959 - Al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustariyy narrated to us, Yahya al-Himmaniyy narrated, Qays ibn al-Rabi narrated, from Abu Ishaq, from Harithah ibn Mudarrib, from Abdullah ibn Masud, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to Ibn al-Nawwahah: Do you testify that I am the Messenger of Allah? He said: Do you testify that Musaylamah is the Messenger of Allah? The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Were it not that you are an envoy, I would have killed you."
"8987","8960 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, al-Masudi narrated, from al-Qasim, he said: Abdullah was approached and it was said to him: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, here are people reciting the recitation of Musaylamah. Abdullah turned him back and remained as long as Allah willed him to remain. Then he came to him and said: By the One I swear by, O Abu Abd al-Rahman, I have just left them in a house, and that mushaf is with them. He ordered Qarazah ibn Kab, so he marched with the people with him. He said: Bring them. When he brought them, Abdullah said: What is this after the spread of Islam? They said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, we seek Allahs forgiveness and repent to Him, and we testify that Musaylamah is the liar who fabricated against Allah and His Messenger. Abdullah asked them to repent and dispatched them to Sham, and they were nearly eighty men. Ibn al-Nawwahah refused to repent, so he ordered Qarazah ibn Kab, who brought him out to the market and struck his neck, and ordered him to take his head and cast it in his mothers lap. Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah said: I met an elderly shaykh among them later in Sham, and he said: May Allah have mercy on your father; by Allah, had he killed us that day, we would all have entered the Fire."
"8988","8961 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, al-Masudi narrated, from al-Qasim, he said: The first to spread the Quran from the mouth of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was Abdullah ibn Masud; the first to build a mosque in which prayer was offered was Ammar ibn Yasir; the first to call the adhan was Bilal; the first whose horse went out early with him in the path of Allah was al-Miqdad ibn al-Aswad; the first to shoot in the path of Allah was Sad; the first of the Muslims killed on the Day of Badr was Mihja, the freed slave of Umar ibn al-Khattab; the first tribe to ally with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was Juhaynah; and the first to willingly pay charity of their own accord were Banu Udhrah ibn Sad."
"8989","8962 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, Sharik narrated, from Ashath ibn Sulaym, from Abdullah ibn Ziyad al-Asadiyy, he said: I stood beside Abdullah in Zuhr or Asr and heard him reciting."
"8990","8963 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, Abd al-Salam ibn Harb narrated, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, one who saw Ibn Masud narrated to me, he said: It is as if I look at him while prostrating, parting his elbows until I could almost see the whiteness of his armpits."
"8991","8964 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, Abd al-Salam ibn Harb narrated, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Murrah, he said: I entered the mosque and said: I shall pray at every pillar. I came to Abdullah while he was sitting, and his companions were with him discussing my matter, and he said: If he knew that his Lord is at the first pillar, he would not leave it until he finished his prayer."
"8992","8965 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Murrah al-Hamdani, he said: I resolved to pray behind every pillar in the mosque of Kufa. While I was praying, Ibn Masud came to us in the mosque, so I went to him to tell him of my affair, but a man beat me to him and told him what I do. Ibn Masud said: If this one knew that Allah, Exalted and Mighty, is at the nearest pillar, he would not pass beyond it until he finished his prayer."
"8993","8966 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, Abd al-Salam ibn Harb narrated, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Abu Abdillah al-Barrad, he said: I was with Abdullah, and he said: For me to step on a live coal is dearer to me than to step on the grave of a Muslim."
"8994","8967 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, Abd al-Salam ibn Harb narrated, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, from Abdullah, he said: The wedding feast on the first day is a right, on the second day a bounty, and on the third day show and repute; and whoever makes a show, Allah will make a show of him."
"8995","8968 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, Abd al-Salam ibn Harb narrated, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Abu al-Bakhtari, he said: There was a dispute between a man from among the companions of Abdullah and his wife. She said: Your condiment and your family's condiment is nothing but the milk of my ewe. So he swore he would not eat any of its milk. A guest came to them and she served him with the milk of her ewe. The man said: You knew I do not eat it. The woman said: By Allah, if you do not eat, I shall not eat. The guest said: By Allah, if you two do not eat, I shall not eat. So they spent the night without supper. The report reached Abdullah, and he came to Abdullah. Abdullah said to him: What came between you and your family? He said: It was not divorce, nor zihar, nor ila. Then he told him the story. Abdullah said to him: I adjure you, when you return to your family, that the first thing you do be to eat of the milk of this ewe; and I consider it better for your soul that you expiate your oath."
"8996","8969 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, Abd al-Salam ibn Harb narrated, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn Masud saying: Allah, Exalted and Mighty, did not send down any disease except that He sent down for it a cure, except death."
"8997","8970 - Muhammad ibn Ali al-Saigh al-Makki narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated, Marwan ibn Muawiyah narrated, al-Hasan ibn Amr al-Fuqaymi narrated, from Yahya ibn Hani al-Muradi, from al-Harith ibn Qays, he said: Abdullah ibn Masud said to me: O Har ibn Qays, would it not please you to dwell in the middle of Paradise? I said: Yes. He said: Then cling to the community of the people."
"8998","8971 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Arim Abu al-Numan narrated, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated, from Mujalid, from al-Shabi, from Thabit ibn Qutbah al-Muzani, he said: Abdullah ibn Masud once addressed us with a sermon the like of which he had never addressed us before nor after. He said: O people, fear Allah, and hold to obedience and the community, for they are the rope of Allah which He commanded; and what you dislike in obedience and the community is better than what you love in division. Allah did not create anything of this world except that He set for it an utmost limit at which it ends, then it decreases until the Day of Resurrection. The bonds of Islam have been firmly set, and they are about to decrease and recede until the Day of Resurrection. The sign of that is that you sever your kinship ties, and poverty spreads until the rich fears nothing but poverty, and until the poor finds none to show him kindness, and until the beggar stands between the two Fridays and nothing falls into his hand. While the people are thus, the earth bellows a bellowing like the lowing of cattle; every people think it bellowed from their own courtyard. Then there is a return, and it bellows a second time with the choicest pieces of its liver. It was said to him: What are the choicest pieces of its liver? He said: The like of these pillars, of gold and silver. From that day gold and silver will bring no benefit until the Day of Resurrection, and a man will find none to accept charity of his wealth."
"8999","8972 - Umar ibn Hafs al-Sadusi narrated to us, Asim ibn Ali narrated, al-Masudi narrated, from Mujalid, from al-Shabi, from Thabit ibn Qutbah, from Abdullah ibn Masud, that he said: O people, hold to obedience and the community, for they are the rope of Allah which He commanded; and what you dislike in obedience and the community is better than what you love in division. Allah, Exalted and Mighty, did not create anything except that He set for it an end at which it ends. Islam has come with firmness, and it is about to reach its utmost limit, then turn back and decrease until the Day of Resurrection. The sign of that is that poverty increases and kinship ties are cut, until the poor finds none to support him, and until the rich sees that what he has does not suffice him, and until a man complains to his brother and his cousin and he gives him nothing, and until the beggar walks between the two Fridays with nothing placed in his hand. When that happens, the earth bellows a bellowing, the people of every courtyard seeing only that it bellowed in their courtyard. Then it rests upon them as Allah wills, then the earth suddenly casts forth the choicest pieces of its liver. It was said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, what are the choicest pieces of its liver? He said: Pillars of gold and silver. From that day no benefit will be had from gold or silver until the Day of Resurrection."
"9000","8973 - Uthman ibn Umar al-Dabbi narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Raja narrated, Zaidah informed us, from Abu Husayn, from Abu Qutbah, from Abdullah, he said: Cling to this community, for it is the rope of Allah which He commanded; and what you dislike in the community is better than what you love in division. Allah did not ever create anything except that He set for it an end. This religion has been completed, and it is heading toward decrease. The sign of that is that kinship ties are cut, wealth is taken without right, blood is shed, the kinsman complains to his kin and he gives him nothing, and the beggar goes about between the two Fridays with nothing placed in his hand. While they are thus, the earth bellows with the lowing of cattle as it casts forth the choicest pieces of its liver; and after it no benefit will be had from gold or silver."
